      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>MY DEAR NELLIB</head>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIB</p>
        <p>Many excellent specimens of millinery are ncrib to be seen, but, as usual, the varieties and tastes in this direction are legion. The newest hat is a large scoop, curving downwards over the ears, and again rising slightly over the hair at the back. This shape is very becoming to some faces, especially when the brim of the hat is lined with black velvet or some soft tone of colour chosen to suit the tones of the complexion, the colour of the hair, and that of the eyes. Perhaps the most becoming hate of the present season are the white or pale grey felt hats lined with soft colour, such as duck's•egg blue, !Ikeda green, shrimp pink, or the milder tones of tomato red. These are practi- cally the background for the face, and the possibili- ties that lie in them, in this sense, are endless. The downward curving of the brim, at the back, serves to surround the hair, too, with a becoming frame of colour which may be so chosen as to enhance ite charm of softness and of tint.</p>
        <p>Jackets are becoming very popular now and fur 800 MS to be the prevailing fashion. Very costly OHM are conspicuous on those of the wealthy, but In these days of imitation, the poorer classes can get capital substitutes. The article that is to be a feature of the winter is white fur. Already I have seen fluffy, snow-like boas with a multitude of tails dangling in front. Huge storm-collars and revers are being put on to ermine capes, the white fur being fox, and extremly delicate in texture. On many garments two furs are frequently mixed together. Rich sable is lined with ermine, and seal also is lined with the same regal-looking skin. Laoe appliqué plays a triumphant part with fur, though it may seem to have no eonnexion with it, and of course the fringes, which appear almost on everything, are apparent. On skin coats and cloaks fringe formed of sable tails figures, and at other times of fur, velvet, and jet in handsome bugles.</p>
        <p>At present dresses for young girls in soft silks and Patina are quite a speciality. The dresses are made in sensible and hygienic styles, which are far more appropriate for young girls than tight- fitting boned bodices. Almost all the new sleeves come down in a point over the hand. The most comfortable coat•sleeve widens a little at the wrist, and turns back in a fold, which ends in two downward points, each separate from the other. Dress sleeves are moulded to the arms, but so very tightly are some of them sewn in that the seam looks exactly like the joint in the arm of a Dutch doll. A good way to make a plain cloth skirt look smart is to pub on a couple of frills of the cloth, so that they form the outline of a tablier. Headed by a band of fur, or a little braiding, or both com- bined, these will give quite an air to the dress. At the same time, a plain skirt may far exceed in effect an elaborately trimmed one. If it is well cut and well hung, and made of good material, it needs only a graceful figure to make it unsur- passable. A neat coat that a friend of mine has just reaches the waist at the back, almost covering a belt that passes under the long, rather square fronts. These are cut straight down from the lapels, which, like the outline of the coat, are finished with several rows of machine-stitching.</p>
        <p>There is quite a rage for spots on woollen materials, as well as velvet and velveteen. They are placed so close together as to have a (tinting effect upon the eyes, making the spectator feel almost giddy. In black on beige, or in white and black, these epotted materials are among the smartest of the hour. The prevalence of bright colcurs in cloth and tweed for afternoon wear is one of the features of the season. Whole costumes are seen in rostered or pale see blue tweed, and a peculiar shade of metallic pale green is also in favour. The hats and toques worn with these are invariably black, for no second colour dare be worn with one so vivid in the gown. A little black is also occasionally introduced on the dreg itself, with several rows of fine black stitching es trimming. The coat has the collar and lapels outlined with a similar trimming. The boa is of black for, and the little toque has ivory ribbon sewn with narrow black velvet, and a cluster of autumn leaves chosen to harmonise with the tint of the gown. The large buttons are in oxyaised</p>
        <p>Your loving, JENNEm.</p>
        <p>A Book for Ladies.</p>
        <p>The knowledge contained in this book is ot PRICE- LESS VALUE TO EVERY MARRIED LADY, and has been the means of brightening the lives of thousands. It contains a largelamount of valuable information. All will prGfit by reading it, as the knowitage gained is priceless, and cannot but do good. Seat in sealed enve lope for two stamps.</p>
        <p>MRS, M. F ARLEIOII, (Box 63,) 104 NOW= imp, Longs. Ls</p>
        <p>P • 7/1") P L'N el 7 wip • - e s'"\</p>
