      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>McDOUCTAL I /98 PATENT SELF-P. A BING FLOUR MAKES THE BEST PASTRY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>g WOM EN'S CHAT."</head>
        <p>g WOM EN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Lord Micauley, in his essay on Moore's life of Lord Byron, remarks upon the propensity which the British public has, for going to sleep on a subject for a hundred years, and then suddenly waking up in a fury of indig- nation at that which has been ring on all the time under its nose. That is what will halopen some day with regard to our treatment of the dependants of our soldier 4 who fall in battle, or are otherwise killed in the execu- tion of their duty. Concerning the welfare of the widows and families of non-c,minissioned officers, and men, the State undertakes no responsibility, but leaves their maintenance to the Royal Patriotic, lard other funds, which are subscribed by chariv. In this respect we have an example in the United States, which has spent many millions in pensions to the widows and families of • men killei in batt!e. So generous is this recognitim that it is alleged there are some sturdy widows who have ben drawing pensions since 1814, and it has even been a-serted by defamers of American officialdom that some of the recip:ents, purport to be widows or children of men killed in 1778!</p>
        <p>It was almost by accident that M. Jean de Reszke discovered the gold mine he had in his vcice. Tne eminent professor whe had clime of it, insisted that it was a baritone, and trained it as such. The outcome of this course of treatment was—failure, and for ten years, when he might have been deliehting the world, he kept silence. In 1882 however, he returned to the stage, after, at the importunty of a friend, having tried the higher register, and having discovered that a poor baritone might 1;e an unrivalled tenor. M. Jean de Reszke's father and mother had fine voices. His brother Edward's quality in this line we all know, and his sister, until she married, appeared at the Paris Grand Opera. His brother Louis, at home, in Poland. has a superb voice, but he avid not cultivate it, preferring instead, to cultivate his land. Miss Florence Marryat, whose loss we are all deplor- ing, was the youngest daughter of the famous Captain Marryat, the great " novelist of the sea." She was born at Brighton ar d her first novel Love's Conflict" was written just thirty-four years ago. In her time she was a high-class comedy actress, an operatic singer, la most successful entertainer and lecturer, an ardent Spiritualist, an editor, and—ene of the most successful novelists of her day. Altogetker Miss Marryat wrote close upon eighty novels, the majority of which have been translated into several European languages. Though still a great festival in the Gaelic countries All Hallcws Eve (October 31) is not now much:observed in England. The London branch of the Gaelic League, however, decided to celebrate it this year, in a unique fashion, which took the form of a Ceilidh, at which songs were sung in Irish. Scotch, Welsh and Manx. There were Irish and Scotch national dances, and Irish lied Scotch bagpipes provided some of the music. All Hallowmas derives its importance from the popular usages which have distinguished the day itself, or the night preceding, called the eve, or vigil. There is reason to believe that this wag a pagan before it wee transformed into a Christian festival, and there can at any rate, be no question that the ceremonies to which I refer, are of Druidical origin. Bonfires, bell ringing,and domestic merrymaking, in which ale or wine, mixed with the pulp of roasted apples, was the principal beverage, marked the eve of Hallowmas. The well- known p.m of Burns. " Hallow e'en " will immortalize the ancient ceremonies which it relates. By Roman Catholic and High Church people, the day is set apart for the commoration of all those saints and martyrs, to whom no particular day is assigned. It has been ascertained that if all the inhabitants of tbe British Isles were seized with a desire to attend a place of worship, 25,000,000 would be crowded out. Taking the buildings belonging to every denomination there is only space to seat 15,000.000. The population is 40,000,000. bberefore if all were to attend a church chapel, or other place of worship, the accommodation would have to be three fifths larger titan it is at present. Some of the newest gowns are being built upon corsets, or what closely resembles this article of attire, and the effeet may be said to leave nothing to be desired. A woman I noticed at a reception one night last week, who locked as though She had bsen melted and poured into her frock, was, I learned later,wearing one of these novelties. Nothing further in the way of coraets is re- quired with such a gown ef course. Fabrics for evening dresses just now are perfectly ex- quisite. Painted satine, silks, Tramline, etc., abound, and some of the new, painted fringes, are lovely beyond description. The painted blossoms and leaves, appear, as the fringe flutters to and fro, to wave and sway, as real ones, when stirred by a breeze. Victoria Sandwich.—Take 3 ozs butter.beat to a cream. Add slowly 3 CM powdered white sugar, and 3 -zs flour. Beat the yolks and whites of two eggs to a stiff froth, mix, and add, beating well. Flavour with a few drops of vanilla, then pour into two well greased tins. Turn out when a pale golden brown, let cool, epreal one half with preserve, and lay the other on top. A tesspnonful of baking powder should be added ta the flour if not self-raising. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
