      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>VXA MIN ER, FRIDAY, NOVZI BER 3, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE VXA MIN ER, FRIDAY, NOVZI BER 3, 1899.</p>
        <p>much, but he could just trace the outline or two taen who were walking slowly towards his retreat, apparently carrying something between them. 1)00 looked with all his might but could make nothing of it.</p>
        <p>"They're going to the Dog," thought the boy, "but whatever can they be a-lugging " Bob became interested, and kept his eyes glued to the chink. As the strangers appro%ched nearer, Bob saw, to his dismay, that they were turning off the high road and coming straight towards his place of refuge. He did not at all wish to be discovered, for as most probably the travellers would stop at the inn, they would, of course, tell his father of his whereabouts, and Bob felt that he had fought enough for one evening. He was preparing to jump down from his hiding-place, when, giving one more quick glance at the two men, a gust of wind at that moment blew back the covering of their bundle, and to his horror Bob saw that the burden they carried was an inanimate form. The boy felt as if glued to the spot. He could not even find the necessary courage to jump down and run away; indeed, now it was too late, and he would most likely be seen. He had, however, enough presence of mind left to creep close to the thatch, leaving himself just able to peep over a rafter. There it seemed to him that he waited for years. He even thought that he had been a fool not to escape when he still had ample time, and then suddenly he heard a faint rustle, and the half-broken door was softly pushed open. The two men came in very cautiously, though as the night was so rough they did not expect to find the desolate shed occupied by a human being. Bob held his breath as they entered and closed the door behind them.</p>
        <p>"This will be a good death-house," said one of the men in a low voice. His companion was not so cautious.</p>
        <p>"He can have stumbled in here," he said, doggedly, as if answering an objection. "I thought for a tnoment he was going to shew fight." _</p>
        <p>"Best leave him here,' said the first speaker. “Shew fight! Why, he has had no Jima to tarn even."</p>
        <p>"If anyone finds him? " "Not just yet, anyhow, not before morning. Why, there's no one hereabouts for miles, except the landlord of the Dog and Thick. Gad! he is more often drunk than sober. The sooner we are off the better. A. mere accident, remember, and he crawled in here to find shelter."</p>
        <p>"He will have time to cool his brains anyhow, and time too to digest." "You are sure there was no one along the road? It's best to think of one's own skin, for it's not my lord who will care." 'Care He cares for nothing in heaven or hell.* "You are wrong; he's a fool about-" At this moment Bob, whose curiosity had made him crane his neck a little too far, displaced a small bit of loose wood, causing an mudible creak. The boy felt that his fate was sealed. He held his breath for very fear, but happily for him his visitors thought only of outside danger, and the one nearest the door flung himself on his knees, and thrust his head out into the darkening gloom. There was silence for a few moments. Bob felt sick with the apprehension of being discovered, for he could still trace the dark, motionless form lying on one side of the shed. At that moment, however, came a very distinct sound from the direction of the inn. _ _</p>
        <p>"It's only old Babbitts's crew," said the out- looker. "Come on, I'm off to my lord; he will be impatient to see us." "The girl spoils the show," muttered the other, but I'm not my lord's relation in vain." Bob again craned his neck to see all that was possible to see, for the spirit of adventure was born in him this evening. If only he could have seen their faces, but they did not turn towards him, and besides the darkness was quite a sufficient mask. "Here, cover him up with this dry fern," said one of them. "What is that used for in these parts ? " "Litter, but I thought this old boat-house was never used. They gather the dead fern at times to clear the bank. We had best cut across to the Hall. I have my directions." "Which side of the pqnd ? Look, it will be some time before this gallant wakes or is dis- covered."</p>
        <p>The last speaker gave the motionless body a last shove with his foot, and then both men crept out of the boat-house and disappeared from Bob's sight. The boy remained motionless for fully ten minutes in his retreat, fearing that the two might return, then at last cautiously letting himself drop —his powers of climbing were unlimited—he knelt down by the strange bundle of dead tern which he knew covered over a man's form. Was he dead? This seemed a dreadful possibility. Had he been murdered by those two cowards, and what had Bob now to do? The idea of slinking hnio and of ridding himself of responsibility by flight did not cross his mind. Nothing would have been easier than to get help at the inn and to carry the lifeless form into the Dog and Duck, but Bob felt that something was wrong here, and that his father was not the man to right any wrong. Well, he must do something, and the sooner the better. He crept out of the boat-house as if he himself were the guilty party. Then he argued, but was there any- thing N% ron g ? Could the two men by chance have found this poor traveller on the road, and could they not have felt as puzzled as Bob did now as to how they were to dispose of him? The lad was not introspective, and did not make any profession of virtue, but he had never willingly hurt when he could heal. His creed was simple. He loved his mother and hated his father. He preferred kicks and ill-usage to following the ways of his father, which ways never led to good; but beyond this Bob made no profession, nor did he keep all the commandments. As to honouring his father, had he done so he would have despised himself. Bob was perhaps born with a brave, honest nature. He certainly knew nothing of heredity, and at this moment his meditation was not as to wrong or right, but as to what he was to do with the stranger and what could be the meaning of all he had unwittingly seen. Ten minutes later Mrs. Rabbitts did not start as she heard Bob's familiar tap at the window, for these two had quite a code of secret signs well understood by them alone. "0 Bob, was you hurt much? " said the mother, opening the window. "Don't come in, your father ain't a-bed yet, and there's a very rough lot drinking to-night." "Hurt!" said Bob, scornfully. "Give me the lantern, mother; there's a poor fellow out yonder as wants help." "What's the matter, Bob, don't you get into any more trouble with him." - -</p>
        <p>"Trouble, mother! Hang it, but would you have me see a fellow die without giving him a help- ing hand? " "I don't say that," said poor Mrs. Rabbitts, sadly, "but don't bring anyone here. I'm terribly afeared that you and your father will do each other mischief some day." "Never mind about father; give me the lantern." "And I'll put the light up high when it's safe for you, Bob." "All right; give me some brandy and don't take on about me, mother, I'll not let anyone touch you anyhow, not father, no not if the devil him- self--" And with a grin that shewed that Bob's spirits, at all events, were not broken, he took the old lanthorn and hurried off, making a detour in order to keep out of sight of the the tap-moth of the Dog and Duck! When he regained the ehed his light revealed a young man of about thirty, dressed in a rough suit of tweed. His face was very delicate in outline, and Bob settled that he was a gentleman. Then immediately pouring some brandy down his throat, he rubbed his hands and slowly raised him up against his knees. For a long time there was no sign of life, but still Bob persevered. The lanthorn light flickered on the deadly pale face till Bob learnt every feature by heart. He noted the resolute chin, the hair falling over the broad forel3ead—there was no hair on his face—but the eyebrows almost united over the bridge of the nose, and were of a much darker colour than the hair.</p>
        <p>- _ "Ode Daggs," muttered Bctb, who had picked up queer oaths from his father's friends, "but I'd swear that this at all events ain't the sinner. If he can be rubbed back to life, then I'll do it. He's warmer now, by gad, he is." At last, to Bob's inexpressible delight, the stranger heaved a deep sigh. "Where am I?" he said, slowly. "I can't think or recollect." "You ain't in a palace," said Bob, with a chuckle. "Here, sir, git some more brandy down. Ain't the best, but it's warming, and this boat- house is _enough to dank anyone as wants a bit</p>
        <p>warming." - "I've been a fool," was his next remark, at which Bob laughed softly. Ho had learnt never to be overloud in his expressions. "Shouldn't be surprised a bit if you had been dem low to get yourself hero. , Why, if I hadn:t a-come back, I take it there was but poor chance for you on this cold nicht."</p>
        <p>`4Who are you? " was the next remark. "Oh, I ain't anyone to matter about. Only Bob Rabbitts, you know." "Bob Rabbitts. Well, then, give me your hand, PDX lad, and, by the way, I hope your hea,rt 40 wan,' as your brandy. ConfO-urai fit thoug,ll6, I've had enough of it." Bob was delighted, and chuckled again.</p>
        <p>"Ain't surprised. Father can make a man dead drunk in no time with it. Hell fire' I call it, i;ot you've stood more than I fancied you would. Now, hoist yourself up, sir, and lean a bit on me."</p>
        <p>*.And what am she say r Wad enough to gi that, I daresay, for since her worthless son wer to gaol she has lyen rery poor." "Well, she can't be very poor, because sh threw the sixpene.e away." "Throw away what you gave her! Sh awl indeed, be a wicked witch. 7„,,,,,</p>
        <p>"Never mind, papa, we are rich, aren't we A sixpence more or less does not matter to n You are always giving me money. I on, wish-"</p>
        <p>"What, my Ruby ? " "That there were shops here. I should like 1 spend it in pretty things, and I should like to gii it to a lot of poor people and make them happj Oh, I slould like everyone to be happy, even pa old Tippety Coles." "But if you were a beggar yourself, darling, ho many who had received from you would pay bac your benefits ? " "But we are not beggars; we are rich." "I shall be busy to-night, Ruby. You must g to the schoolroom to Nancy and go to bed aftA your supper." "Busy on my birthday, papal That is too bail You ought to think of me. But you think of to many people." "I am always thinking of you, darling. Kiss no and go. I shall take care, as usual, to be sure yo are safe."</p>
        <p>Ruby's laugh woke the echoes. "Safe! What could happen to me here, of al places in the world?"</p>
        <p>"Of course, nothing, dearest, but—it is curious Ruby, that you are not like your sex and that yo never feel fear. Are you afraid of anything my child ? "</p>
        <p>-Ruby leant her charming face on her two palm and looked thoughtful. "What should frighten me, papa ? " "Nothing, nothing, but that is the point wit: most women—they fear mere shadows." "But lam not a woman. You said so, paps I'm only a girl." "Well, go now, and you shall see me agaim Ruby, whatever happens never be afraid." "Come, then, upstairs with me, dear dad. Na is so afraid of you; not really, but she pretendi I tease her and tell her she only does it becalm she thinks that I do not treat you with enoug respect."</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
