      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</head>
        <p>NOBLER THAN REVENGE.</p>
        <p>BY ESME STUART, Author of "In the Dark," "Inscrutable," "A Woman of Forty," "Sara's Mother," 860.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CHAPTER I.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER I.</p>
        <p>At this present time the tourist has, alas! discovered the lonely spot where once the quaint inn stood, desolate among the heather hills, and but partially protected on the north by ever green fir woods. To-day the inn has been meta- morphosed into an hotel. There are golf-links a few miles off, and the great sheet of water for- merly known as the pond is rechristened "The Lake." Instead of the solid punt, not beautiful but useful, there is a fragile pleasure-boat, which meanders round the water at so much the hour, whilst from the banks the children impudently beg for pence. Thus year by year the loneliness of the land dis- appears, and with it much of the wild romance that made our fathers braver and bolder, if less scrupulous, than they are to-day. Each generation, however, has its special good points and its special character. Each age looks kindly on its peculiar sins, and loudly applauds its uncultivated virtues as it marches boldly on, destroying with a smile what it can never recreate. But why cry over spilt milk? Metaphorical milk had little to say to those old times, and nothing to do at this moment with young Bob Rabbitts standing in the kitchen of the Dog and Duck, situated on the wind-swept Galley Hill heath. Had it been possible for the painter, Rembrandt, to be present, he would have found a welcome subject in this low-raftered room, dark with age and smoke. He would have welcomed the open 'chimney, with its pent roof and its corner seats, and he would not have omitted the red flagged floor and the light from the wood fire. To-night this light fell on a boy's face, by nature open and good-tempered, but at this moment red with passion and plainly expressing hatred. In the high-backed chair, made of solid English oak, sat the landlord of the Dog and Duck, smok- ing a short pipe, and every now and then eyeing his son with the stare of a half-drunken bully, visibly gratified by each new burst of rage which his gibes wrung from the boy. On the other side of the fire sat the meek, frightened mother, trembling with fear as to what might happen to her boy, though long ago she had lost all fear for herself. "I'll be danged, but you shall go, Bob," said Babbitts, spitting into the fire and looking side- ways at his son. "Here, old woman, fetch us another glass of rum. It's cursed cold to-night." "You've had too much already, Joe. Let Bob alone. The squire's house's no fit place for a lad." "He'll go where I tell him or he'll go to H--," was the answer. "I won't go," said Bob. "If the squire wants his dirty jobs done he can get someone else to do them." "No, he can't. I've promised that you should go, Bob, and go you shall. The squire ain't a man to be refused; besides, it's all for your good. You want the devil taken out of you, and the squire tan do that job better than most." "Let Bob be," murmured his mother. "I'll not go, mother, never fear. I won't leave you here with him," said Bob, intimating his father with a jerk of his head. "Then I'll teach you to have a will of your own, young varmint," roared the landlord, throw- ing down his pipe. He had been drinking all the afternoon, and now that the winter's day was dra*ing in he was by no means in a pleasant frame of mind. The poor mother started forward, and tried hurriedly to place herself between her husband and her son. "Don't you touch the lad, Joe. You'll be sorry for it when you're sober." "l'm drunk, am I? Well, then, out of my way-" With a backward swing of his arm the landlord flung his wife away, and in falling the poor woman hit her head against the wooden kerb. She uttered a half-cry of pain, and the sound maddened Bob. It made him lose all sense of his father's strength. He was conscious of nothing but great wrath; he saw nothing but his mother lying moaning on the floor, and with the fierceness of a young tiger he .threw himself against his father's advancing figure. The struggle w.is sharp, but of no great duration, for just as the boy had fastened himself like a vice round the landlord's right arm, his mother, filled with horrror at the sight, struggled to her feet.</p>
        <p>"Bob! Bob! For Heaven's sake leave go! It's your father." No other voice would have made the boy loose his hold. He dropped his arms as if struck with paralysis, and the half-drunken landlord, feeling his advantage, closed on his son. "I'll teach you your duty to your father, you young rascal I Take that! and that! And now go and cool yourself out of doors! There! out with you I " Joe Rabbitts was a powerful, thick-set man. His short arms were all muscles, and, besides this, the drink had made him dangerous. He opened the door and flung the boy out on the bare ground with such force, and with so sudden a jerk, that Bob was stunned as he fell with his head against the garden gate. He did not hear the door shut and bolted against him, nor for a time was he conscious of the cold wind which blew through his shaggy hair, and the rain which penetrated his jacket. Nature had, in one moment, drowned all pas- sion in oblivi9n. _ _ .</p>
        <p>When Bob returned to consciousness his first idea was that he was in bed, but the rain beating upon his face soon recalled to him the scene with his father. He jumped up and tried to collect his scattered senses. At present it was no use return- ing home, for Bob, taking note of the gathering darkness, knew that it must be about six o'clock, and that his father's boon companions would now be with him. He must wait till the landlord had been carried to his bed, a very usual ending of the day's drinking. Bob's anger was now con- siderably damped. Besides, he was accustomed to these violent scenes, and took them as part of the day's work. Not that he had any intention of giving in to parental commands, but he knew that for a few days he must, as far as possible, keep out of his father's sight. The Dog and Duck was built close to the high road. Once it had been the half-way house on the great posting-road running between Huckdale Heath and London. But it was now forsaken by its old lovers, and had not yet found new admirers in the shape of tourists. The building looked desolate enough to the outward eye, but the land- lord did not complain too much of want of custom, the truth being that all the good-for- nothings of the district assembled there, feeling themselves safe from the parson's eyes and not likely to be interfered with by the Galley Hill squire. Indeed, it was hinted that he and Joe were fast friends, and that when the house was closed to the public the squire would sometimes look in if Rabbittswas sober enough to entertain the guest. It was what some of them called a "dangerous long way" to the next country town of Huckdale, and a good eight mile or more to the town on the opposite side of Galley Hill. Who was there to fear, for most of them took no account of God or of the devil. When Bob's brain had cleared a little he wondered where he had best go. He cared nothing about the rain, and he knew very inch of the country and was unacquainted *ith fear. But he wanted to get back to his mother, for Bob's one strong passion was love of the poor feeble woman who passively allowed herself to be tyrannised by her brute of a husband. IL 3 dared not yet return home, however, so he ran down to the edge of the pond, where some four hundred yards away was an old boat- house, which had often served him as a shelter from his father's anger. He climbed up to a kind of ledge among the rafters, a favourite hiding-place of his, and crouching down, he gave himself up to thought. His father wanted him to enter the squire's service as out-of-3oor boy to do any odd jobs that wanted doing, but Bob hated the squire, and still more he hated the idea of having to give up his wild, free life, and the care of his mother. Though he worked hard about the house, and looked after the old pony, he had plenty of time to roam about when his father was drinking with his friends, and was too much occupied in this manner to trouble himself about Bob's movements. As he sat in the cold boat-house, going over the scene with his father, he fancied he heard a slight sound of steps on the high road. Bob's ears were always on the alert, for necessity had sharpened hearing. Above his shelf there was a chink between the wall and the thatch of the ruined boat-house, and through this chink Bob could see the road for some little distance. It was too dark to distil:4ll44</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CHAPTER 11.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 11.</p>
        <p>Not two miles from the old boat-house was Yewdale Hall, the abode of Sir Harry Beauworth, a man whom everyone feared and hated, without being able to give a reason for it. His manner was always calm, and he never indulged in visible anger or vulgar passion. But the instinct of his fellow-creatures was not to be destroyed by the absence of these signs. They felt that much was hidden beneath the surface of the outward man. Many a time a man who had unwittingly offended the squire was puzzled, because, though he was not rebuked, something disagreeable was sure to happen to huiii, and after some time even the most stupid began to realise that cause and effect never failed though all warning was absent. What added to the nameless terror of being Sir Harry's enemy was that the misfortunes were never traced back directly to the squire; indeed, the "old gentleman" was usually credited with the evil, and no one could disprove that. The poorer folk had therefore made up their minds that Squire Beauworth was too sensible to do his own work, but employed, for a mysterious consideration, the willing, if invisible, author of all evil. It had been expressed in this way by the village blacksmith.</p>
        <p>"Ills dawted friend does his business for him that's how it is you never catch out the squire; and it's very well known that every May morning lie brings he three drops of his blood to drink. If the devil died—as they say—when the Blessed Babe was born, I take it he came to life agin when our squire come into the world." It was a great consolation to settle the question in this way; who would have refused to believe that they were afraid of a mere man unaided by a my st erious agency.</p>
        <p>The Hall lent itself to legendary stories of dark things. It was many-gabled, contained narrow passages within, and queer rooms everywhere. Much of it was panelled, and looked gloomy enough, to give rise to the idea of ghosts. Indeed, the place would have been dulness personified if it had not been for one being who lived there, and who was the reverse of dull or mysterious. This was Ruby t eauworth, and Sir Harry doted on his only child, so said the villagers, for the country people had but little to feed their imaginations upon but the doings and sayings of the squire and his daughter.</p>
        <p>Ruby, who was all kindness, was not always successful in dealing with offenders. She would suggest to them to throw themselves on the squire's mercy, and to make full confession. It was like asking a bird to hop into a snare, assuring it beforehand that it could not possibly be hurt. "You don't borrow the devil's darning-needle twice," said those who related their experience of the tender mercies of Squire Beauworth. "I'd rather carry coals than beg ought of the master, least of all mercy; he says Yea,' but he means Nay, ' " was the often-expressed opinion of those who had tried.</p>
        <p>Had a stranger, however, entered the Hall on this special winter's evening, nothing would have led him to guess the aforesaid facts. A large fire was blazing on the open hearth, and gleamed on many a treasure of carved oak and shelves whereon reposed priceless china. Ruby was sitting in an old carved chair, looking like an inhabitant of another and more beautiful world, as with smiling lips she chatted on to her father. He sat opposite to her, leaning a little towards her as if to listen to each word she said. His eyes were fastened on her face, where grave and gay looks would succeed each other like glinting sunbeams on a polished surface. "Papa, do you know that to-day is my birthday? I'm seventeen. Fancy seventeen whole years old, and Nr.ncy says I'm grown-up. Is it true? I know no other girls, so of course I have to ask you questions." "Grown-up, Ruby! What an ideal You are a child still. No one is grown-up till they are twenty." "Then I've got three years more, dear dad. I almost wish I were old—quite old." The squire laughed. "You old, Ruby! Why, the world would be old too by that time! Tell me, child, you like your home, don't you ? " He looked round the half-lighted hall, and then at the fresh young face before him. . .</p>
        <p>"Like it, papa! Why, I love it; I would never change it. It would break my heart to leave it. But I should like to know some things about the wicked world, as Nancy calls it!"</p>
        <p>"What has Nancy been saying ? " asked the squire, fixing his eyes intently on Ruby, and speak- ing in a very slow manner. His voice was musical, and he never spoke too loud. Ruby too loved music, and could usually be heard singing about the old house.</p>
        <p>"Oh, Nancy did not say much—she never does. I believe she does not know much, but she puts it on you, and says you would not like her to gossip! Isn't that fine ? "</p>
        <p>"What do you want to know, my Ruby ? " "Well, all about grown-up people. Do girls marry always at twenty? Ladies, I mean, because Betty Smith married at eighteen. You remember it, don't you, dad, dear, and you said she was a young fool and--" "Well ? " "She had to leave the cottage. I don't know wily, but she did cry so, and said she was so unhappy, and would I ask you to help her." "And so you did," murmured the squire, smiling slightly. "Yes, but you could not do anything. Bill Smith was so ungrateful, and said it was all your fault. He did not know how you had tried to help them, did he ? "</p>
        <p>"No, he did not know," said Sir Harry, leaning back in his chair.</p>
        <p>"You said that the poor were very ungrateful after that, didn't you ? " "I ought not to have said that. So Nancy tells you nothing about the world outside Yewdale ? " "No," said Ruby, laughing. "But she says 'in' she might she would.' 'Why, who should prevent you?' I said, papa. Dear old Nancy, she Is so funny. But you know everything. Tell me all about weddings." "Weddings! That is only for grown-up young ladies to talk about. Would you like to be married, Ruby ? " "Yes, to a Prince who comes unexpectedly. Do girls fall in love after they are asked or before, papa?" "Love! Why it is all nonsense. It means nothing. You must love your father, Ruby, and no one else, and then I shall choose your husband."</p>
        <p>He laughed as if he were makiiag a joke, but there was no smile on his lips. He rose and came to where the girl was sitting and passed his hand over her abundant curling hair. "You love your father, child, very much ? " "Yes, you know I do. But, papa, I did think that when I was seventeen you would let me go to bed later. Nine, you say, is the time for girls." "Yes, the time for lovely complexions like yours, Miss Ruby." "What is a lovely complexion? I really don't care much about it, only you like mine and say it is lovely. That's because you are my father." "Yes, it's because I'm your father. If you were pale, Ruby, I should think- No, you must go to bed at nine till you are twenty, or till you are married, and then we shall see." "Do you know, papa, Leo was idle to-day and would not come for a walk."</p>
        <p>"So you have been in the village to-day, and Leo would not accompany you. Did you see anyone?" "Oh, Tippety Coles, you know, papa, the old woman they call the witch." "Yes, I know her."</p>
        <p>"She looked so angrily at me when I asked her if her rheumatism was better."</p>
        <p>"Angry at you? And why? " "I don't know, papa. She said something about her son, and that it was all your fault that he was in gaol." "Indeed! She must be off her head." Again the strange smile which Ruby did not notice.</p>
        <p>"Of course, for I told her how good you were to everybody, and that, if it were possible, you would get him out. But, poor thing, she only cursed me. It was dreadful, and I ran away." The squire contracted his eyebrows till they almost met, then he took his daughter's hand in his and fondled it gently. "Cursed you, my darling! She must be a bad old woman.</p>
        <p>"Oh, not bad, papa, only a little off her head. I be,ll. eive_a her asixPegce .</p>
