      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SOLDIER'S HISTORY.</head>
        <p>A SOLDIER'S HISTORY.</p>
        <p>Stirring Story for Stirring Times. Mr. SAMUEL WILLIAMS, of Brithdir. a stationmaster the Nnowdon tramroad,is a man with a stirring histoi which a "Ni.rth %Yaks Observer" representative elicit from his inf dept and manly lips. Be is an old soldier whom in tbet.e warlike times we may be proud, and story is apprwpriate to present interest. Born forty-fi years ago at Di, wark, Nottinghamshire, Mr. Williar fitqvcd with the 23rd Royal Walsh Fusiliers in Burma and was, in fortitude as well as in courage, a credit the splendid record of that famous regiment. For he faced pain as well as danger. "In Bunnell said be, " I had oltesn to march with wet feet," al the reporter vial able to see (though the have fell( we's tc..o modes, to mention it) that he did his duty Me expense ot great suffering, which did not end wh he returned to civil his.</p>
        <p>"For three years," he said," my fret, summer az winter alike, were intensely painful. For some tinse was employed at the Slate Quarries, but I had to give my work owing to the pain I suffered. Having had aught relief I got employment at another quarry, but t .old complaint again compelled rue to leave my win Lastly I was apoctinted to take charge of the Behr station on the Snowdon tramroad, but it was with t greatest diffiuulty that I could attend to my duties. took rue three-quarters of an hour to walk a distance could now cover easily in quarter of an hour. So pai ful had my feet become when the rheumatism took fu I:old that I often wiihed myself dead. They were bui ing like seething fire, and, though I tried a number romedies and ombrocatione, I could find no lief, uotil I took Dr. Williams' pink pills I pale people." *1 Living in such a remote place as you do, how e you cc me to know of mese ?" " Well, I saw acccunts of the cures they have effect in the newspapers." " When did you firit take them ?"</p>
        <p>" Towares the citadel last year. After taking the box I found hardly any improvement, but the see() box eased my pain considerably. The third box ga me complete and permanent relief." " Do you continue to take them ?" "No ; I to.)k the IBA b-.x two months agoiand I bx. had no pain sinc,i. Now I can walk miles without stiff ing any pain, and if you bad been a quarter of an bo later coming here I wouid be on my way across ft mountain."</p>
        <p>Hardy anything causes such agony as inflammato rheurnat;sm. Bed-not needles seem to be soaring t tender uerves. It is not cold alone that causes rhe iratkm,though cold and thine often bring on the attac Bet the original evil is in the blood, and it is bocau they act direct upon the blood that Dr. Williams' pii pills have cured so many cases of rheumatism,rheumal gout, and sciatica. They are likewise praised for On cures of anemia (or impoverished blood), and even co aimption, which often succeeds amemia. But subs tutee do not cure; the true pills are always labelled wi the full name—Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people and may be had, if desired, from Dr. Williams' Medici Company, Holborn Viaduct. London, post free at tv and ninepeace a box—six boxes thirteen and nine.</p>
