      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>WARRINGTON MAN'S SAD END.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MAN'S SAD END.</p>
        <p>Cuts His Throat at Winsford..</p>
        <p>Mr. H. C. Yates, coroner, held an inquiry at tl Oddfellows' Arms Hotel, Winsford, on Monday afte noon, into the circumstances attending the deal of George Parkinson, of 167, Station-road, who, Saturday afternoon, was found lying dead in tl bedroom of his house with a wound in his throat. Ellen Layham, 167. Station-road, said tI deceased was her stepfather, and was 53 years ace. For some time he had been a labourer Dallam forge, Warrington, but for the last 13 wee] had not followed his employment, having suffer( from a bleeding cancer under the left ear. Be 131 lived with her during the last month, and tl week before he came to her house he. visited Mal chester Infirmary. He was there told by the docto that hi a case was hopeless, and during a convert Lion he had with her, he told her that thi gave him t 3 understand that if they cut the cancer I would be dead within three minutes. Since be had bec with her be had been bedfast. About three o'clock Saturday afternoon she was in the bedroom with bit accompanied by a woman named Alice Rough. SI offered to make the bed for him, and he motioned to h not to do so. As he appeared to be resting, she eu gested to Mrs. Hough that they should go downstai and get a cup of tea. This they decided to do, az after being downstairs about five minutes they beard thud on the bedroom floor. They rushed upstairs, at she saw the deceased lying on the bedroom floor fa downwatda. She thought his cancer had burst, so sl ran for assistanee. The deceased had been unable I speak since Thursdayond was in great pain. There wl a hag in the room, and she thought be must have takr the rsz,r with which he cut his throat from this. 1 had never expressed any determination to take hie life. Alice Rough staying at 175, Station road, sail st had ben stayine: with the decease.? for several day She was with him on Saterday afternoon and during few minutes absence, whilst she went to get some te she hearts a noise upstaiis. On going to see what it w, she felled the deceased lying on the floor with a viz it 1 his side. A- out ten minutes only had elapsed from ti time ebe lets him. The 6eceassid had not partaken any food since Thursday ()wing to his throat fus ing become stepped op, and prior to then he was on able to take liquids for some time. P,C. spoke to being called to the deceased house at 345 a Saturday afternoon. Ile found hi lying on the bedroom floor, face downwards, in a pool blood. Be was near ths window, and quite dead. sax )r was lying by his side. There was a wound c the left side of the neck, about two inches long and ye. deep. 11,-th hands were marked with blood. A nig, chair in the room contained a large quantity of blood,' it was evident that the deceased had cut his thro whilst leaning over this, and had then fallen on the ft) and died.</p>
        <p>The Coroner in summing up remarked that the wour appeared to have twee deliberately inflic:ed, and it wo Er tliem to say the state of his mind at tha time be col nated the rash act.</p>
        <p>rhp jury rnturn-e a verdict of " Suiclde whilst tni rnrarily inure "</p>
