      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>NEWS OF TIIE GAIVIE.</head>
        <p>NEWS OF TIIE GAIVIE.</p>
        <p>THR WORLD'S CiiriarprolisKrr.—The negotia- tions which "The Pittsburg Chronicle Telegraph" has directed for a world's championship match at checkers between Charles F Barker, of Boston, and Richard Jordan, of Edinbu•gh, Scotland, are com- pleted so far as it is possible for the American to go until Jordan is heard from. Barker in the first in- stance, through "The Chronicle Telegraph," cheer- fully resigned any claim he might have had to the world champioaship, and at a confarence with "The Chronicle Telegraph" representative willingly accepted Jordan's V. Stewart restriction which .Jordan proposes in lieu of the 1889 Reed vs. Barker restriction that Barker offered. Mr. Barker is anxious for a match, and is willing to give £2O for expenses, provided Jordan goes to America and plays the match prior to his tour of the cities. Bar- ker's minimum !•take money figure is £2OO, and he is willing to post a larger sum if agreeable to Mr. Jordan.—" Glasgow Herald."</p>
        <p>BOOK V. NATURAL PLAYRR.—AII exnert players are " book players." A "book player" is simply a student of the game—One who refers to the records of the correct move as he would to the dictionary for the correct spelling of a wind. Dictionaries do not always agree with each other; neither do checker books. The most apparent characteristic of " natural players " is confidence in their know- ledge of the boarls, not, infrequently amounting to glistening conoeit. It is not difficult for a person with a distinct aptitude for the game to bec3me a first class" natural player," recognised locally as an invincible champion and checker master. It is a task to become a fairly good "book player." Uncle Josh may be " 'cute at figgers," but that does not make him a good matbemaiician.—Bllsworth's Checker Book.</p>
        <p>The small diagram will enable our readers to understand the notation by which the moves of the game are recorded. Black c,ccupies the squares num- bered 1 to 12 and white those numbt,ed 21 to 32. The figures thus 11-15 indicate that a move is made from and to the squares so number.d.Bla ek has the opening move and the oafs of fignres apply alternately to Black and White to the close.</p>
