      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Councillor Bennett on Street Improvements..</head>
        <p>Councillor Bennett on Street Improvements..</p>
        <p>Os the motion of Alderman SurroN, se:outlet! by Councillor JAMES Evans the minutes of the Street Im- provement Committee were confirmed_ Among the re- solutions passed were the following :—That the Borough Surveyor be instructed to complete the new street from Upper Bank-street to Academy-Erect, and that the plan of the line of frontage of the sew works of Messrs Greening and Csinpany, Limited, in &amp;well-road be approved. Councillor BENNWrT said he WAS not at the meeting when the width et a number of streets was &amp;fined, but he thought the matter was of such importance that the Council should not be asked to pass it without having plans of the scheme shown to th.m. They were prac'i- cally determining the width of certain streets for all time. With the exception of the members of the corn- mitt2e the general body of the Council could not hav- any detailed knowledge of the important procedur, which was so desirable. fle should have liked to nave seen a large Wan submitted to show the proposed width of the streets. _</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON: The whole of the plans have been before the Council and passed at different times. The notices have actually gone out. Councillor BENNETT We are practically tnapping out the town in a few minutes for all time. althoueh I have no doubt the Borcugh Surveyor has considerf d the inat:er its merits. It bad been laid down that 63fc was the width for the four principal streets, and that wa not adequate. At some time there was no doubt they would have to increase that width. Ile would move an amendment that the minutes of the committee referring to the line ef frontage be referred back for a large plan t 3 be produced, as it was important t.iat that should h d</p>
        <p>_ The MAYOR lam sorry you have not brought this matter up in committee. Councillor 13ENINETT : I am sorry I wax not there svben it VMA discus',ed.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON was surprised that the matt ir Ind been brouht up that evening. The widths were not Wading nu the Council; they were to protect them is the event of any alterations taking place in tho town. They ceulci make the streets wider it they thought fit from time t 3 time. (Hoar, hear) Councillor SMETHURST : You don't widen the streets when you purchase property. The minutes were then agreed to, Councillors BENNETT and SMETHURST being the only di3sentients.</p>
        <p>Health Committee—The Hospital Extensions</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the 4th October, a depur atin of the property owners and residents in and near Goulden- street attended and objected to the erection ot the pro- posed lodge and new entrance to the hospital from Goulden-street, when it was resolved that the Borough Surveyor be instructed to obtain tenders for the erection of administration blo3k and pavilion, and that the erec- tion of the new lodge and its position be deferred for further consideration. The Town Clerk having submitted a report on the Small Dwellings Acquisition Act, 1899, it was res)lved that the the same be submitted to the Council.</p>
        <p>These minnte4 were agread to,on the rnotinn of Alder. man SUTTON. seconded by Alderman ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTER. At a meting held on toe 19th October, it was re- solved :-4' That notice be given to the committees hav- ing charge of the following matters. informing them of the desire of this committee to proceed with the paving of Wilderspool Causeway, and requesting the committee to proceed with the laying of the pipes and other work,: forthwith :—Tramways, electric light, telephones, water and gas. The MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes, and remarked that a great deal of work had been done during the year. Councillor BAIRD seconded, and the motion was car- ried.</p>
        <p>THE LABOUR EXAMINATION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the sth October it wa.a reported that the avera2e attendance at the Public Elemer.itAry Schools during the month of September was 10,507 ; corresponding period last year. 10,497 ;increase 10. 'The following is the result of the recent examination for labour certificates : - -</p>
        <p>_ . Passed. Failed. No. presented In St. VI.. 114 ... 63 ... 51 If 11 10 V., 53 ... 37 ... 16 .1 11 IV., 13 ... 9 ... 4 11 „ „ 111.. 1 ... 0 ... 1</p>
        <p>109 72</p>
        <p>Coutleillor SHAW GREEN moved the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor Saumcocx seconded,—Carried. The Museum —Another Handsome Gift.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Museum, Library, Arts, and Technical Instruction Committee thanks wero tendered to a number of donors. It was resolved that the special thanks of the committee be sent to Mr. F. C. Thompson for his donation. A letter from Mr. E. Worsley,PhAtiking the committee for its vote of condolence on the death of his father, the late Mr. J. E. Worsloy, was read and it was resolved that Mr. T. J. Ridgway be added to the committee in place of the deceased gentleman.</p>
        <p>Alderman itOBEIRTS proposed the adoption of the minutes, and referred to the special value of Mr. Thompson's gift of local buttesilies and moths. They were beautifully mounted and in a splendid state o! pre- servation, while the Committee als3 a;tached special value to them as they were local. Councillor ELLISOti seconded. and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>TOWN ;TALL, PARE AND BATHS COMMITTEE• The report bt the Bathkeeper was read at a meetina of the above Committee, from which it appelred that the income from the baths for the past four weeks was £ll is, and for the corresponding period last year £l3 75 43 ; decrease. £2 64 41 Plans of the proposed extension of the Volunteer quarters, and the application for a lease of 21 years were considered, and it was resolved that the tender of Masers. J. M. Greenhalgh and Company, t) supply furniture and fittings for the Borough Surveyor's office. at £56 4s Bd., be accepted. Councillor SSIRTRIIRST moved the confirmatim of the minutes.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR seconded.—Carried. MARKETS COMMITTER.—RENTS OF THE STALLS. The Markets Committee at a meeting held on October 2nd resolved that with a view of revising the present rents of the stalls in the market the B3rough Surveyor instructed to prepare a plan showing the position and capacity of each stall. It was further resolved that a deputation inspect the markets in Blackburn and Burnley, and repert thereon. It was reported at meetiag held on the 30th October, that the total in- come from all slurces for the pant two weeks had hem] £192 21. 81 , against £173 9s. 41. the corresponding period last year. Alderman EILITCIINSON moved the confirtnation.of the minutei. and, alluding to the question of the proposed revision oi the rents of the stalls in the market,said the reprrt would be presented at the nerd; meeting of the Council.</p>
        <p>CouncElor JOHN ROBERTS seconded, and the molon was cirried.</p>
        <p>GAS COMAIITTEE......NEW FUEL ECONONISKR.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on October 2011 it was reported that the average illuminating power of the gas tor the bast four weeketwas equal to 1819 candles. It was re- solvei that the tender of Messrs. E. Green and S,n, Limited, to supply one of Greeti's Patent Fuel Elopom- isers, with horizontal engine fixed on frame, at the eum of £146; and that of Mr. James Wood to supply the printing and sta'ionery required by this department for the ensuing year be accepted. The DEPUTY MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>COUTICiIiOr LONGTON seconded, and :the motion was agreed to,</p>
        <p>NITATER COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>None of the items contained any item of pnblic in- terest, excr,pt the resolution that the tender of Messrs J Simpson and Company, of London. for engines And pumps requcred at Winwick, at £2,954, and that of the Glenfield Iron Cr,mpiny, Limited. for the supply of valves, &amp;e.., at £135 he acupted. The minutes were agreed to, on the motion of Alder- man FRANCO:AB, seconded by Counciller Llsz.</p>
