        <head>'ME EXAMINER FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3, 1899.</head>
        <p>'ME EXAMINER FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3, 1899.</p>
        <p>no Board of Trade under the Electric Lighting Act be printfd, tilac the ten,tr of the Kif4n En;ineering Com- pany ft): 1..335 be accepted, and that the maximum charge) for oacrizy supplied be as fol- lows !—Pr lighciug, 6 i per Board of ['rade unit for heating and 'illative power. 33 per ISoard of Trade unit.</p>
        <p>Ttio DEPTJTY MILYOR mover! the CONfirtifltiOtt of the ininutes, and pAnted ot tat it was not yet known what till rrquinetwats of the town would be, but tivi ['Own Ckk lad itiatald a circular to the inhabitants ask. in who would like to take the electric light, aid they hop(d to be able in a abort time to obtain and give all the informat tra they wanted. Councillor SMITHIIRST sec3nded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>General Purposes.</p>
        <p>PILOP SD TRAMWAYS. -LOCLL LIMIT RAILWAYS.--.•</p>
        <p>THE WAR: L)CA.L RELIMF FUND.</p>
        <p>At a !meting of the above committee it was resolved that application too mad' to the Board of Trade for a provisional order to coeitruct and wolk tr onways within the borough. Further proposals trivia.; hen rec-ived from Mr R If Sco;ter reap, cting the co.aatruction of light railways within tie boroulti, i wa44 reso:ve I that the same be referred to the Tramways Sut-Committee, and that .the following gentlemen be appointor/ a com- mittee to raise and distribute funds for the reli If of the wives and children of reeerviats eatlea up for active ser- vice, ad also for the relief of refugees in tho Transvaal viz. :—rii Mayor.Aidefinan Raberta, Alderman Ratahin- son, Lieut.-Colonel Ridgway, Captain E Wilton, and the Team Clerk.</p>
        <p>Tne Mtnros moved the coefirmatioa of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Altierinsu FRANCOMB seelnded.</p>
        <p>Clunciller BPNNErr said he would like to express his Weiser° that the resoluthe with reference to the appd- cation to the Board of Trade for a provisienat order to construct and work trans vests was included in the minut is. He thoeght the committee would do well to pus() Cat) mater forward, as they would all like the system in ()pc:colon as early as p essible. When they had the trenewa.ys they wmld like to have them complete, and thrones the centre of the town. Ha sileuld like to know why Bridg,e-street seemed to les No slowly dealt with. They hail power under the te.t they obtained during the recent session to carry the widening of Bridge-street as fir as Market Gate, and he would like to know how the matter was progressing. Councillor Sueenuasr sail he w mid like to know when Mr. Johnson's place was to come d..nya. Was it to be a stumbling block in the street? The MAYOR : No, I think Mr. Johnsan will be pre- pared ta go on with his alterations as Kiln as the work proceeding below his premises is complete. Referring to the relief fund for the wives and children of Re- 'pesetas. the Mayor announce i that they hail had a very handsome donati4n from Colonel Spratt and Major Molyeeux Sel and his wife, Major Smythies and Maj ir Roo.ne. As they represented the military, they though!: they should aid their names to the list. He noped that met withtheir approval. Ceenc Ilar BEINNETT again mild i for inform ttioi as to the top of Bridge-street. Toe MAYOR S4id hi was afra'd teey coeli not give them tht information yet. They had not yet quite de- cid the matter. Ho did not thiak there wes any like- lihood of delay—no unnecessary delay,at any rate. They were endea.vonring to treat with ever:ooe thy and they were going on with their work as f set as they could.</p>
        <p>The minutes were confirmed. Adoption of the Small Dwellings Act. Al.termait SUTTON moved :—" Tuat t le Town Coun- cil heteby undert.ikes to act under the Small Dwellings Acquisition Act, 1899." The Alderman said the question had to some exteat been previlully considered by the Council, and the report of the Town Clerk on the Act, which had been Font to every member of the Council would, he thought, fully explain the same. The object of tht, resolution was to see that powers a dealing with tnli dwellings under this Act were kept in their own hatuis. . .</p>
        <p>Councillor SHA.W-GREEIN seconded the resolution, but remarked that he did not see that the Act was going to be of very great advantage ; it applied only to those who were residence in houses which they wanted to purchase, and ae these facilities were already offered by a number of building societies in the town ha did not expect that there would be much gained by the adoption of the Act. Still he was aware that it would be neceesary to adopt it is order to keep certain powers in their own hands. Councillor PEACOCK thought the adoption of the Act was a good idea, and he should like to see it carried out. He maintained that building societies did not offer such facilities as were provided by the Act. They would be able under the Act to borrow money at 3 per cent. 31 per cent., or 31 per cent., and the half per cent. added would cov-r the necessary expenses which would be incurred. They could afford to lead the money on such termS, and he submitted that no solicitor or a building society or mortgagee would lend money on similar terms. Therefore be had misch pleasure in sup- porting the resolution. Ceuncillor BENNETT said another point occurred to him. A very great indirect advantage from the adop- tion of the Act would be that if people were enc•uraged by municipal corporations to become the pos,essors of their own homes, the tendency would be that the character of those houses woeld be very materially im- proved. (Hear, hear.) If a man owned his own house he was naturally house proud, and he would have an eye to its comfort, convenience, and excitation. For that reason they ought to Support the adoption of the Act.</p>
        <p>Alderman ROUSTS Said a disadvantage was that the Act was so restrictive in its op-ration, not only as re- garded its aeplication to one pawn acquiring one hou.e, but also to the fact that the benefits were ri- stricted to a particular class. It was originally intended, as he read the Bill when passing through the House of Commons, that the Act should benefit as much as any. l) ly the working class. He thought it did nothing of the kind. There were 301 y a few working men, whose wages averaged from 30e. to 325. a week, who could possibly live in their ewn houses. Therefore, the good of this Act was limited by that fact. As far as it went it seemed to be an Act which it was very desirable to adopt. It was also desirable that they should adept it prevent the County Council coming and setting up am outside authority, and creating some difficulty which they would like to avoid. Whatever nenefit there was in the Act it ought to be takes advantage of. (Hear, hear.) Councillor LONGTON.referring to the remarks of Alder- man Robeqs, said the b.nefits to the working class would depend upon the value of the house to be ac- quired. The facilities offered by the Act would apply as much to a £lOO house as to a £3OO house. The working man, he contended, would benefit by the Act. Alderman ROBIRTS : Provided he has £6O to start with. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. LONGTON : Not exactly £6O, but a good propor- tion of the value. Ha had pleasure in supporting the adoption of the Act. Alderman SurreN said they would be able to deal with the Cases as they cropped up. As had been ob- served by Alderman Roberts' the principal object to be attained by adopting the Act was to keep the powers out of the hands of the County Council. The MAYOR said nothing would ba done, and no m oney would be advanced without the full approval of the Finance and Estate Committee. So that they might de- pend upon all these matters coming before them in d - tail.</p>
        <p>The resolution was adopted. NEW BYLAWS</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved :—" That the Councl do hereby make and adopt the Bylaws under the Musicipal Cor- poration Act, 1382, approved by the Council on th. 3rd October, 1899, and order the common seal to be affixel thereto."</p>
        <p>previous oectsiens. He could very truly eaderses every- thing "deice hal been saii aliernaan Setten, an it Ise tnieht he allowed to refer to the Mayoress tire, he could o-ily faintly eche the feelieg of regarci.l and respect in which she, was held, by emphasizing to the fullest possible exteat every word Aocleien in Sutton had uttered in regard to her. In regard to the Mayor hi 3 second year of office had bean an excee:ioaelly difficult sled arealua 0(.19. Wliri he looked over the list of attendence; of the membere of the Coen ih, he found that the Mayor head id the list with 178 attend ”3C6,1 at cemmitteen and Coancil meet- ings. That list furnished the best evidence of the enormous am yant of attention and time the Mayor had given to the work of that muni- cipelicv. Great as hod been toe duties devolving open him this year. they had to some extent perhaps been aggravated by—should he say—the utterly no- justifiable noe'tiou ot a very sated section of the coin- menity. He di i not wish to make that an eppertuuity of entering any defence of tee action of the Mayor in re- gard to the Bdl meetly adopted, but he did say that he had never in all his life seen statements made publicly by men who euent to know better, which centained snore !nelice and lege truth than those which were dis- played upon the walls of tke town in regard to the scion of the Corporation of which the Mayor had been the muce-respected and honoured head. Their sympathies went out to him because of the unjustifiable attacks made by this body of utterly incompetent and irresponsitale men, and ke :should take an early opportunity of dealing with this phase of the qeestion. He had great pleasure in secoudiag the resolution so ably prooosed. (Applause:) Alderman FRANCOMB said he heartily supported the motion, and endorsed every word that had been said by Alderman Sutten and Alderman Hebert&amp; Flo could 'URI up the question by saving that the Mayor had devoted a great am twit of time to his duties, and had also given a great amount of practical knowledge to the duties devolving upon him. He had done everything it was possible for a man to do as Mayor, and he was exceedingly pleased that the Mayoress had been included in the resolution. He hoped that where he retired from office, he would be long spared to be of use to the town. (hear, hear). Councillor LONGTON said he was pleased that ths Mayer oa retiring from the chair had earned the sincere and hearty thanks of all the members of the Council. It was a great honour to be elected Mayor for one year of such an important and ancient borough as Warrington, but it was a still greater compliment t 3 be re-elected in the following year with the unanimous support of the Council, ano the aporebation, he believed, of the whole of the ratepayers of the horoueh. That in Itself was a high oomplimentond that night the resolution of thanks would be unanimously agreed to by the Council. there were, of course, occasions which were very trying to a Mayor, but even when at a particular meeting some members lest their temper the Mayor's dignity was maintained under trying circumstances and his worship did the best that could be done, although he telieved that the importance of the proceedings wes rather ex- aggerated by toe press and the public. It was hardly fair that the whole of the Council should be branded in ceneequenc sof a ceuple of gentlemen having be too persistent in their arguments. (Laughter.) The DEPUTY MAYOR endorsed all that bad been said with regard to the Mayor. He withed to thank the Mayor for the relief he ha i elven him from duty as deputy Maor. Toe Mayor had given him no work at all to do. (Laugher.) The motion %as carried with unanimity and hearti- ness.</p>
        <p>Toe Mayon, in reply, said the speakers had spoken at such lenwh, and wi, ta so much praise for his services, that he felt somewhat embarassed, and he did not answer them as he should like to do. He had had so many votes of thanks daring the last few days that they really became embarassing, bit he must say that he should look back with p!e Asa's) and pride as long as he live I to the 17 or 18 years services ho ha l rendered to the C wporstion. (Hear, hear.) It might be truly said that tie had devoted a great deal of tinac to his work, mid he hoped he had done so to the ben interests of the town. (Hear, hear.) He loved his old town, and nothing grieved him more than to hear anyone say an unkind word about it. He owed them a great debt of gratitude for the kindness and courtesy they had shown to him, also his fellow townsmen,and particularly to the officials of the Corporaeion, whe had el ways been most courteous to him and helpful by granting him any information he might r-quire to carry out too duties of this honoarable office. tie could only say on behalf of his wife that she was very anxious indeed to do all that lay in her power for the benefit of the town, and it was through her entirely that he accepted office a second year, altnetaah lee was stricken down with illness at the time. She had done so little during the first year that she wished to do something during his second year of office, and ha was sure sic ned done her level best as Mayoress. (Ap- plause.) Now, about the future. There was, he sup- posed, no doubt that Alderman Roberts would be his successor, and he had been Siloyal to him that ho could promise Alderman Roberta that he would be equally leval to him. tie hoped every gentleman round the table would give laim all the support a Mayor required. With regard to the meeting of the Council, which had been referred to, he forgave the membirs in question, because there was no animus shown to him. He thanked them heartily for the kind manner in which they had treated him not only durieg his tw3 years of office but during the 18 years he had teem a naemher of the Cour:cit. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>THANKS FOR THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THANKS FOR THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON moved the tollowing motion : " That the best thanks of this Council be presented to John Edward Wrieht, Eq., alderman of this borough, for the satisfactory manner in which he has discharged the responsible duties devolving upon him as mayor and chief magistrate of the borough, during this his second year of office, and for the unvarying courtesy and impartiality displayed by him when pre- siding over the deliberations of the Coun- cil, and in his intercourse with its members." In doing so,he said the form in which the resolution was drawn wal somewhat of a stereotyped character, but be thought they would all agree with him that in what- ever forcible words the resolution was couched as ex- pressive of their feel,ngs towards the Mayor,they would only convey the thanks they felt in their hearts for the services renaered by him during the past two years. If in submitting this resolution be were to speak for an hour, he could not more effectually acknowledge on be- half of that Corporation the extent and value of those services than in the few words he would use. Alderman Wright had throughaut conscieatiously and faithfully Performed the duties of the office with dignity and p:?r- feet impartiality, and with A full desire to serve the town to the best of his ability. (Hear. hear.) They all knew in a town like 'Warrington, where the work of the Corporation was every year increasing and becoming more important, the mayoral duties could only be pro. perly carried out at much personal sacrifice of time, as well as pecuniary loss, and it was to his honour and credit that Alderman Wright had ever hetitated to devote whatever time and patience had been required for carrying out that work in a creditable mac- ner. (Hear, hear.) He must not omit, in proposing the resolution, to refer to the valuable aid and assistance the Mayor had always received from his excellent wife. (Applause.) It appeared to have been her fixed desire and determination to make his mayoralty as happy and successful as she could possibly make it. and there could be no doubt that her genial disposition and sincere attempts to do her very best to ple4si everybody', had won for her universal esteem. (H tar, hear.) He was every year more convinced that the success of aMavor greatly depended on the help hs received from hie wife, and he was more confirmed in the belief he had elways entertained that the " grey mare " was really the better " Mayor" of ths two. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) Be had pleasure in proposing the resolution, and he hoped everyone would give it their cardial support. (Applause.) Alderman ROEERTS said it had been his privilege to second votes of thanks similar to the present one on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>C0&amp;e,7.1. thee rose. STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>The C0&amp;e,7.1. thee rose. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE BURNING FATALITY TO A SERVANT.</p>
        <p>A Curious Coincidence.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held in the Room, at the War- rington Police Station on Monday by Mr. S. Brighouse county coroner, t Inching the deetleof Sarah Beardshall, a domestic servant, cAso died in the Infirmary en Thursday night from the effects of teerns.—The first witness was 8 pleira Beardshill, 33, Lloyd-street, mother of the deceased. She said her daughter was twenty years of are and was engaged by Mr. Robert Bruce Mur- ray, The Elms, Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath, as a dom'estic servant. On receiving information on Friday the 20th inst. that her dautiter had been burnt, she I went to the Infirmary where she saw deceased. She asked her how the accident happened and she replied "I was cleaning the firo irons in the kitchen when a hot cinder must have fallen on me dress. I got up and found my dress was on fire and I rushed to the dining room lio Mrs. Murray. See shut the door and I then ran out into the road." She saw her several times and was present when she died at the In- firmary at nine o'clock on Thursday night.— Alice Murray, wife of Robert Bruce Murray. said that cl-ceasied was her maid. At 9 o'clock on Friday, the 20th hie. see was in the dieing room when she heard a scream. She went into tha hall and saw deceased with her dress on fire. She shut the dining room door, and went into the sitting room and got a ',tree dust sheet which she wrapped round her. She could not extin- guish the flames and deceased pushed her on one side and ran out of the house throueh the f rent door. She (witness) followed her and saw a man put his arms newel her and with the assistance of another man put the flames out. Deceased was then brought into the house and Dr. Parker was sent for. He applied oils and ordered her removal to the Infirrnary.whereshe was taken , an the police ambulance. She (Mrs. Murray) thought a cinder must have fallen on her dress and set it on fire. —Joseph Cock, a labourer, residing at 32, Frederick- street, Latchford, said tbat at about 9 o'clock on Friday morning, the 20th nut , he was in Grappsnhall-road when the deceased rushed outof " 'The Elms" with her clothes on fire. He went to her, and with the assistance of another man succeeded in putting the flames out. 'ls put his arms round her to prevent the flames from rising,and the other man put his jacket round her. They also obtained a rug and wrapped it round her. The flames were rising above her head when she came out of ' the house.—The Coroner said that was a kind of acci- dent that seldom occurred, but it was a curious coinci- dence that when he left Warrington he had to go to Bootle to held an inquest on a domestic servant of the seine age as deceased who died on the mine date under the same circumstances.—The , foreman thought it would be a good thing if servants could be recommended to lie on the floor when their clothes caught fire.—A verdict of "Accidental Death " was returned.—Mr. Murray said he would like it to be understood that his wife did not shut the door and leave the girl in the hall. She came out of the adjoining room and did what she could for her. She shut the door because an infant child and an old lady were in the dinine-room. the chill heiele only partially dreved.</p>
      </div>
