      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S FORTHCOMING CONCERT. The " Faust" Legend.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S FORTH- COMING CONCERT. The " Faust" Legend.</p>
        <p>It has been thought rhst, in connectien with the Concert Recital by the Warrinetou Musical Siety of Gounod'e opera of "Ffust," on Wedneeiay net seer article on the intereving nd extrenie'e fertil tu!ije:t of the Legend itself may not be found unacceptable. That a personify kaocen as D-. Faust, or Faustus, from wile's!' history the Faust L ,end took its rise, ~ctually lived in the earlier part of the 16th century seems beyond deuat. Ha is spoken of in the writings of contemporeries. Lie is maid to have tsetse born either at Kaittlingen in Wil:teraburg, or at Rda near Weimer. His parents were poor, but the begetet of a wealthy Linde enabled him to study medicine and fliagi•oi at Crac sea. Ha is described in a let`er written in 1507 as Faustus Junior., and as bting skilled in neerorrancy, astrology, magie, chirornaeov, and other doubtful sciences. His character appears from general testimony to have been of the worst description Tee date of his death is uncertain, but it was not later than 1544, for Johann G .et, a Protestant Oleo- , logien, relates that he himself sapped with Faust at Basel,where he was attended by a dog and horse that were evidently demons, and further describes hie re- markable end.how tne devil straugled hint, and how his dead body lay cencantly on its fee° on the bier, aithough turned upwards as often as five times. Another bantemporary confirms the account that he died in an inn, during the night, at the hands of his fainilar spirit, when his hour had come. Lather is said to have spoken freqcently of Faust in conversation, as an illustration of tkm craft and wickedness of the devil. It has bean areued that, as the real Faust called him- self Faustus junior, there was an earlier Faust ; there is no record of him however, possibly the name had already become typical.</p>
        <p>TG what extent the Faust Legend take* it rise from the recognition of the elemental struggle between the higher and the lower natures in man—on tne one Naiad the deliberate choice of evil, on the otAer an uneatiafied aspiration towards the highest goad—how far, in short Faust is but a type ; how much is dun to the still older conception of a compact with the Evil One ; and lasly, what are its bearings in relation to the conflict between kermanism and Protestantism in Germanv at the penal of the Reformation, are questi-ms of great interest, but of course, far beyond the scope of the present slight aket 71.</p>
        <p>The first literary vercion of the Faust story SVAI pub- lished in 1587, at Frankfort. It had extraordinary popu- larity and was quickly translated into English, Dutch, French, and Finnish. Tile Eiglish translation is entitled " The Damnable Life and Deserve' Deith of Dr, John Faustus." It rves reprinted only a few years ago in an editioe edited by Pr-if. lienry Morley, ani published by Routledge. In this rernarka.ble book it is recordeJ that Faust 'tilde a c3inpact with the Devil, signing away his soul in return fo; the services Mephietopheles for twenty-four years. Toe fiend supplies him wiih means, wherewitn he and 1113 familiar, Wagner (not the Wagner of Gounod's opera) are able to live in luxary.</p>
        <p>few nf the necromantic feats recorded in this curious and edifying biography Inay be mentioned. Fuit devours a peasant's had of h sy, tog &gt;the? with too n ,rse and wagon, produces fresh apples and grapss in January, draws forth wine from a table, and raises from the shades the beautiful Helen of Troy, wit n whom he afterwards lives, and who bears him a son.</p>
        <p>The end, as here recorded,is sufficiontly awe-inspiring. "On the night on which his twenty-four year expired he was in company with a group of Studonts in a tavern of the village of Rimlich, near Wittenberv, and to them he made a long address expreqiing his (leip penitence, afbor which he remained alone in his chamber. At midnight a fearful storm arose, horrible hisses As of a thousand serwats were heard, and for a little while the agonising cries of Faust, with a hollow and suffocated sound ; but soon all wag still, in the morning t.lle fl3or of the room was fouud stained. with blood, his brains were spattered on the walk, and his body eras found outside, the head and every number hanging halt torn off."</p>
        <p>Other versions of the story quickly ann,eared, and the enormous extent t 3 whicl ttie 1* auat story has takln hold of the popular imagination may be rsaliied by the fac!., that there was publighed in Germane, in 1815, I c•ini- plate catalogue of Faus:', litorature, in which tne nuather of Reim given by toe hibhgrapber is two tauusAnd seven hundred and fourteen.</p>
        <p>From ti MIAS of mAtter two noble works must be singled out. (1.) Toe Dpc:or Fauitus of th English dramstist, written about the year 1539, which however crude, and even puerile in pans, couttirs some of the fineqt dranattic pamtry in our language, for in- fit trlrtfl -</p>
        <p>" Was this the fate that launel'i a thousand ships, And burnt the topless towers of Muni." " 0, thou art fairer than the evening air, Clad in the beauty- of a thousand stars." And the oft quoted</p>
        <p>" Cut is the branch that might hare grown full atraight, And burned is Apollo's laurel bough." (2.) The" Faust "of Goethe (completAri in 1831) in which id achieved the transforma7.l3o of Faustus from • vulgar conjuror into a persooific ition of humanity. It is to Goethe, mars over, tl%t we owe the exquisite conception of Marguerite (Gretchen). In more modern poetry we have the " Manfred "of Byron, the " Paracelsus "of Browning, the " BaldAr " of Dobell, the " Spiridion" of Grior.le Sand, tha "Brand" of Ibsen, the " Tannloi odor," cf Grisebazh ; awl in fiction, the " Z anoni " and th 3 " Msrgrave," of Lord Lytton—.2ll betraying room or less affinity with tha typical figure of Faust. Tire familiar " Golden Legend" of Longfellow is practically the gains story with a happier ending.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Feusr ix ART</head>
        <p>Feusr ix ART</p>
        <p>First in importance are the ancient wall paintings in Auerbach's cellar at Leipzig, bearing date 1525, that is to say during the lifetime of the real Faust. fhAn there is a well-known engraving by Rembrandt, and others (still older) by von Sichem. Nx the illustra.tions to Goethe'a •• Faust" by Cornelius, Rltzsch, Scileertz Seelbach, and Kreeling. Tinier this heading may IN; included (for brevity's sake) the picturesque drama pro- duced by Henry Irving at the L7ceura Theatre, in 1885, an adaptation of aoe:he's play by W. G. Wills, FAUST IN MUSIC. In a very useful and interesting series of articles no the Fau.t Legend, and its mulical treatment. published in the Musical Tunes of 1886 Mr. Frederick Corder enumerates no fewer than thirty-one more or less impor- tant compositions founded on the story of Faust. He admits that there may be, and probably are, many more.</p>
        <p>The most noteworthy are Sp3hr's opera of " Faust " (1818) ; Lindpliuter's (1791-1856) 0./enure and Inciden- tal Music to Goethe's Faus', ; E !ward Lessee's (1876) " Music to Goethe's Faust "of which the writer speaks with enthusiasm ; Berlioz's " Faast " (1846) long neg- lected, but since its production by Charles Halls in 1880, a favourite work with the larger chsral 800ietie4— G3uood'3 opera of " Faust " (1859) ; opera, " Mefistofele " (1868) ; Richard Wagner's " A Faust Overture" (1839) ; and Liezt's (1811-1836), " A Faust Symphony:"</p>
        <p>_ -Mr. Corder remarks in conclusion that no other sub- ject has ever evoked so much interest among musicians as Faust bau done.—ln their hands we may leave him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Gounod.</head>
        <p>Gounod.</p>
        <p>The life of CHARLNB GOUNOD—tmlike those of so many of the greater musicians—does not furnish very muck material for the biographer. He was born in Paris, 17th June, 1818, received his early musical eduaation from his mother, a dis- tinguished pianist, and at the age of 18 entered the Conser- vatoire, where three years later ho gained the Grand Prir de Rome. Of his residence in Rome his whole career bears trims, for here he chieffy occupied himself with the study of the old masters, espeeially Palestrina. In 1843 he returned te Paris. visiting Austria And Germany on the way and becoming acquainted with the music of Robert Sehumann. In Paris he became organist of the Missims etrangeres and even attended for two years a course of theology, with the intention of taking orders ; later, however, he determined to devote himself to music. His first success as a composer was his " Masse Solennelle," produced in 1851. This wee followed by his first opera, " Sapho " (1851), by the choruses to " Ulysse " (1852) by the opera " La Norma Saoglante " (1854), and by " Le Medecin malgre lui (1858). But It was not until 1859 that Gouned, then over 40 years of age, produced the great work that at once established his position in the domain of music. This was " Faust," pro- duced March 19, 1859, at the TheatrelLyrique, with Mime. Miolan-Carvalho as Marguerite. The libretto, founded on Gcethe, by Messrs. Barbier and Car* if it gives little of the spirit of Gcethe, is yet admirable as a drama, and :Jeyond praise as an opera boo The first production of the opera in England wee at tier Majesty's Theatre, June 11, 1863. Following " Faust " came the operas "Philemon at Bands " (180), " La Keine de Saba " (1862), " Mireille ' (1864), and " Rom6o et Juliette (1867). From 1879 to 1875 Gounod was residing in England, where he lived a retired life, seeming to shrink from public notice, and disdaining public applause ; he appeared, however, at the Philharmonic. the Crystal Palace, and a few other concerts. After his return to France wee produced the operas " Cinq Mars (1877), Polyeucte " (1378), and "Le Tribut de Zamora (1881). In sacred music Gouned has given us in addition to several masses, at least one orator', of the very highest class, viz : " The Redemption," proclaimed at the Birming- ham Festival, 1882, aud followed in 1885 by its sequel. More at Vets.' Of his smaller works such as ' Gallia," his cherch music, his songs such as 1. Nazareth " and " Le \Talton," his "Funeral March of a Marionette," and his " Meditation on a Bch Prelude," the popularity is un- qnestionable. lie died at St. Cloud, Oct. 18, 1893. -</p>
        <p>Gounod was a consummate mast4r of the orchestra, in the treatment of which be has originated many beautiful and impressive effets. There is a singular blending in his music of the mystic with the voluptuous —sensuous melodies with solemn harmonies of a religious character. That he had not the gift of humour was stiewn conspicously in his treat- ment of Molibre's he 'Melee:is malgre lul, Of all his operas "Faust "has retained the most permanent hold on the operatic public of today, bat h. is a tribute to the essential beauty of Gouttod's music that, however unsuccessful as Operas some of his works hay, been thy have all contributed charming morceaux for the enjoyment of concert andienees. A.S.R.</p>
