      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>* **-**-*-********-*-**</p>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting. The annual meeting of the Warrington Trades and Lab )ur C3uncil was bell at the" Waggon and Horses" Inn, Bucterrnarket-street, on Tuesday evening. Mr. A. C. Binki presided and there were also. present Mesirs. Plumb, Rathbone, Grealey, Main, Yeary, Worris,ft Bird, El., Lee, Baker, Parsons, Burrows, Massey, Sivonry, Plinston, Wilcock, Edwardson. Fargber, Whitby, Whit- taker, and.W Bullock (secretary).</p>
        <p>ployers in the district, the Council is pleased to state that there has only been one cause of dispute during the past year with trades affiliated with the Council mad their employers, viz : ['be filesiniths of Warring- ton and district, who attar 25 years silenes have awakened to the necessity of tr)ing to better their con- dition. The dispute in question is so fresh in the memory of everyone that it nesds but a few remarks of mine in explanation thereof. Previous to July 1899. negotiations were opened between the local Megaliths and their employers, with a view to a revision of their price list, which has practically been the same for 50 years ; the employers ignore I all commenications ad- dressed to them on the question, and the association used all available means to avert a dispute, but as a last resource a communication was for warded to the employers that having received no reply to the request for an interview as a means of an amicable arrangement being arrived at, or the recification of the anemones existing, the men would cease their contracts on a certain date, no answer to the questions being forthcoming or any recogn!tioe of their grievance being entertained by the employers, there was no other alternative but resert to the bitter arbitrament of strike. On June 29th, 1899, the men ceased work, the whole ef them were out 15 weeks when one firm made arrange- ments satisfactorily for their employees to resume work; the reminder will being out at the time of writing. The Council having endorsed their action in the effort to obtain a reasonable living wage, strongly recommended them to the various trades' eoeieties as being worthy of supeort, and are pleased to state there are some sotie- ties, local and provincial, whieeo have levied their mem- bers so much per week until the dispute is settled. We conseler the filesmiths have a just cause for complaint, when, atter having served seven rare apprenticeship to the trade, ',Ages on the whole do not exceed £1 per week, this being utterly insufficient to ensure for them a decent existence, and it remains for branches and societies to rally round this little army of trades union- ists, r eider them all the support possible, and secure for them that which is just and right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>TEM MUNICIPAL ELICTION3.</head>
        <p>TEM MUNICIPAL ELICTION3.</p>
        <p>The CIUIRTIAN said be had received letters of apology for non-attendance from Messes Podrn-ore,. Stott, Nswall, Weaver, Brows and Plumb; they were unable to be preient as they were taking part in chi electioa in Latch feed Ward.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLums said he ha I besn rsqumsted by the dale- gates who had sent the letter to ask the Council if they could ese their way to po4tpone the election of officers for a week. They would like to have attended the meeting hut unfortunately could not come that night on ancoant of the election.</p>
        <p>air. GazaLir tn)v,.d that the election of offilera take place in a fortnight buZ aftlr discassina it Wl4 de- cidel that the offilers h lectei that night.</p>
        <p>THE ANNUAL REP MT.</p>
        <p>The SICEINTAAY (Mr. W. Bullock) read his annual repoi as follows: In submitting nay fifteenth annual report and balanee sheec, and the 24th of the Coun- cil, I am pleased t 3 c mgratulate the Council upon its increased niemherehip, there bring three secieties who have beclele affiliated during the past year, viz :—N wstgants heal branch ; wheel wrigits and blacksmitiss, and boiler makere, which is indicative that local branched and societies generally, are begin- n'a? ato recoznize the real urgent necessity for combination. I may here m ,ntiou that un ier circum- stances over whin we had no control, we were unable to i.sire a printed rep )rt and balance sheet f3r the year 1898, and with reference to that period we are pleased to state there were no secessions but an ine.rease ty twar notable branches, viz :—The Amalgamated Society of Engineers, and the Warrington breach of the NAtional Uai ma of Shop Asaistants, clerks, Sic. Daring the past year the council has been as active and infl rential as ia any preceding year. and local indu trier and trade have experienced a some- what prosper lug period, all branches of industry having beea fairly well employed, an i statistics will show that it has been in a better state all over the country which must be welcome to all. During the past year the business of tee council has ben carried on in a steady, and progresaive manner,aad the attendanoes of delegates have been exceptionally good.</p>
        <p>LABOUR REPERSENTLTION.</p>
        <p>During the past year, Mr. Malkin as representative of the working class on the Board of Guardians gave an account of his stewardship for tie past twelve months, and an address upon poor law ad miaistration, which was instructive ani interesting. Tue Council having regard to labour representation on the public bodies of the Borough, endorsed the action of Mr. Malkin in contest- ing Bewsey Ward as an independent candidate, although he was not the nominee of the Ceuncil and being a labour representative on the Board of Guardians it was con- sidered a duty to render him all the support possible to secure his return, and having made the contest a tie, necessitated a seeond election, with the result that Mr. Malkin was defeated by a small m sjerity. It is the intention of the Council to give their support to bona-fide Isleciur candidates who c )me forward in the interes: of the workers, as the interests of their class can b)st be accomplished by sending members of their own class to represent them whether imperially or locally.</p>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>This lubjeot is one that has undoubtedly given rise to a great amount of interest ani earn3stness on the part of the Council daring th 3 past year. And it is a matter for profound regret that the question has not, so far, re- ceivei from our legislators the attention which its im- portance dsmands. To many soliciting Parliamentary honours it has already proved a grad electioneering cry. The extent of pauperism among the aged and de- servinz poor which is revealed by Parliamentary reports and Poor Law statistic; is truly alarming. Were our rich legislators as moots in earnest to lessen-by legisla- tion the pa' Ity in our midst, as the poor are to relieve one another, there would, are this, have been practical legislation on the subject. Many schemes have b3en submitted to the Council and discussed. The result of the deliberatioes in the Council was that it was decided to support any scheme that may be brought before the House ef C ommons, to be non-contributory and the age limit be 60 years for pars ms to &amp;aim ; and that the member for the Borough, Mr. R. Pierpoint, be requested te give the same his supnort, to which the hon. gentle- man replied that this difficult question hal bean referred to a committee, and that no Bill on the matter would be brought forward by the Government this session. L&amp;VC&amp;SHIRIS TIMM COUNCILS' FSDERA.TI)N.</p>
        <p>This question engaged the attention of the Council. Early in the year, a communication was received frum the Manchester and Salford Trades' Council that by a federation of this ellaract3r tha workers of Lancashire would bes brought closer in touch, and that simultane- ous action might be taken by the various Trades Coun- cils on any question affecting the interest of the workers, and after considering the matter it was resolved to j )in the federation. When the firat meeting was hell in Manchester January Tch, 1899 to appoint officers and executive committee t.venty-two Trades' Councils were represented and subsequent meetings were held for the cl isideration of Important questions social and industrial. Several Trades Federation Slvmes have been before the Council but notning of a definite character has been arrived at in this direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>APPEALS.</head>
        <p>APPEALS.</p>
        <p>There being many appeals to the Council from differeet parts of the country during the past twelve months the Council had in the mej ority of cases renommenced them to the varines societies affiliated with the Council for their sympathy and sup- pert, and in most cases were responded to tnrough the different branches. They were so numerous however, during the year, that all could not receive the.suppert which was needed to carry on their struggle, or resiat encroachments by unprincipled employer ; and having regard to gn appeal locally every endeavoar was pushed forward to render what aid was possible. Appeals were made from Oxford Tra les' Council, International Peace Crusade, Trade and Labour Protest to the Government, Ruskin Hall Association, Shop Assistants and o Mrs, for the moral support of the Council—the latter was an application to support a bill introduced in the House ot Commons by Sir Charles Dike, the special features of which are for a uniform and earlier closing of shops and and a deputation waited upon the Member for the Borough, Mr. Pierpoint, and the interview was instruc- tive and satisfactory, showing the difficulties a private member of the House hal in gettine a bill through com- mittee. He promised it should have his earnest con- sideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ORGANHATION,</head>
        <p>ORGANHATION,</p>
        <p>We are glad to view with satisfaction the past and present efforts at organisation, and direct if poeeible the mind's eye to the future that lies before us ; let our aim he to build up a streng and powerful organisation. Ex- periences recently have proved that no branch or Society, or Association, eeuld successfully compete against the federation of capital, if dieoreaniseci Union- ism is a path that is leading upwards,in wages, in hours of working, and social conditions. In every instance when application has been made to the council for assist- mice to form a society for the advancement of their in- terests or from those already in existence to develop and strenethen their respective Unions, representatives from the council have been appointed t o render every pessible hell,. An invitation was given to the Council to attend a parade ani church service of the Warrington branch of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants which was well attended and collections were made en-route in aid of the Orphans Fixed connected with the Association, and an open meeting of the same branch was held in the Coeeperative Hall, Sankey-street Sunday October Bth, to hear an address by their general secretary, Mr. R. Bell. He being called away at the last moment, Mr. Hudson, organising secretary for Ireland was a very good substitute. Several other branches, and societies have been visited by delegates of this Council during the year, and have been the means of meeting a stronger spirit of unity than previously ex- isted. I have every reason to believe that the Council will be still better organised the ensuing year than the one just closed.</p>
        <p>DISPUTES: As showing thl good feeling existing between em-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RULES.</head>
        <p>RULES.</p>
        <p>During the past year the Ceuncil have revised and had printed the rules for the guidance of the couneil, and which come into operation in the ensuing year. It having come to the knowledge of the Ceuneil recently that s,cieties do not return a reasonable proportion of ther financial members at the end ot each quarter we trust they will do so in future, it being otherwise itl2- pwiblo to give a fair estineste of Trades' Unionists aaiatee with the Council. There are twenty-twe societ;ee representine upwards of 1.400 members. We feel sure there are mere memhers whe might be returned and ao increase the contributions adequately to carry on the ordinary business of the Council. Many matters of interest are necessarily passed over in this report, but I trust enough has been set forth to encourage all Trades Unionists to persevere in their eff 'tele to secure an im- proveeeent in their social conditions. Ia conclusion I may mention a complaint frem the Ileirlressers' Associa- tion th it members of Trad.A' Organisations have their oasorial operatio is performed at shops where individuels ollow other employment during the day and that of hairdressing, See, at night and it is the desire of the Council that those shops should only be patronised. which are recognizsd by their association. I thank the delegates for the courtesy and kindness shown towards me and the officers during the past year, and hope that unity of action will be the aspiretioa of all. The GEMMEL/4 said they thanked the Secretary very much for the report. Tne amalgamation of trades to the Council during the year had been very satisfactory. On th 3 motion of Mr. IlaTaßows,secondei by Mr. WHITBY the report wag unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>TITS FAMILIES_ OF LOCAL -RESERVISTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wzt.comt proposed the following resolu-tiOn "That we, the members of the Warrington Trades' Council use our beat endeavours to bring before the notice of our trades' committees the advisability of taking up a weekly col'ection to assist the wives and children of the reserve men who are or may ba sailed upon in tiais town to fight for their Q4een and country." M. RATEIB )NE seconded and the motion was carried' unanimously.</p>
        <p>ELECTION or OFFICERS. The following officers were elected for the ensuing year i—Prelident. Mr A C Biuks (Amalgamated En- ginesrs); vic,-president.tdr J El wardson (wiredrawers); executive, Messrs Malone (railway servants), Greeley (bakers), Woods (h)ilermakers), Plinston (assurance agents), Malkin (ironworkns) ; secretary, Mr W Bullock (wiredrawers) ; treasurer, Mr J Battersby (filestniths). The CHAIRMAN in returning thanks for his election as president said he hoped the ensuing twelve months would be the most prosperous inexistence of the Counoil. (Applause.) He congratulated the tailors on their amal- gamation with the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FATAL FALL DOWNSTAIRS.</head>
        <p>A FATAL FALL DOWNSTAIRS.</p>
        <p>leMr. S. Brighouse, minty coriner, conducted an in- quiry at the Police S:ation on Monday afternoon into the circumstances attending the death of Samuel it alphs, 1, Gandy's-yard, who died at the Warrington Infirmary on Satarday from injuries received by fallingdownstaira. —Detective Barlow said that deceased's wife was unable to attend the inquest as she T. as ill. Lie had obtained a statement from her to the effect that at 2.40 on Satur- day the 21st Octob?r deceased went upstairs with the intention of going to bed. She went into the yard and on returning five minutes afterwards found him stand- ing at the bottom of the stairs with no cloth- ing on but his shirt, bleeding from a wound at the back of the head. She asked what was the matter and he said he had fallen down stairs. He was taken to the Infirmary bie, refused to stop there so he was allowed to go home. On Sunday he became worse and was again taken to the Infirmary where he was detained. He (witness) thought that deceased must have gone on the landing to shout to his wife, and had fallen down stairs. Ho was perfectly sober at the time of the accident. He had worked at one place for twenty-five years and had never lost a day.—The Coroner said that was a very good character.—Nurse Hill said that deceased was admitted to the Infirmsry on Sunday the 22nd met, suffering from 3. fractured base of the skull and died at 1-15 on Saturday lait from acute pneurnolia. He had been to the Infirmary the previous Saturday but would not stay in.—A verdict of "Accidental Death" was re turned.</p>
        <p>CSP Per Cent,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>HUMAN AILMENTS</head>
        <p>HUMAN AILMENTS</p>
