      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>THE comment of the Con- The e Continent Country and tm • ental Press and the mani- fest feeling displayed in one or tw3 capitals should be of value in compelling us to close our ranks and to cease wrangling about the origin of the war. Any disaster we nay have to bear will gratify our enemies on the Continent, and dispose them to make and write the most irritating and unjust remarks. It has always been so, and the only novelty about the matter is that, owing to the more rapid trans- mission of news, we are able to read what is said before the smart of disaster is over. Our duty is to bear these assaults with fortitude, and to re- member that the dignity with which we carry ourselves in misfortune, and the confidence with which we look to ultimate success, cannot fail to make an impression on our slanderers. In France the feeling is one of exultation, and it can readily be accounted for. We have reduced French influence in Egypt to a minimum, we have delivered the final blow at Mandism, and we have defeated the French plan of cutting us off from Central Africa by compelling the evacuation of Fashoda. There are journals in Paris which minister to the lowest feelings which animate the boulevardivs and the bitter enmity.of the hirelings of different political parties. They can never re- port any good of us, and they are, therefore, in great glee just now over &amp; misfortune which is hardly greater than France herself has suffered in Northern Africa with little hop 3of actual redress. The better class of people in France do not share these malignant animosities. They know better than to think that we have wrecked our chance of victory in South Africa, and that we shall be speedily suing for intervention by the European Powers. In Holland and in some parts of Ger- many, where there have been strong sympathies for theßeers, we cannot expect our misfortunes to be discussed with regret. It is rather sad to know that in Italy and Austria there is a strange disposition to regard with satisfaction the reverse we have suffered. The more sober opinion of Germany is of a somewhat different complexion. It is admitted that our weakness for the moment is the result of a prolonged diplo- matic struggle, in which we could hardly have des- patched sufficient troops to South Africa without precipitating the war we were striving to avoid, and it is suggested that even now we are relying too much on an army which is smaller than the work we have to do will require. The Russian press has been as bitter against us as it can be, and this is the more remarkable as the CZAR has arrested a movement for enrolling volunteers for the Transvaal, and the educated Russians are not anti-British. It is pleasing to know that the attempt to collect subscriptions for the Transvaal in Southern Russia has been a failure. The drift of opinion OR the Continent goes to show that our strength and resources are respected, whilst there is rejoicing over misfortunes that may lower our pride. Our isolation creates feelings of annoyance and jealousy, and the passive attitude of the German Government adds to the volume of senti- ment against us. Events, indeed, are driving us more and more into close relations with Germany, and it is well we should note the fact.</p>
        <p>THE problem of housing the</p>
        <p>Cheap Housing.</p>
        <p>working classes has been with</p>
        <p>us now for several years, and it seems likely to remain. Public improvements , formerly exercised a strong influence in making overcrowding common. But though we have passed special legislation providing for the re- housing of families dispossessed by clearances we have only touched the outside of a very large ques- tion. It is, perhaps, greater in London than any- where else, and that often leads to the belief that it is almost entirely a London question. That it is not so a little inquiry is sufficient to demon- strate. Towns grow and population concentrates, so that the same question is constantly suggesting itself. In the rural districts there is also a similar problem, complicated by local considerations,of ten urgent and seldom easy to deal with. In the towns rents are high because accommodation is often scanty, and high rents for poor dwellings, or parts of dwellings, invariably lead to ether evils. It hardly appears that any one solution of the pro- blem is possible, but the direction in which public opinion is travelling is towards the provision of cheap houses by municipal and local authorities. Primarily there is the question of land, and that presses in the towns. Land cannot be procured at low prices, and where it can be so obtained it is usually at distances from the centre which bring in another matter—that of cheap conveyance from the suburb to the centre. Where the local authority is working the trams this seconlary difficulty has been satisfactorily met. Land and material are dear, and hence the prime outlay en a house fit for a workman andlis family is con- siderable. Until we can reduce this outlay it seems to us that we shall be unable to master the problem. The price of land is governed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>by many facts, and we hardly see how it is to be made much cheaper. Suitable sites may be found, and if compulsory purchase, with small legal expenses, were possible, much might be done in towns as well as in the country. The question of materials and structure is by no means free from difficulty. The more elaborate are the pro- visions of building and sanitary authorities, the greater the cost of construction. If we could utilise some other materials besides stone and brick there might be a hope for cheaper building. As to what materials are available we cannot say. A mixture of wood and brick is no longer favoured, and iron is too dear to be much used. The point is of sufficient importance, however, to command attention, and we should like to see it seriusly o discussed by competent persons. Little origin- ality has been displayed hitherto in this line of structure. Houses are built to last for many years, and then durability, of course, adds to the cost of erection. In towns there can hardly be any question of using any other materials than bricks. But in the country it might be possible to construct houses partly of wood and partly of brick that would last a considerable time and be not too costly. The suggestion must be limited to regions where wood is fairly abundant. But in the colonies comfortable houses are erected of corrugated iron qnd wood that last for years, and ought to be possible in many parts of this country. As things go, they would probably not be per mitted, and hence we must not blame the land- owner in every case for high rents. The cheap house is the thing to discover, and when we have found it the rural part of the problem will be much easier of solution. In the towns the new builders, whoever they may be, will have to be content with low profits if rents are to be reduced by one-third, which seems to be essential.</p>
        <p>There is reason for seriously considering whether we, in Warrington, should not have an" Indepen- dence Day" entirely our own. November Ist, 1899, was undoubtedly a great day for indepen- dence and might possibly be worth celebration. Independent candidates contested four of the wards in the town in open defiance of the political edict that there was to be compromise and no fighting. We could have admired their indepen- dence had they been men of light and leading, but until they came forward as candidates for the con- fidence of the ratepayers as little was known of their independence as of their existence. That they were defeated goes without saying, and the electors generally showed an independence of the independent candidates which must have been mortifying to these champions of disinterestedness and freedom. The war-cry was all right but the veices which called it were not those of men whom the ratepayers could be expected to look up to and bestow th3ir confidence upen. In this fact lies the secret of the sad fall which the independent candi- dates have had. They were men of straw—espec- ially one of them—and as such have found that independence alone is but a weak reed to depend upon.</p>
        <p>The Ratepayers' Association, if it has not shone ref ulgently, has been very prominent throughout the election. Three of the candidates, Messrs. Gough, Cunnington, and Cliff° were nominees of the Association, and attached their names to a poster manifesto emanating :from it. This state- ment of" reasons why," etc had the usual hall- mark of illiteracy and bristled with unfounded as- sertions and insinuations from beginning to end. Nobody is more in favour of the criticism of kublic affairs than we are, but the form it takes when in the hands of the Ratepayers' Association is repul- sive to every right-minded and gentlemanly in- dividual. Here we have unwarranted attacks on men who strive hard,—if not always with the hap- piest results—to carry out their pledge to the electors and improve the town they govern. The Ratepayers' Association—an excellent name for a motley assembly—does not hesitate to roundly assert that the control of affairs is in the hands of an inner circle, the members of which are accused of working for their own interests, ' while cheap sneers at the expense of deputations, salaries, and other matters about which the best informed adherents of the Association can have only a very perfunctory knowledge, follow in due course. Then to cap all, comes the following grandiloquent paragraph: "We want men of independent character and courage to represent us, men who can be relied upon to resist all pres- sure from interested parties, and who will work for one object only : the interest and welfare of the general body of ratepayers." The names of the Association's nominees follow,and the obvious inference if that they are the paragons of virtue "wanted," Figures are said to be able to prove anything; there is nothing more certain than that the figures announced from the Town Hall steps proved the disbelief of the ratepayers in the mem- bers of the Ratepayers' Association.</p>
        <p>Believers in signs and portents who have re- posed faith in the prognostications of Professor Falbe, the German astronomer, may take com- fort from the intelligence that, with the near approach of the day on which the fulfilment of his prophecy is due, the Professor is wisely beginning to hedge. The comet may not destroy the world on the 13th inst. after all. It may miss the earth, or it may be powerless to work any harm—which is just what the Greenwich authorities said in plainer language long ago. The day, indeed, is past when half-baked scientists could alarm the world with supposititious horrors, and frighten the weak-minded into a premature grave. Mauper- tuis has shown us the optimistic side of the picture in his reply to the dismal Lambert. Lambert 'saw "the Evil One behind every door." He predicted that a comet would one day bring back the universal deluge, or make the earth perish in a lake of fire, or shatter it to small dust, or drive it outside the orbit of Saturn, and thus inflict on us a winter several centuries long, with slow torture and death for this world's inhabi- tants. Maupertuis points out, however, it is just as likely a collision would so drive the earth that we should enjoy perpetual spring, or that the destruction of the comet in the collision would fill a whole kingdom with gold and diamonds. But, after all, these planetary disturbances have been predicted from the earliest ages, and have never happened; and the world does well to remain , sceptical of both good and evil.</p>
        <p>The decision of the Government not to send a Volunteer regiment to the front created disap- pointment on the one hand, and gratification on the other. It is natural that military men who have made the army their profession should resent the importunity of occasional soldiers, who would share those rewards of war which the regulars have been pursuing for years. But, as Lieutenant- Colonel Balfour points out in the " Contemporary Review," it is fair to look upon the question in a more serious light. The country pays for the Volunteer force in the hope that it will obtain a reasonably Gtrong defensive force for the home district. But it is idle to argue that a force which never witnesses war can be made efficient by mere peace manoeuvres. The Volunteers are a gallant set of men, but auccess in the battlefield does not fall to the brave unless bravery is leavened by scientific knowledge. A fair example of what we mean is afforded by the capture of Lieutenant-Colonel Möller and his Hussars. The Lieutenant-Colonel and his men, though they had trained for a number of years, had never had actual experience of the real thing, and they fell ingloriously into an ambush. To deny their readi- ness and bravery would be absurd. It would be absurd to deny the bravery and readiness of the Volunteer force. But that fcrce cannot be ex- pected to fulfil its purpose properly if it is debarred horn practising real war when the chance comes along.</p>
        <p>There are still some members of the Manchester City Council Nitho cling tenaciously to the idea of discharging the sewage of Manchester into the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3, 1899.</p>
        <p>tidal waters of the Mersey, a little above Warring- ton, by means of a culvert. The simplicity of the scheme is its most charming feature and it may be likened unto the device of the housewife who hav- ing swept her house, dumps the rubbish at her neighbour's door. At the meeting of the City Council held last week a proposal was brought forward to revert to the culvert scheme in prefer- ence to the construction of bacteriological beds for the purification of sewage. We are gla.i to notice that such a policy of despair as turninc, untreated sewage into the Mersey did not rasa with any measure of support, and the inhabitants of War- rington and district may breathe freely once more. Dean Maclure at the Manchester Diocesan Confer- ence last week was kind enough to say that War- rington had " an atmosphere' and and suggested that the Training College should be removed in conse- quence. During the hot weather Warrington un- doubtedly has "an atmosphere," and one of no mean powers. The worthy dean might have gone further and stated that Manchester was largely to blame for it by polluting the Ship Canal and the tributaries of the Mersey. If the culvert scheme of the Manchester Corporation had been carried into effect, Warrington would have had the sewage of Manchester washed against its shores by every tide, and the unpleasant conditions which already prevail would have increased to such an extent as to make Warrington an unfit place to live in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST BAZAAR, WIDNES. The bazaar which wai held last week in Lacey-street schoolroom, in aid of a new chapel for the Primitive Methodists, realized the handsome sum of £250. PRESENTATIONS AT THE BOARD SCHOOLS, RUNCORN. The teachers of the boys' department recently ere- sented Mr. A E Parr, one of their colleagues-, with a fancy bamboo music table, in token of their good wishes on the occasion of his marriage. Mr. W Joynson, who lately left the school to take an appoint- ment at Simm's Cross Board School, Widnes, has been the recipient of a handsome writing desk in evidence of the esteem in which he was held by his late colleagues. SidocKING ACCIDENT TO A RUNO)RN SHUNTER. About noon last Monday, Robert Bold, a young married man of 3. Speakman-street, Runcorn, was admitted into the Accident Hospital, Widnes, suffering severely from injuries received whilst shunting at West Bank Dock. Bold had charge of a horse which was pul- ling a wagzeu to be loaded, when the horse became restive and threw him down, the waggon passing over his lee and the wrist of his left arm. The doctors at the hospital thought it necessary to operate on the injured limbs. Much sympathy is felt for his young wife. ST. HELENS ROWDIES AT WIDNES. At Widnes, on Monday, before the Mayor and Major Lewis, Thomas Fallow, J. Carney, J. Farriker, A.Culleo, and J. Campbell, all of St. Helens, were charged with be- ing drunk and disorderly. On Saturday a large party came from St. Helens to see the football match between Widnes and St. Helens, and the police in the evening had an anxious time. Campbell was also charged with assaulting Police-constable Thornton by kicking him on the leg; A fine of 23 61 and costs for being drunk was imposed on Campbell, and 51 for the assault. The others were mulcted in amounts of 5e 6.1 each. WEEKLY CONCERT at. MERSEY MIsSION. Tnesday eight last,Miss Dyer's coucert party favoured the sailors and boatmen with their presence at the Mersey Mission, and rendered a capital prograneine. The jovial missioner, Mr. W. Shaw, presided,and a large audience showed unmistakable Appreciation of the several items which follow :—Pianuforte set°, Mies Garvie; songs from Messrs Neill, J Hutchinsori,C Thorn, Captain fucker, Master Buckley, and Miss C Jones; dance by Mr C Thorn ; duet, Miss C Dyer and Mr Hop- wood ; and a selection on the fairy bells from Mr R Careen. The accompanist W3B Miss Garvie. The pro- ceedings closed 'rite the usual votes of thanks followed by the National Anthem. FREE LECTURER AT THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE, RUNCORW. On Monday next, the first of a series of free lectures, in connection with the Free Library, Runcorn, will be given at the lecture-room of the Runcorn Technical In- stitute, by Mr. Robert Newatead (Chaster), on " British Birds," when the chair will be taken at 8 o'clock by Mr. F. Wiswall, J.P. (chairmen of the Runcorn Uruan Dis- trict Council). The second lecture will be given on Monday. Nevember 20th, by the Rev. C. W. L.Christien, subject, " My friends on the Shelves." The other lec- turers for the seoisen are as fellow 3 i—A. Lancaster, Esq., librarian of the Public Library, St. Helens. W. Stainer, Esq., M.A., headmaster of the Higher Grade School, Widnes. Ray. J. Osborne, 8.A.. and T. A. LAW.. renson, Esq. M.A.</p>
        <p>SONS OF TRMPERANCR, RUNCORN. The usual fortnightly meeting of the above order was held on Saturday night, in the Greenway-road School. Mr. Thomas Cooke occupied the chair. A varterly report was read, prepared by Mr Thomas J. Holt, grand scribe, dealing with the numerical and financial state of the order in the district, and was consideed satisfactory by the members. There had been a fair increase of both adult and juvenile members during the quarter. The funds were in a good condition, the central fund now being £4Bl 153 11d. Before the close of \ the meeting the Chairman referred in sympathetic terms to the recent serious accident te several of the Weston m-na- hers, viz: Messrs. William and John Dunbabin. The former occupies a prominent position in the order. It was resolved to send a letter of sympathy to the sufferers. The following officers spoke to the resolution : Messrs. S. Tomlinson, D. Bisbrown, James Moores, and Thomas Whyte. The meeting then closed. RUNCORN V. FARNWORTH HARRIERS In inter-club run, the first of the season, took place last Saturday, Runcorn Harriers meeting Farnworth Harrier, at Farnworth,and engaging in a cross-country run. There was a good muster of runners and great interest was taken in the run. The paper was entrusted to Messrs Willis and Shallcross, who laid a capital trail through Ditton to Peck's Hill, traversing the outskirts of Farnworth, a distance of ab3ut eight miles of heavy country. The pace was taken up by J. Hosker the northern crack, who made it a hot one throughout. The result of the race ended in favour of the home team by the narrow margin of nine points. The folowing is the order of finishing :—Farnworth team : 1, J Hoskin ;4, Broome ;5, Haynes ;6, Flynn ; 7, Radcliffe. Runcorn team : 2, J Cadman ;3, W Ray ;3, Hopkins ' • 9,Welch; 10, Hill. Total: Farnworth, 23; Runcorn, 32 points. BRUNSWICK SCHOOLS,—ORGAW ReetTAL. The Rev. C. W. L.Ctuistien (superintendent minister) presided over a large attendance of teachers and friends of the above school gathered together within its precincts last Monday evening,the eecasion being a reci- tal by Mr. Musgrove upon the American organ recently purchased for the schools. The qualities of the instru- ment were ably shown by Mr. Muegrove in his rendition of" Allegro " (Bache), " Roinauce in A" (Mozart) and other solos. In addition the following items were ren- dered : —" Mee of Harlech." quartette Darts' ; "Daddy" Mies A Taylor ; " Sign of the Cross," Mc Walter Shaw ; " Alone on the raft." Miss Stearin ; " 03 the banks ot Allan Water," quartette party (Misses Jones and Bir- kett, and Messrs Morris and Birkett) ; " Blow, blow. thou wintry wind," Mr Porah ; " On lovely peace," Mires Taylor and Birkett "The Holy City," Mr liaire- ton ; " Now by day's declining lamp," quartette party. Addresses were delivered by the Rev. C. W. L. Christien and the Rev. J. A. B. Malvern, both on the appropriate subject " Music."</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH CHOIR SOIRRE: This annual gathering took place on Tuesiay evening in the Brunswick Guild Parlour. About forty members of the choir, and their friends, partook of an excellent coffee supper prepared by Mr. Cox the chapel-keeper, after which Mr. J. Birkett presented suitable tokens of esteem to Mrs. Booth and Mrs Bowyer, two lady mem- hers of the choir who had enteral the bonds of wrdlock since their last annual gathering. In snaking the presentation Mr. Birkett referred to the past services of both ladies and wished them every 'joy in their new life.—Mr. T. Burrows spoke of the value the ladies had proved in times past.—Mr. H. Hughes followed with an inimitable speech embracing the delights of married life. At its conclusion, the ladies returned their sincere thanks to their friends,and assured them their services were always at the disposal of so good a cause. The party then indulged in various games, interspersed with musical items, awl altogether a mast convivial evening WAS spent.—Daring the evening the members of the choir were sounded as to whether they would run a cancert for the assistance of Mr. Robert Harrop,a memberof the St. Paul's congregstion,rlcently goneblind,and all expressed their willingness Otake part in so philanthropic an undertaking. A Cam) BURNT TO MUTH AT RUNCORN. Mr. R. D Amon, coroner, held an inquest at the Run- corn Police Court. last Wednesday, on the boly of Archibald Rose, infant son of Mr. and Mrs. James Rose, Gilbert-street, Runcorn, who died the previous day from the result of burns.—The mother gave eviience of identification and sail she left the house at 8 o'clock on Tuesday Morning to go out washing at Mrs. Littler's leaving her son Jim, aged 13, to lo)k after the house. When she returned at dinner time she found her boy Archie burnt. The child was but three years old.— George Barker, bricklayer, 2, Hope-street, said he was p assing thrones Gilbert-street on the Tuesday morning when a little girl ran up to him and said "Archie had been playing with fire and got burnt" He want into the house and found the little child with his clothes on fire. He got a coat, enveloped the child in it, and put out the fire. Then he held the boy whilst an- other man cut off his clothing with scissors. Afterwards he assisted Police-Constable T. S. Bowyer to dress the burns with oil, of which they had a bucket-ful. Every- thing that was possible was done. Subsequbntly Dr. McLennan came, but not say anything to him, but he saw the Dr. shake his head.— ['he Coroner did not think it nezes:ary to call the boy James to give evidence.— The Foreman: No we don't think it necessary to call the boy.—The Coroner summing up observed it was per- fectly clear" that the child diei from shock following upon burns accidentally sustainel " and the jury re- turned a verdict accordingly.—At the request of the jury the Coroner then highly complimented the witness Barker for his prompt attention to the boy.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED FOOTBALL FRAUD -A. SMART CAPTURE BY THE RUNCORN POLICE.</p>
        <p>David itrthur Peck, alias A. and T. Riley, described as a clerk, and a native of Arnley, Leeds, 35 years old, has been for sometime busily engaged in working an alleged fraud in several of our neignbouring towns. His modus operandi has been to induce publicans to pay for advertisement in allotted spaces upon a large card which he represented as to be printed setting forth the various ties in the Northern Union Cup. Having secured pay- ment, of course, in advance, Mr. Peck would decamp and his customers were left to wait the publication which never came. In the course of his travels, he reached Stockpert, and several publicans of that place being badly bitten the police were notified, which re- sulted in a description of Peck beieg issued beneath the all important word " wanted." Eventually Peck reached Runcorn, bet unluckily for him his description had arrived before him, and though he succeeded in obtain- ing" ads," from Mr. Samuel Houghton, Mr. Peter Riley and one or two other publicans it was not so very long before Detective Bowyer, of the Runcorn Police, effected his capture. As far as Runcorn is concerned, the little episode concluded before Mr. John Ockleston, at the Police Court last Saturday morning as, hen Peck was remanded to await an escort of the Stockport Police. In answer to the magistrate he admitted he was the Peck described by the Stockport Police. but said " what he had been doing in Runcorn was perfectly legitimate, and ho had dune it before."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By s CORRESPONDENT.' 'She prosecution of parents under the Elementary Education Act for the neglect of their children's education, has for some time—so far as Runcarn is concerned—been somewhat of a farce. One natur- ally assumes that the object of the pros.-!cution is to get the child to school so that it might partake of the benefits, legislation has ensured for it. Though undoubtedly the object is a good one, seldom does it achieve its end, for one sees the same faces and hears the same names continually before the magistrates. A ludicrous part of the procedure is to observe the unceremonious way the people step up with their five shillings (made up of the maximum : 6d and 43 6d costs)—sometimes before the case is called, but nearly always before the Bench announce their decision the money is paid over and the defendant half way out of the court. Thus it is plainly to be seen that the people who most offend in this matter have come to regard in a measure the payment of the fine more as an indem- nity—or license fee—on payment of which they are at liberty to keep their children from school for a further period.</p>
        <p>In the majority of the school eases we see too the wife, a neighbour, or, often enough, a little child. represent the" Case "—armed, of course,with the inevitable 55.—whilst the father, the all respon- sible parent is generally left out of the reckoning, and in many cases never hears of it, until eu- lightened by his neighbours or the Press. I would suggest, therefore, the Bench insist upon the at- tendance of the fathers wherever possible, and make them cognizant of their remissness as guardians of the well-being of their own ofrapring, feeling sure the loss of time to them in addition to the fine of 58 will go further in securing a better attendance of the scholar at school.e.nd a diminution in the number of appearances of the same parties before both the School Attendance Committee and the Bench.</p>
        <p>The discussion upon the advisability!, of engaging an expert to guide the Runcorn Urban District Council upon the electric lighting question, which took place on Thursday morning is somewhat instructive, inasmuch as it thows who are and who are not opposed to a discussion of the question on its merits. The cost, I understand, for the " expert evidence " will be twenty-five guineas, and if the expert engaged is one worthy of the name, this sum should prove to be money well spent. Mr. Imison, it is easily seen, takes up a position indicative of his future policy, and does not disguise it. Mr. liandly, on the other hand, lends himself open to criticism, for he says "it is only ' experts ' that will offer to purchase," and advises the Council to sell out and get its money back," before incurring any further casts. He evidently for the moment forgets. If the Council does not know the value of the article they are selling, they are liable to part with it for less than its full value ; and further, if the Council are justified in selling vow on those terms, they have got no further than where they commenced, and might have let the limited companies fight for it in the first instance,</p>
        <p>The park and commons to cost £4,000 has been a fearful " bogie " to many of our townspeople, and comments have been rife as to what should be done in the matter. Now is the opportunity for all to speak. The inquiry takes place on Thursday next at the Town Hall, Runcorn, and all who consider it should be opposod ought to at once come to the front. It is rumoured that a large number of dis- affected people are about to oppose the scheme strenously. All I hope is that the matter will be approached calmly and in a dispassionate spirit, so that neither side may do anything rash.</p>
        <p>Some people seem to think an effort should be made by the local authority to purchase the Gas Works; others seem to think that nothing of the kind should be contemplated, and ruefully shake their heads at the mere mention of the project and say "Waterworks I" Still it does not f011.,w be- cause the town made one bad bargain they should make anotker. The buying of a gas works can never be a bad bargain whilst gas is used; For it is a commodity that affects every individual rate- payer. The profit on the gas undertaking, I feel sure, would pay for its own loane,and in the matter of rebates and cheaper and better gas—for there is plenty of room for improvement—l feel therelwould be a decidedly altered condition of things. There would also be a less rate to pay, as well as a more cheerful disposition to pay it.</p>
        <p>......................••••• St. Peter's Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>The three-days' sale realized the'sum of £l4O, and with £BO subscriptions a grand total of £225 has been received.</p>
        <p>Undenominational Ragged Sunday School.</p>
        <p>A sacred service of song, entitled "The Roll Call was rendered in the Ragged School,Upper Bank-stre it by the choir of the Bostock-street Mission, on Sun - day evening. There was a goof attendance, and collections were taken in aid of the school fund. Organ Recital.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening. Mr. Frank Hickman gave an organ recital in Gelborne-street Baptist Church, there being a good attendance. The following pro- gramme was gone through :—Paraphrase from "Hymn of Praise," chorus, "Praise thou the Lord.' " Adagio," " fie coanteth all your sorrows," "Tie Night's departing" (Mendelssohn) ; serenata (Braga ; toccato (Dubois) ; melody (S Smith) : re- miniscence of an evening service interrupted by a storm (G E Lyle.) Mr. Hickman rendered his part of the programme in excellent style. The choir very efficiently sang the anthem "The Radiant Morn," and a solo entitled "Lead kindly light" was well sung by Miss Christian. A collection was taken in aid of the choir hinds. Sad Death of a Child at Lower Walton.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Dobson, coroner, held an inquest on Tuesday, touching the death of Florence Beatrice Parr, aged 16 months, daughter of John Parr, a waterman,residing at 3, Murley-road,Lower Walton who was drowned in awash tub on Monday.—Lydia Parr said she was in the parlour on Monday after- noon at 2 o'clock when her son William Henry aged three years, who could neither hear nor speak came in and pulled her by the skirt. He pointed to the yard:and on going there he found deceas4d's head downwards itra wash tub which was almost full of water. She pulled her out and gave an alarm.— Margaret Simmoads, a nurse, residing in Taylor- street at the back of Morley-road said aka heard screams and ran to Parr's yard. She found Mrs. Parr with deceased in her arms. She applied artifi- cial respiration but it was of no avail. The child never breathedfrom the time she firsc saw it.—A verdict of "accidental death "was returned.</p>
