      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the above Council was held at the Council Chamb,r, Town Hall, Ruhoorn, on Thursday morning. there being present :—Mere. Samuel Taylor (vice chairman) presiding, James Prit- chard. J H Kirkham, E Gatloy, S Walton, Thomas Jones, H J Grice, E Imison, W Handley, J Wilson, D Bisbrown, Sweetman, R H Posnett, J W Lightburn, with F A Lake (law clerk), E Marshall (ecretary), James Wilding (surveyor), and Dr F McDougall (medical officer). MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer's report showed there was no extraordinary devolopm..nt in diseases during the past month. Twenty-four deaths were registered, one being the subject of an inquest, being a death rate of 16 4. Tee number of births registered was 46.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ELECTRIC LIGHTING QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTRIC LIGHTING QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Highways and Buildings Committee recommended that a horse be purchased for tbe sum of £6O for the Surveyor's Department ; that the Committee be em- powered to engage an electrical engineer te report as to the supply of electricity for power and lighting purposes, and that the plan of the Liberal Club, Regent-street, for the insertion of a floor en iron stanchions and other alterations, be approved. Tne Chairman (Mr. S. Taylor) moved the adoption of the above mieutes and observed that the committee were asking for power to engage an electrical engineer to report upon the question with the object of the com- mittee having fuller information at their disposal as to the advisability of electric lighting. They had at pre- sent to remain in a position which rendered them unable to report at all. Mr. M. Sweetman seconded', Mr. E unison inquired whether from the remarks of the Chairman they were to conclude that the committee might be influenced to think it advisable to proceed with something contemplated by them which would re- quire an estimate. The Chairman considered at present the committee could not decide upon the lines they intended to proceed upon and they had every information to get before they could proceed. Therefore the committee thought it advisable to ask the Council for the power to engage an expert, which they considered requisite. Mr. unison thought he could understand the matter if it were one of urgency, but considering it was a matter which would take no harm by stand- ing over fcr some time he did not see the need for the committee to ask for suzh a power. He argued that the Council had gone to the trouble of obtaining the Provisional Order for the purpose of pre- venting other people from" filching" powers. When it was obtained it was almost resolved that the first oppor- tunity should be taken of disposing of it. That was it was a sa'eable article, and that they (the Council) could control the supply of electricity at possibly no cost to the ratepayers. Before the order was obtained they had what he would call many glittering offers from various electrical companies, but since it was secured those offers had not been repeate 1. A scheme might incur the expenditure of about £ll,OOO, and before anything was done in so important matter he considered they should take into consideration the experiences of other towns, and ferret out the reasons why this or that place had to abandon the electric lighting after its adoption, and to see what special conditions their own district offered. He wouid not agree to anything that might be carried out for the few at the expense of the many. There was no one present around the table who could say that he had'a:mandate from the ratepayers of Runcorn to supply them with electric light. He ques- tioned whether they were as all lively to get one hun- dred private people to take the electric light and were they going to provide those people with a very brilliant light at the expense of the remaining thousands of rate- payers ? Do not let themigo as in other inatters—teke certain steps and then get the ratepayers to absolve them—but let them have the voice of the ratepayers upon the matter firat. He did not know exactly whether to move that the minute relating to the electrical engineer be struck out, or whether to move that the minute be substituted by appointing a small committee to make such inquiries as would be best suggested to them. Mr. 'Handley suggested that the minu`e relating to the engagement of the engineer should be dealt with separ- ately and the others passed. The Law Clerk pointed out that would not materially- alter the case, and explained that it was simply to con- sult an expert that the committee desired power. Mr. Imison observed " the power to do oft make ill deeds dene." (Laughter.) Subsequently he proposed an amendment that the minute relating to the engineer be omitted. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Wilson said he understood that the Committee and Council ought to have certain information regard- ing the matter of electric lighting, and he took it there was no expert in such matters upon the Council. The committee had decided to call in the services of an ex- pert so aa to obtain information which oth^r wise they could not obtain. Certain people were desirous of pur- chasing the provisional order and the Committee were 1 desirous of going the best way about their work and calling in the services of an expert to enable them to elm,' to a wise and proper conclusion. They wanted to knew which would be the wiser step to get a supply from someone else,or whether it would be best and most rcoeomical for them to set about an installation Of their own, which would be put down for the benefit of the ratepayers at the cost of the ratepayers. The Chairman *deed there had been a good meeting of the committee at which the minute was passed and they were very careful in avoiding any unnecessary expense. Mr. W. Handley said he would second Mr. Itnison pro forma. He took objection to the matter at the Commit- tee. Already they had so,nt out of current revenue something like 13 in the £ and were expecting to get it back again by the disposal of the order. The sum they expended upon an expert would be added to the sum they had already expended. If, however, they disposed of the order thee all the expense of an expert would be saved. He contended it would only be an expert that would offer to purchase the order, and that being so it would be better for them to get their money back and let others rake the responsibility. Mr. Grice advocated sounding the ratepayers upon the question and getting them to absolve them all from the matter by pronouncing either a desire for or against the electric lighting. Mr. D. Bisbrown said as no one wished to take a leap in the dark they wanted information as a committee and were desirous of calling in an expert ti supply that information. It would not entail a very large expendi- ture. The Chairman said they were not pledging themselves to provide electric lighting for the town. It was simply a question upon which they must have expert inform i- tion. Mr. Imis on asked whether he was t understand that the expert was to advise them upon the several offers to supply the town. The Law Clerk explained that if they could get offers for the supply of electricity in bulk, the information would tend to show them whether it would be wiser to sell or to carry on the work themselves. On a vote being taken Messrs. Imison, Handley. Pritchard and Grice voted for the amendment, and the rest for the original proposition which was carried. Mr. Imison then moved a further amendment " that as the contemplated expenditure had not been provided for in that year's estimate, the expenses of the engage- me• t were not to be met before the end of the financial year." Mr. Grice secended. The amendment was however lost, only Mr. Imison and Mr. Grice voting for it. The minutes were then confirmed. THE WATER COMMITTEE AND THE HALTON WATER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>The following letter has been received from the Run- corn Rural District Couucil ;—Runcorn, 27th September, 1899. Dear sir, By the direction of this Council I enclose a copy of analysis of water taken from the water mains of the Urban District Council of Runcorn, in the Parish of Halton,and I am instructed to request that the Urban District Council will kindly have the reservoir at Halton cleaned out with the least possibla delay. The Rural District Council propose taking a further sample of water for analysis when this has been done.—Yours truly, Geo. F. AsirroN.—E. Marshall, Esq. The analyst makes the following remarks :—"I can- not recommend this water for general use, as it contains too much mineral matter in the shape of limemagnesia, and chlorides which give it the flavour of mineral water."</p>
        <p>The Water Committee recommended that the follow- ing particulars be embodied in a reply :—"The Halton reservoir was cleaned out four years ago, and had not previously bean cleansed for fourteen years. The Water Engineer reports "that on the 11th September last, a very poor supply was reported in the neighbourhood of the Wesleyan chapel at Halton. When the four inch main was examined an obstruction was found in the pipe and a new valve and length of pipe was put in." The analysis of water enclosed in the aforementioned letter was dated September 25:h. The sample analysed would probably be taken shortly after this obstruction was discovered and the new pipe put in. The water at the time the sample wee taken would still be disturbed and this would account for the diseolouretion. The Surveyor submitted to the Committee 12 samples taken from October 3rd to October 23rd and these show the water to be clear. To forward copy of tit,. last Bacteriological Analysis of water received from the School of Pathology, Liverpool University College, and dated August last, which showed that the water did not contain any disease producing germs. These minutes were confirmed on the motion of Mr. J. Pritchard seconded by Mr. J. Wilson. PARLIAMENTARY AND GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE. This Committee repored a letter had been re- ceived from the English Industrials Limited, of Man- chester, asking, with a view to the introluctien of a £5 Telephone rate into the district, whether the Council would give the Company applying for a license, its sup- port. It is racom nended that no consent be given for the next session. The Committee further reported it hal considered the Provisions of the Small Dwell- ings Acquisition Act, 1899, and recommended that the Act be not adopted. Thee alzo recommended that the bills of costs of the Law Clerk and of his Parliameatary</p>
        <p>SOLDIERS' FAMILIES RELIEF PROPOSALS</p>
        <p>BOMOCEA FOR THE BATTLE-FIELD.</p>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>FOR WOUNDS, Cuts, Bruises, Broken Skin, Sore Places, generally, use Homocea often, and plenty of it.</p>
        <p>INFANTRY need Homocea for enointing the feet on the march, and fer sores caused by chafing accoutrements.</p>
        <p>CAVALRY need Honaocea for troubles caused by rough riding, damp saddles, etc., and especially for piles.</p>
        <p>SEE THAT NO FRIEND goes to the front without Homocea. What eloe can they rely on to touch the spot 'and soothe the aching part "</p>
        <p>Agents in relation to the application for and obtaining of a Provisional Order for the Electric Lighting of Run- corn be taxed and that the Council expresses no desire to be represented at the taxation. Mr. W. Handley proposed the adoption of the above with the exception of the first paragraph and the resolu- tion referring thereto ; as, since the meeting of the Com- mittee was held, another aspect had beea thrown upon the matter and be was desirous that the Committee should have a further opportunity of considering it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hewitt seconded. Mr. Gatles referring to the ncommendation respect- ing the Small Dwellings Acquisition Act, objected to the words that the committee recommends that the Act be not adopted " aid said as far as he could remember, it was decided by the committee that the matter lie on the table. lc was ultimately decided that the matters taken ex- ception to be left open, and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>TECINICAL INSTRUCTION COMMITTEE. Mr. Thomas Riley was appointed a member of the Teehnical Instruction Committee. FURTIER LOANS: £365 WANTED. _</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee recommended that application be made to the Public Works Loin Commissioners to lend the sum of £265 at 21 per cent for 25 years for ex- tension of water mains k. 3., and the sum of £lOO at 2i per coot for 30 years for the strong room. That the tender of Mr. James Mort for the constructing and fix- ing of cuFsboards for the Strong Room and the Fire Brigade Station be accepted, and that the Surveyor be authorized to erect an iron arch for lamp at the entrance to the Town Hell, at a cost not exceeding £5. The financial report WAS adopted on the motion of Mr. Jones, seconded by Mr. Imison.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said on behalf of Mr. Wtswall he would ask the Secretary to read a communicaton from Lady Brooke, the local secretary of the Soldiers and Sailors Families Relief Association. The communication asked whether the Council considered it desirable that a town's meeting be held for the purpose of raising funds for the relief of the. soldiers' familes which had been left be- hind. The general opinion expressed was that it was very desirable to hold suzh a meeting. Mr. Taylor said it was a most worthy objfct, and he felt sure Runcorn would not be behind in doing all in its power for the families ot their soldiers. It was an object to which the working people of the whole country would contribute to the utmost of their means. As they all knew there was not a more favoure i people on the face of the earth than the people of the United King dom. They could sleep in their beds without fear, be-- cause they kad their soldiers and sailors to look forward to, to protect their interests and lives ; therefore he felt sure the object was oae which would appeal to every- one. Mr. Posnett suggested that steps should he taken to ascertain whether the fund belonged to the Patriotic Fund or not. He was in favour of such a meeting, and hoped that the interest of the Volunteers could be o`,- tained so as to make a good meetinf, ; but he thought they should try and ascertain who were the local people to relieve and provide for them first. It was decided to reply to L rdy Brorke to the effect that such a meeting was considered advisable, and offering her any co—operation in the matter she desired. TEE PARE kWD PLEASURIt GIROUND.-LOCAL GOVERNMENT</p>
        <p>TROOPS FOR THE TRANSVAAL should WITHOUT FAIT, be provided with Homocea, as there is nothing more soothing or healing known.</p>
        <p>sequomce. Half backs are expected to play together, but that is no reason:wby either should sink his individts- ality and merely follow instructions. Harris and Fish were both very dangerous and gave a good account of themselves. In the centre Isherwood aril Jones did lots of good work but neither was finished in his play. The passing of the two is their worst fault. At full back Ashurst was safe and ener- getic. He looks likely for being the right man in the right place. Joliey and Eden were the pick of the for- wards, and their speedy following up revealed much of the secrct of the " A " teim's succlss. On the Tyldesley side Aldred was far away the test man. Be is a very speedy re3olute thireequArter, and should develop into a fine:player with luck and opporzunity.</p>
        <p>Warrington climbed aghia to fourth place by virtue of Saturday%) win and Salford's defeat at Oldham. They ought to fall no lower if they only play with the same dash when away as they do at home. I am not exaggerating when I say that they ought to get four points out of half the clubs in the League. The table up to date is as follows : P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. For. Against. Runcorn Oldham Swinton Warrington 9.. 5. 3. 1 9 20— 78 .. 7 7... 35...11 Salford Widnes 9.. 5. 4. 0.. 5 16— 58... 4 11... 41.,.10 st. Helens Leigh Prouglit'n 9... 4... 5... 0... 5 13— 49...10 13... 59... 8 Rochdale Hornets 6... 4... 2... 0... 9 5 3g... 4 12... 44... 8 Stockport 7.. 1. 8. 0.. 4 7 29... 9 p 1... 81... 2 Wigan Tyldesley 10.. 0...10... 6... 5 3 19 ..22 4?....179... 0</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BOARD lICGIIIIRY.</head>
        <p>BOARD lICGIIIIRY.</p>
        <p>Tbe Law Clerk intimated that the Local Government', Board bad fixed the inquiry into the loan of £4,000 for the park and common, and £6O for the hor,e ambulance for the 9tb of November at a quarter to eleven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>DISAPPEARANCE OF A RUNCORN WOMAN</head>
        <p>DISAPPEARANCE OF A RUNCORN WOMAN</p>
        <p>A Mysterious Affair.</p>
        <p>The sudden disappearance of Mrs. C!ara Harrop,of 32, Bridgewater-street, Runcorn,wife of Mr. Robert Harrop, who for some years has been a sufferer from defective eyesight, has given rise to vague rumour,and it is feared in some quarters that she has thrown herself into the canal in the vicinity of the Top Locks. On inquiry we learn that Mrs. Earrop, who is but 34 years of age,went to bed on Tuesday night about 11 o'clock, but at about 6 o'clock tsrs the following mornitim her husband missed her and she has not been beard oCsince. This afternoon Mr. Byfield and Mr. Tanks wits the as3istance of the police dragged a portion of tie oanal as the inquiries around the neighbourhood had elicited nothing, but up to the time of writing with no success. It is said that Mrs. Harnp has for sometir"e been slightly deranged, and fears are entertained thst she has committed suicide whilst in an unsound mental state.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [By "Oval ist."]</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. [By "Oval ist."]</p>
        <p>Warrington's point average got a handsome lift on Saturday at the expense of Tyldesley, who, as the wooden-spoonists ot the division could nr,t be expected to make a very strong resistance. The home team won by a bigger margin than I expected they would, and twenty-two points to nothing is a performance in the scoring line which ought to satisfy anyone. It cannot be said that the score was put together in the best style, bat as it was put together and in spite of the hest efforts of Tyldesley we ought to be content.</p>
        <p>There was something pathetic in the eight of old " Billy " Berry leading the Tyldesley men on the field once more. Be has been thrown off and put on the team a few times in the last season or two and his ap- pearance or disappearance may be considered a sort of barometer of Tyldesley's strength. When they are doing fairly well Berry is consigned to obscurity; when fate is against them, then the committee are glad to have him behind the scrummage once more. Probably his services are cheap. At any rate they ought to be.</p>
        <p>Berry and Worthington, the burly forward, were the only tv o familiar figures left of the old brigade. The others were newer and younger, some of them smarter but all lacking the experienceof the two stalwarts of old. Tvidesley are in a sad way and appear to be deeper in the mire than Wigan, their present companions in mis- fortune. They have the nucleus of a team in several good forwards, and a couple of smart backs, but the others want a lot of licking into shave before they are fit for first-class company. The Warrington lads taught them something, and one hopes the lesson may be of service to them.</p>
        <p>There is something' surprising, too, in the fair measure of success Warrington achieved considering the youthful- ness of the players doing duty. There are only about two men of lengthened experience among the lot, yet the younger men generally hold their own wherever they go. We are fortunate in having several players young in years but possussing more than the average skill and cleverness on the football field. Other teams, Syldesley for instance, try desperately hard to find promising junior talent, but it is not forthcoming. In Runcorn we have an example of the opposite character. Success continually attends their efforts at developing the abilities of the junior players of the district. There is no team in the League with a lighter or more youthful set of backs than Warrington and yet there are many clubs who cannot cell themselves as well represented.</p>
        <p>Saturday's game—l had better return to it—was fairly enjoyable and moderately exciting. There was nothing startling about it, for Warrington's absolute superiority in every department of the game was early manifest, The backs were able to do as they pleased, almost, so that scoring was fairly prolific. There was plenty of room for improvement in the combination show how- ever. The backs have a bad habit of lying too close to- gether when passing, and at the same time all run towards touch. If they opened out more tbe to men taking the ball couldhave far better chance of baffling the intending tackler in front. At the very least they should always leave room for one oppon- ent to pass between them. As it often happens the backs are running so close that when the ball is trans- ferred the man In front has no difficulty in stopping either of the two, players and sometimes both if he iE smart. There is a lot of practice required before the Warrington backs can handle the bill with the abilty necesary to make the best openings for the two storing wings, Harris and Fish. But I will give them credit for a sonsiderable improvement on last year's feeble eff)rts at combined action,</p>
        <p>Duckworth was the shining light on Saturday and ful- filled the expectation that a lot of pcople bad, viz., that he would show up hr more consptuously if he went on his own a little more. Bth half hicks have brains and they should use them as they think best in an emerg- enuy, both independent of each other and in concert as the case may be. Dackworth scored two b?autiful trts on Saturday, and at the same timc made numerous open- legs for the men bthind. Had his captain been playing he would probably not have felt at liberty to act as he did, ad the score would have been so much less in con-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Harriers.</head>
        <p>Harriers.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Htrriers fixture on Saturday was an inter - club run with Present Harriers when 30 runners turned out from headquarters,the Sloop Hotel, Sankey Bridges. The paper was entrusted to Messrs. Prwer and Whit- taker, who laid the trail over some splendid country in the direction of Bold and Buttonwood of about six miles, but it was decided not to race throuth and to have a mile break. The runners lined up at Sankey Chapel House, which is about a mile from home, when the signal was given to "go." After a splendid race Jehn Riley succeeded in getting home first, T Devlin second, Josepie Riley third, and G Eastwood fourth, all of Sankey, whilst the Prescot inn came in the follow- ing order :—W VrellioE!si 13th, 14 Rigby 15th, E James 17th, ard E Matkcr 18:h. Tho ankey won by 53 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>OUF, FOOTBALL CC IPETITION.</head>
        <p>OUF, FOOTBALL CC IPETITION.</p>
        <p>(COUPON ON rmi E 7.) £5O Offered Weekly—iCloo Aggregate Prize.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION. £33 WON</p>
        <p>Two of the matches in this oompetition —Blackburn Rovers v Glossop North End and Bedminstrr v Gravesend-- were put off in conscquence of English Cup Ties. The re- sults of the other matches were as follows : Derby County drew with Notts. Forest, Sheffield United beat Aston Villa, Wolverhampton Wanderers best Newcsstle United, West Bromwich Albion beat Sunderland, Bury beat Preston North End, Stoke drew with Bverton, Old}ism beat Salford, Gl-ucester beat Bristol. The following competitors gave eight correct results, and therefore divide the £3O prize : Mr If Proctor, 5. Spring Vale, liasiingdon, Lance ;Mr R Stebbing, Fenny Stratford, Bucks (2 correct coupons) ; Mr H Pittam, 72. Fore-street, London, B.C. ; Mr E Johnson, 19 Castle Hill Avenue, Folkestone.</p>
        <p>The fo!lowing competitors gave seven correct results, and therefore diside the Consoratioa Prize r —Mr C Mitchel, Devonport ; Mr d N Stephens. Barnstaple ; Mr J G Tavistock ; Mr It D Nelson, Davenport , Mr A Kirby, Leeds Mr F C Barker. Gateshead ; Mr C Barnes, Lincoln ; Mr. J Green, Cardiff ;Mr G Price, Walsall ;Mr 13 Taberner, Coal- ville ; Mr A }gelding, Batley; Mr C Karmen, London; Mr W Sottey, Berkhamnst-ad ; Mr A Goodman, Leytonstone; Miss Ada Lewis, Wellingboro ; Mr J Henderson. Liverpool ; Mr F Kellett, Kensington ' - Mr D Hoyle. Leicester ; Mr ft A Gar- ner, Doncaster ; MrJ Davies, Maidstone ; Mr W Lee, Mar- gate, Mr J Cullum, Spalding ; Mrs Simpson York; Mr J Par- ker,York ; Mr J Robinson,Hlstree ; Mr W Holmes. Newport, Hon. ;Mr 13 Worsley, Clitheroe ; Mr R Tate, Nottingham ; Mr P Taylor, Yentnor ;Mr J R Adams, Lowestoft; Mr H Kingston, Chester ; Mr S Webster, Croydon ;Mr Arthur, Tunbridge Wells , Mr P West. Bristol; Mr G Dunmore, Birmingham ; Miss It Arkwrigist, Bedford ;Mr J Draper, Northampton ; Mr G Nr-wtori, P,dinburgh ; Mr F Darby, Newport Pagnell ; Mr Cotton, Luton ; Mr HH E Pearce, Rotherham ; Mr J. Davison. Liverpool; Mr j Fowkes, Bed- ford; Mr W J Turner, Stroud ; Mr A Baglin, Thrupp, Nr. Stroud ;Mr J Carr, Spilsloy ; Mr G Lock. Crediton ; Mr J Waterwerth. Lytham ; Mr A W Tily, Montpelier, BrIst)1 ; Mr W G Davies, Harlesden ; Mr A J Howley. Gloucester ; Mr 0 Lee, Newcastle-on-Tyne ; Mr F Mayhem, Kennington ; Mr Funnell, Carshalton; Mr A G Cook, London ; Mr A Darnell Kettering; Mr J Phipps, Leeds; Mr W Tugwell, Nottingham; Mr J Ramsey, Folkestone ; Mr 13 13 Bruce, London; Mr 13 Maple, Bedford ; Mr A Astles, Manchester. _</p>
        <p>The £3 Coupon Prize has been won by Mr. B Maple, Oak- Lands, Busmead Avenue. Bedford.</p>
        <p>The winners ot the £2O prize have had two points each placed to their credit for the aggregate prize, and the winners of the £lO prize one point each. A list of the principal scorers for the agvregate prize will be given next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
