      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of seiesce is suzia as will benefit all classes ef people. S )me of the greatest discoveries of the age, while they nava proved directly beneficial to thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Even some of the beet medicinal preparatioes, however much they may do to remove one class of diseese. or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hold of another dis- ease, or leave it entirely untouelled, or if they benefit one Cain of patients, they may injure another. Now the great need of the age is some scientifically arranged pre— paration which will cope effectually with the prevalent diseases of this country,which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adapted to the needs of the merchant and the werkme.n,the professional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk,the factory hand,the miner, and the roadside labourer. It shauld be. tee, such a preparation as contains no injurious ingredients, and which may be. taken with impunity by the weakly child, or the delicate lady, as well as by the stranger constituted man. Such a discovery would desirve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal Preparations, and would indeed be an Invaluable boon to Suffering Humanity. Now this much needed boon has been ound, has been tried, and wherever proved, it has been ound satisfaatory. We refer to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic, which when once tried has been always recommended, and has proved success- ful when all other medicine hag failed to give relief, and we may say further, that it has proved permanently beneficial, when other preparations at best only give temporary relief. It is strongly recommeuded as The Best Remedy of the Age for Endigestion in its different forme, such as Sick Headache, Pains in the Side. Giddi- ness, Loss of Appetite, also for Nervousness and Nervcus Disorders, Sleeples3nese, Neuralgia, Low Spirits, and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved very beneficial to persons suffering from great Weaknes., either after an illness, long confinement to ill-ventilated rooms, or any ether cause. It strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengtheus those parts of the system which have been weakened by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilym Evans' Qainine Bitters being purely vegetable, it is a Natural Tonic.and suited to all ages at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere scknowledeed to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease. If you suspect that your health is beginnine to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your ccnAitution by taking now a course of this excellent Tonic, which is sold everywhere in bettlea at 23 9.1 and its 6i each, but should any difficulty be ex- perienced in precuring it, the Proprietors will forward same for the above prices, carriage free. Avoid Imita- tines. When purchasine,see the nanie,"Gwilym Evans," en Label, Stamp, and Bottle. Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitt,re Manufacturing Company, Limited, Lianelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A MARE AT WARRINGTON. At the Warrington Police Court, on Friday, before Alderman Roberts and other magistrates, Thomas What ley, Merserstreet, was charged with having on October 15th cruelly illtreated a mare.—P.C. Gibbs said that at 11 o'clock on the date in cmstion he was on duty in Wilderspool-road when he saw the defendant in a field playing with a mare. After patting it on its hinder quarters he behaved in a disgusting manner with a stick. The animal appeared to be in great pain. He got the defendant's name and address and told hirer that the ease would be rep)rted.;—P.C. Br,idley corroborated. —Mr. Ferguson, veterinary sureeen, spoke t- examining the mare. The injuries might have resulted very seriously..—De..fondant was fined 103 and costs (£1 144 63 ) Family Neglect..—An Enlisting 'Bus Driver. John Reeves, formerly a 'bus driver at Warriogton and last from Cork, was brought up at the Warrington Police Court on Monday charged with running away from the borough on October 13th, leaving his wife and two children chargeable to the Common Fund of the Warrington Unioo.—Mr. Bail Shearer prosecuted en be- half of the Guardians. He said that prisoner's wife and ! two children were admitted to the workhouse on October ! 13th, the man, who was a 'bus driver, haying deserted them. The Guardians ordered a warrant to be issned but it was found that prisoner had enlisted in the Army, The matter was discussed at a mectin and ! it was decided that he shoule be sent for, the children I being very young. He was apprehended at Cork where he was staying with his regiment. It was found thatl on enlisting he gave a false declaration, saying he was a! single man. The wife had now left the House and it was not known where she was living.—Prisoner said he ! had repeatediy told his wife that he would leave her if ! she did not give up her dirty habits. They bad been turned out of one house in Knutsford-road on account of her keeping the plaze so dirty.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner's wife did not appear to lee a dirty pr omq n —The magistrates decided to send the man to gaol far a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Hoberts (in the chair), Coun- cillor Smethurst, Dr. Gornall and Mr. Hepherd. Nstoneoutt's SQUABBLES --Mrs. Oxley. Oxford-street, was fined 5s and costs (14s) for assaulting Mary livens, Ox- ford-street on the 19th inst.—Complainant gave evid- nee to the eff,.,ct that she wee in a shop when defendant came in and made allegations against her. When she remonstrated with her she hit her in tne free with a jog ; Sarah Gleeve, 64, Scotland-road, was fined 5s and costs for assaulting Jane Pearae. 15. Bowec-street on the 24th inst.; Margaret Delooze, 49, Foundry-street, was charged with making use of threats towards Mary Travis on the 24th inst ; after hearing the evidence the Magistrates bound defendant over to keep the peace and ordered her to pey the costs (es). BRUTAL TRE.A.TNIENT OF • WIFE —Robert Henry Grice was summoned for assaulting his wife. Alice Griee, on the 23rd October--NA. A. Browne appeared for the defend:mt.—Con:- plait:eat said that at 9 p.m. on the date in question her husband came home and struck her acioss the face. He afterwards picked up a chair and attempted to strike her across the head with it, and put her and the baby out of the house.-9everal witnesses gave evidence bearing out this statement.—Mr. Browne said it was a meet pitiful case. the parties having been married for only twelve mouths. He had only been Instructed a few minutes ago and defendant bad had drink that morning. He thought. Mr. Taylor might do good work in that case.— Complainant then applied for a separation order,—The Magistrates fined defendant in the costs (ills 6d) for the assault, and granted a separation order defendant to pay Ts 6d a week towards the maintenance of his w fe and child. AFTER NINE we EEC. —William Horton, 12, George-street, was summoned for deserting his wife Hannah Herton. on tbe 15th inst.—Complainant said that on the date in question defendant used filthy language to her and afterwards packed up his things and went away. He had never come back since and had not sent any money. They had been married nine weeks —Defendant said that at night the house was always full of people drinking. He would not have lived twelve months if he hed stopped there. (Laughter.) Coun- cillor Smethurst : Why did you marry her ?—Defendant: I was persuaded to marry her. I did not want her. (Laughter.)---The case was adjourned for a month, the magistrates not being satisfied with the evidence! fglornmso ASSAULT ON A WOMAN.—Charies Naylor. 14. Cumberland-street, was summoned for assaulting Mary Beans, Orford-street, on the 21st October.--Complainant said that on the date named, defendant and his wife came to her house the back way. He asked if her " bass " was in and she replied that he was not. He then got hold of her by the wrists, and aregged her into the kitchen.and knocked her over the sofa, injuring her back. He struck her in the face and discoloured her eye, and then dragged her in the yard by the hair of the head.—Defendant, wh a denied the offence. was fined 5s and costs El in all) or 14 days. SATURDIT.—BefIre_ Messrs. Hayward (in the chair), R. T _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Fakelough ar.d T: Birch,3ll.</head>
        <p>Fakelough ar.d T: Birch,3ll.</p>
        <p>DRVNEENNESS.—EIizabeth Binson was on the evidence of P. C. Carter fined 5s and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Liverpeol-road at 953 the previous night ; ;Tames GMlion drunk ard disorderly in Orford- was charged with being street at 11.25 on Friday night.—P.C. Smith proved the ease.—Prisoner's wife informed the Bet.ch that IP-o• husband had been a soldier for twenty-four years and it was only during the last seven months he had taken so much to drink.—lt being the men's first offence he was discharged. 110 ND kir.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair). Ceuncillors Smethurst and Shaw Green. and Messrs. S. Wright, J. W. Hayward, and T. Bi:chall. TEREArs.—Martin Cooley. 11. Bakehouse-yard, was einrged on a warrant with making use of threats towards Thomas Conner, on the 27th Octeber.—Coonar said that prisomer told him he would take his life and would ' swing " for him.— Prisoner, who had previously been before the magistrates for assatPt and wilful damage, was ordered to find two sureties ef £25 to keep the peace, or go to gaol for three months. HE COULD FIGHT THE WHOLE POLICE FORCE "—Alfred Taylor, 31, Bostock-street was, on the evidence of P.C. Gibbs fined ss. for being drunk and disorderlv in Wilderspo.)l-road at 12 45 on Sunday morning —The officer state I that prisoner who was acting in a very disorderly manner said he could light all the—pllice force. MISCELLANEOUS —Thomas Morgan, I,l3aCk Stamford-street was fined ss. and costs for drunkenness.—Hata Morgan, Back Stamford-street, who did not appear, was fined las and costs for making use of obscene language ou the 21st October.— p.C. Bruen proved the case. -</p>
        <p>TCl3DAY—Before Mr. Birshall (in the and Councillor Shaw Green. - - -</p>
        <p>DRUNICENNESS.—TrAward Ryan was fiO3d ss. for being drunk in Bank-street, at 9.10 the previous morning ; P.O. Clough proved the ease ; On the evidence of P.O. Metcalf. John Flaherty we. cent to soot for seven days for being drunk and disorderly in ilorsemarket-4reet ot one o'clock on Mon- day afternoon. WEDNESDAY.—Before A idermaa Pairciough (in the chair) and gessr.. Birehall and Ge.ides. SUSPICIOUS CHARACTER SE N 1 To GAOL.—Thomas Smith was charged with being found on the enclosed premises of the Gas Works. Mersey-street, with intent to commit a felony at 2-15 that morning.—Charles Hein:T.3, night watchman at the Corporation Gas Works end residing at 84. Wellington- street, said that at the time named he maw the prisoner walking about the yard near the etThies and stores. When</p>
