        <p>jucileate on the School IN aard oases). ANTI-VaCciNs.TloN.—James Henry riokthall, 12. Waterloo- road. Runcorn, was granted an exemption from vaccination for his child Dorothy born on the 14th of August last.—He was afraid vaccination might harm his child. DRUNK AND Diroitunnvz.—Thilker Wilkinson. Weston, and William Woodrow. Merton-itreet,were lined 10s, includ- ing costs for be! g drunk and disorderly. DOG AT Lamm—Walter Spearing. 25. Chneoh-street pleaded guilty to having allowed his dog to be at large on the 15th October, and was lined ss. including costs. A STREET NUISANCE—John Collins (15), of Hughes' Court. pleaded guilty to setting off fire works in Cooper-street on the 19th day of Oetaber.—Conettable Wilson said he heard a number of reports of fireworks exploding in Cover-street and on getting there caught the defendant in the act of fir- ing one —Fined ss. including costs. bCIIOII. CASES.—George lidge, Shaw-street; Albert Ravens- weft, Poole-lane 3 Sarah A. Lea,Gilbert-street ; Joseph Lloyd. Leinster-street ; John -3. Sriaw, Windmill-street ; David Gee, Windmill-street; and Thomas Newton, were fined 6d. and 48. 6d. costs for nlgiecting the eduJation of their several ahitdren•</p>
