      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>VIRTUE OF CLEANLINESS.</head>
        <p>THE VIRTUE OF CLEANLINESS.</p>
        <p>If " cleanliness is nex; to g-rdliness " then we may gain a valuable objnt lesspn in its application to manu- facture by obsaving a model facory which from a small beginning has grown into a mammoth business where cleanliness is strictly observed. All the articles prepared are edible, and for Efids cleanliness is particu- larly insisted upon. It has bean a delight to malty to pay a visit to iii#ol2, near Cambridge, and inspect Chivers great fruit Farm and Factory. Toe fruit is chiefly grown by the firm itself in their own plantations, and the diatric; in the neighbourhood of the fact3ry. Plucks I early on a summer's morn, the fruit is made into jam, or the juice extracted to flavour Chivere Gold Medal Jellies. Here is the testimony of Dr. Gordon Stables. R.N., "Motsrs. Chivers have come to the front with Jellies "which really add tl our health and luxury. No home "is comp e'n wilraut these pure and excellent "Jellies." A Grocer remarks, "Your Jellies are "preferred to other makes. Customers will have • Chivers.' I thi •ik that speaks for their excel! .non." " A Q C. vi rites "Kindly send me a sample of your Jellies. " I am told that they are marvellously good " A Baptist " minister says-," My wife considers your Jellies perfec- "'titan, and I fully endorse her opinion." Sold by Grocers and Stores in packets, Half-pints Vid. Pints 4 , Quarts Si. A Free Sample will he sent on re- ceipt of post card. S. Chivers and Sons, Ristou, Cam- bridge. Please mention this paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Resignation of the Lymm Medical Officer of</p>
        <p>Health.</p>
        <p>At a special meeting of the Lymni Urban District Council on Taperlay evening, Mr. A. Dewhurst in the chair, Dr. Williamson, medical offi,!er of health, tendered his resignation, which was accepted. Dr. Lunt was appointed hie successor. Warrington Markets.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday wheat fetched 34 lid and 44 per bushel,oats,2s 4t-1. Flour, bet seconds, per 2401 b., 204. Bran, is Id per 201 b. At the Provision Market prices were as follows :—Fresh butter, is 34 and is 4d per lb ; cheese, 6d to 8d per lb. ; fresh eggs, 6 and 7 for is, fowls, 44 6d to 54 61 per couple ; English beef, 4d to 91 ; mutton 61 ta 101 ; veal, 7d to 101 ; pork 61 to Bd.</p>
        <p>The Electric Lighting Scheme.—Local Inquiry.</p>
        <p>The Town Council having applied for sanction to borrow .C 44,966 for purpoles of electric lighting. and £9,336 for the provi•iion of a Central Depat at Parr-swet, the Local Government Board have dir- ected an inquiry to be held into the subject, and Major-General Darley Crozier, R.E., ingpector has been appointed to attend for that purpose at the Town Hall, on Wednesday, November Bch, at 10-45 11.01. Be will then be prepared to receive the evid6nre of all perioni interested in the inquiry. Warrington Hairdressers' Association.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual soiree in connection with the Warrington HairdreAserts' As‘ociation was held in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening. Excel- lent arrangements had been wide by a committee consisting of Mes.rs. A Dumbell, Peter Lawless, A Lamb, T Hedgcock and E Smith (hon secretary) and everything passed off most successfully. Danc- ing commenced at eight o'clock to the strains of Mr. J T Barton's band, Mr W C Brookes officiating as M.C. About 50 couples were present. Refresh- ments were served by Mrs. Joseph Podmore and gave every pa.isfaction, Robbery From a Warrington Shop.</p>
        <p>Between one and two o'clock on Monday morning a robbery was perpetrated at the shop of Mr. Thomas Graves, pawnbroker, the Tower, Bridge- stre. t, Warrington. It appears that shortly after one o'clock a police officer passed the shop and everything was safe, but on returning half an hour later he found that the window had been broken. It was afterwards discovered that four watches were missing and half a brick was found at the bottom of the window. There were six watches on the shelf but as two were left it would appear that the theif had been interrupted. Six years ago several windows of pawnshops at Warrington were broken and articles stolen, so probably the depre- dations will not terminate here.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Concert at St. James' Schools Latchford.</head>
        <p>Concert at St. James' Schools Latchford.</p>
        <p>There was a large attervle.nce at St. Jame's Schools, Latchford, on Saturday evening, when a concert, the proceeds of which were in aid of the Church rmprovement Fund was held. An excellent programme of vocal and instrumental music was submitted, and each item was rendered very satis- factorily. The entertainment was opened with a pianoforte solo" Dance des Onemes " by Mrs. Me- egor, who was loudly applauded. A song entit- led " A dream of Paradise," which was given by Mr. J. H. Fisher, was well received. Mr. Dun- can McGregor rendered a violin sol) "La Wye," in a manner which won the hearty pima- dits of the audience, end Miss A. Reynolds was very successful in her feeling rendition of" Ruth's song." Mr. F. G. Ruddock snug" With the sun- shine end the swallows' and "To the front "in a very creditable manner and Miss Reynolds sang " La Serene.e," and " The mes,age" very testelnlly. An instrumental trio by Airs McGregor, Mr. W McGregor and Mr. H N Uou7liten, was rendered in capital style as was also "The Viking's love song" by Mr. JTJ Fisher. The humorous element was in the bands of Mr. J M Hey, who delighted the aud- ience with his song " Ki'tlebury Brown." His humoreus r?citation "Nothing to wear " caused much laughter. The concluding item was Rom- bery'l "Toy symphony" which was rendered by the following:—Violins : Mrs Powell,Mr J II Fisher, Mr D McGregor; cello: Mr H N Houghton ; Night- ingale : Mrs U N Houghton ; Cuckoo : Miss A Rey- nolds ; Quail : Miss May; bells and triangles: Miss Perry, Miss Coates, Mr Sherwood ; reties : Rev 17 Fi Powell, Mr C S Burgess; drum: Mr L R Rewsen cymbals : Mr C L I` SJate ; trumpets : Mr Stanley Weltoe.Nir Charles Reynolds; piano: Mrs McGregor; Mies Reynolds conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>IPT view of Ihe approaching Football Season, it may be in- J teresting to state that the world-famed rnanuf teturers. Go. G. BlTlStilr t 0., of London are preparing a stock of Balls, which for quality. uniformity, an .l durability, will be unique. These bells will be distinguished by the appearance thereon of their well known trade mark 4-6690. Ask the local SpJrte Dealer for their Catalogue. If Kos procurable, apply to 3 33, Queen Victoria-street. London. Factories —Fecitham, London; and Itlmswell, Suffolk.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches played on Saturday must be seat to the Head Mica, liwsey-street, Warrington, n3t later than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Cheshire v. Yorkshire.</p>
        <p>There was great interest in this fixture on the Birken• head g, ound on Saturday as it was hot only the first Northern Union county match over played in the dis- trict, but also the first county match on this ld-estab- lished ground. The ground was in grand coaditien.and hal been submitted to the smoothing influekces cf the steam roller. The Yorksuire men looked a good Vone man heavier than the home lot, whss lost the tese and started uphill, being immediately called hack for an in- fringement, and from the scrimmage Yorkshire rushed away for Sateliffe to score a try half a minute after the start, but no goal resulted. After the restart Yorkshire were penalised for lifting in the packs, and kicking exchaages ensued. In spite of all efforts the home:tere could not keep Yorkshire, out until a fine rush by the for wards, headed by Glover and Gayter. took play to midfield. Eire Taylor kicking well. Wilkinson had a grand run. N3VV Yerkshire had to de- fend,and were penalised in their own 25. Cheshire were giving their opponents plenty to do. Wersley Booth, Jolley, Wilkinson, and Hough-on being conspicuous, and Butterworth at times played magnitieenely. Play wae very brisk, and for some time Cheshire had a fair share of it. Another rush by Ham- brecht and Lewis gave a c'eaeee t Rigg, who scored neatly, and Lewis landed a fine goal. Damage to Rigg caused a stoppage of play, after which the injured player hed to retire. Half-time : 1 orkshire points, Cheshire, nil. Cheshire made a bold rub from the kick- off in the second half, a minor being the only re- sult. Jolley and Bevan nearly got over, and Yorkshire had a long struggle to dispose of the attack. Another visitor had to retire for a while. Worsley here got away in goo I style, and passed to Jolley. Cheshire still pressed, and Bevan had a futile drop at goal. Booth scored, but Houghton's kick failed narrowly. Butter- worth splendidly fielded to hall, and go: over, but the place kick egain mimed. Cheshire were now having all the best of it. and Wortley scored. Result :—Cheshire, 9 peinte ; Yorkshire, 8 points. The Cheshire team roceived a great evation on reaching the hotel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. The Wooden-Spoonists at Wilderspool.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION. The Wooden-Spoonists at Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Unbrtunate Tyliesley. the woolen-spoonists of the Lancashire Senior Clinpetition were the visitors to Wil- derspool on Saturday. The home team was at full strength with the exception that R. Bate appeared in place of his brother, " Smack" at half back. Tyldesley having nothing to lose by it, tried experiments by intro- ducing three new backs from the MancheAer district, and though p'aiuly lacking experience they shaped fairly well. The weather was fine, somewhat contrary to expectation, and a fair crowd assembled to watch the game. There would probably be about 4,000 spec- tators present when the teams lined up in the following order</p>
        <p>_ Warrington: Ashurst full back ; Fish, Jones, Isher- wood, and Harris, three-qaarters ; Duckworth and R Bata, half backs; Taylor, Morrison, Seift, Jolley, Beard- man, &amp;holm, Eden and Merry weather. forwards. Tyldealey : Makin, full hack; Mkie, Grindley, Madeley, and Aldred, three-quarters ; Berry and Morris- sey, halt-backs; Woodward, Worthington, Tinker, Hal- liwell, Riley, Ashton, Berry and Clewes, forwards. Warrington lost the toss and started operations against the wind, Jones performing the initial kick-off. Duckworth, Bate, and Isherwood rf-d the way into the Tyldesley half. Bate kicked across to Harris who ran up and centred, Jones securing and following up his own punt scored near the corner. He was unable to kick a goal. This early success stimulated Warrington, and Isherwood intercepting one of Berry's slow passes made off for the line at a greet speed. He passed all but the full back who succeeded in bringing him down. Play proceeded in the visitors' half. and Duckworth re- ceiving from the park drew the Tyldesley men on to Harris and very cleverly scored himse:f. Janes made a good attempt at goal against the wind. On resumntion the visitors attacked by good forwarki play, and Jones and Fish impeding Ashurst Warrington were nearly let down. Fish and Jones made one or two nice movements down touch but the wing man was generally upset by Makin. Eden and Merry weather led a forward rush and the bill went over tho Tyldesley line, where it became dead just as the forwards ran over. Tyldesley had another spell on the attack Madeley making twe goo i efforts to get through, Ashurst always barrel the way. Aldred had a free kick at goal but the bill was cbarged down by Scholtzi when apparently welt on tne way. After clearing their lines Warrington attacked again and Duckworth repeated his earlier performance by a beauti- ful run. Jones just missed the goal and half-time arrived with Warrington 3 tries ahead. _</p>
        <p>Woodward restarted and Warrington pressed immedi- ately Jones failing at a free kick. Taylor also tried from a mark lout could only hit the upright. Some desultary play followed for about twenty minutes, tbe scrimmage work spoiling the piety behind. Warringten roused up later on and Duckworth giving to Fish near touch he sped over in fine style and scored io good position, Taylor kicked a goal amid cheers. Fast play by the home backa nonpluseel Teldesl-y repeatedly. Duck- worth, Isherwood and Harris passed smartly and the latter scored a dazhing try at the corner. No goal resul- ted. Aldred created a diversion by a grand run down touch which was ch-oked by Ashurat when looking dangerous. Swift neat scored for Warrington after good play by Isherwood. and Fish had an unsuccessful pop at goal. Just before the finish Morrison caught the ball in front et' goal and rieverly dropped the ball over the bir, raising the horns scora to 2 goals, 6 tries (22 poins) ; ryldesley, nil. Broughton Rangers v. Wigan.</p>
        <p>The Wiganitas were completely overmatched at Brou,ghton, tind the Rangers won by three goals arid five tries to nil. Jackson, Barrett, and Messenger were prominent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
