      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Latchford Rangers v. Widnes White Star.</head>
        <p>Latchford Rangers v. Widnes White Star.</p>
        <p>Played at Wildersp,A on Sttard ty• The visitors won the toss, and Lit:Mord started with 14 men. Widnes pressed, and after a few mieutes play Leadbetter was enabled to score, Harton kicking, a fine goal. Restart- ing, the home team attacked, and play was taken to the Widnes lines, where faulty play by Bate spoiled a splendid chance of the Ittegers guning something sub- stantial. Liadnetter relieved with a kick to centre. From a scrimmage Widoes got possession, and pretty passing to,k play to the home line, where Ashton effected a clearance. The Rangers again pressed, and Bramhall got away nicely,only to be tackled when getting danger- ous. Widnes were penalized on their own line, and Lttchford failed to kilk a goal from an easy positioa. After the each cad was vieite I in rapid buccession. Latchford getting the hes) of matters, and, again, had an easy chance oleqatlizing, but the chance was al- loyed to slip, Widnes cleared for a short period, and at the interval Latchford were pressing strongly, the score reading :—Widnee White Star,ooe goal, one try ; Latch- ford Rutgers, nil. Resuming, Latchford went for the line with de..erminatioo, bu,, were repulsed time after teem Bramhad h,..ing prominent with his strong dashes, and was several times held outside. A free kick to the visitors relieved the pressure somewhat, Ashton being tackled as he was about to return. Latchford got pos- session, and a laxity of speed prevented the home team scoring. The Widnesians now took up the attack, and Leadbetter,rectiving a 'ease at c.ntre,s :fired b-neath the posts after a fine run, the goal kick bAng a failure. For the rest of the game,Letchford remained on the defence- sive and very rarely crossed the half way line. Final : —Widnes White Star, one goal two tries (eight points) ; Latchford Rangers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Yorkshire v. Lancashire.</head>
        <p>Yorkshire v. Lancashire.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the committiee of the Yorkshire Northern Union, held at th, Green Dragon Hotel,Leeds, on Tuesday night, the following team was chosen to play against Lancashire at Halifax this Saturday :- 11 Taylor (Hull), b4ck ; J Driscoll (Hull) and J E Parker (Bramley), centre three-quarters ; A Hambrecht (Bramley), and J Lumley (Leeds), wing three-quarters ; H Rlsinsan (Hunslet), and G Mosley (Leeds Parish Church) half backs ; 1' Broadley (Bradford), J Garth (Batley), A Starks and A Kemp (Hull Kingston Rovers). M Sutcliffe (Huddersfield), A Proctor (Manningbam), W Walton (Wakefield), and J Ramage (Hunslet) forwards. Reserves :—J Metcalfe (Wakefi,.ld), back; J B Goodall (BatleF), centre three-quarter ; H Tulloch (Hull Kings- ton Rovers), wing three-quarter ; Sullivan (Hudders- field). aid Oakland (Batley), half-backs ; R Winekill (Halifax), and F H Chambers (Huddersfield), forward..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Charge Against Salford Spectators.</head>
        <p>Charge Against Salford Spectators.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the Committee of the Lancashire Competition was held in Manchester on Tuesday night. Mr. J. Underwood (Wigan), president, in the chair. The chief item on the agenda was a com- plaint by the Broeghton Ranters Club that a section of the /Raiford spectators committed an assault on their players and committee on leaving the ground on Satur- day, October 14, missiles being thrown at the occupants of the 'bus immediately after the match. After hearing, and carefully considering the evidence, the Committee were of opinion that theSalford Club made splendid police prevision for the match, and were further of opinion that, although an assault was committed, they were in no way to blame. The Committee, however, suggested that the Salford Club should draft a larger number of police into the lane immediately after the match, and that they put warning notices to the spectators on the boarding outside the ground. Halliwell. of Tyldesley, was reported for charging an opponent in the match against Broughton Rangers, and was suspended pending appearance. The Lancashire referees made an applies tion for the fee to be increased from hslf a guinea to a guinea. On a vote the neeesaary two-thirds majority was not obtained, and the application was therefore re- fused. Mr. J Bruckshaw, S:ockport, was accepted as the referee for the match 'against Yorkshire next Satur- day. and Lawton of Oldham, was appainted the Lanca- shire captain. Transfers were granted as follows Holt, Tyldesley, with Wigan ; W Smethurst, Crompton and W liarline,Rnchdale Athletic.with Rochdale Hornets J Morriay. Pendleton, and F Mackie, Salford Trinity, with Tvleesley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>EARLX.STOWN SHAMROCKS 17. KNOWSLEY-ROAD SOCIAL.— Played on the SRA airockl ground on Saturday. The game was fast and exciting from start to finish and whea the whistle blew for the cessation of hostilities honours were even, the score b-ing 2 goals each. On Saturday after till eancluiion of the match, some of the spectar.nrs were not satisfied with the referee's deci- sions and they thereupon sent upon him, the unfortun ate official having to run to escape being maimed. Such conduct as this will very soon bring about the Sham- rocks suspension.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS v. EARLESTOWN ROVERS. —Both the Rovers and Newton were free from league fixtures on Sttiirday and so a friendly eacounter was arranged. This took placa at Nursery Park before a fair gate. Newton were short of five of their regular players, and the Rovers also placed an indifforent team in the field. The game was dull and uninteresting from the start to finish, both teams evidently being well aware that it was only a friendly. Fouls were very frequent, both teams throwing a good deal of feeling into their play. At the c+ll of t'mo the score was Newton 3, Earlestown Rovers 3.</p>
        <p>EARLE9TOW LBIONS V PRESCOT AIg&amp;TELIR3.—PIaTiNi on the Memes on Saturday The Albion.; won the toss, and decided to face the wind for the first half. The Amateurs were the first to show up, and only five minutes had gone by when they obtained the lead. Roused by this reverse the Alnions brought pressure to bear upon the visitors' goal, but t fruitless corner was the only' result. Before the interval the Amateurs further increasA their lead, and when the whistle blew they were two goals ahead. On resuming, the Albinos soon reduced the lead, one of the visit)rs backs putting through his own goal. The Amateurs next scored threa goals in succession, whereas the Albions could only score one, the final score being—Atnateurs. 5; Athlone, 2. Earlestown v. White Star Wanderers..</p>
        <p>The return game between the above clubs was played at Earlestown in threatening weather. The visitors started. Earlestewn were the first; to become dangerous, Settle eluding Wilkinson, and anooting outside the Star right wing obtained possession, Peat defeating Chew. Tney again pressed. Another shot came dangerously near scoring, hitting the cros!,bar, the ball then being cleared. Earlestown forced matters, Settle initiating a pro:waged attack. Lupton, however, frequently inter- vened, and averted any score. A foul in close proximity to the Star goal was taAan by Parr, from which Elsmpson headed over when favourably placed. The wind marred several well-intended efforts by the homesters. Harrison.secur- ing, rut its A nice sprint, and clev,m-ly centred. Hamp- son shooting over the crossbar. A momentary attack by the visitors was nullified, the home left wing again changing the venue, and forcing a couple of abortive corners. NPEwen receiving a good pass from the centre had 3 line opening, but Cottam stepped into the breath, and splendidly frustrated his eff les. Play became more interesting, the home goal hem!r subjected to heavy pressure. Horrigan snit in a splendid shot to Chew,vvhe as finely saved. The homesters were led to the attack by Settle, but the defence and wind prevented any snc- c,As. Earlestnwn were eventually rewarded with a splendid goal, Settle mooting an excellent pass from Flampson, and giving Tlodge chance. The Wanderers played up vigorously and Chew and Johnson each cleared finely. A corner was forcei,the ball going outside. Half-</p>
        <p>time :—Earlestown 1, White Star 1. On turning round Earlestown had the advantage of the hill,and for thefirst quarter of an hoor they mad., qOOl MI of it. pinning the visitors in their goal. lh-y cou'd not, however, pierce the defence, although several good shots were sent is. Ultimately a foul close in goal gave EArlestown a chance. Parr took the kick, and nicely placing in the ruck Waitehouse scored with a swift low snot, thus placinz his side in front. Tha play afterwards was of an even character, the visitors occasionally breaking away, but they never became dangerous, Cattam and Heyes being able to easily deal with all assaults. Final result, Earlestown 2, White Star 1. LANCASHIRE ALLIANCE. Resulta up to Date. Goals. •</p>
        <p>_ Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Agst. Pts. Seacambe Swifts 5 ... 4 ... 1 ... 0 ... 11 7 ... 8 Prascot Warrington Tranmera Hovers 4 ... 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 9 ... 4 ... 5 High Peasley Cross ... 4 ... 1 ... 2 ... 1 ... 3 ... 3 ... 3 Go!borna 5 . 1 .. 3 ... 1 ... 2 ... 10 ... 3 Adlington Bansfurrg Rovers 3 ... Q ... 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 8 ... 1 ▪ nedale U'ted 3 ... 0 ... 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 4 ... 1 Parr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Warrington v. Bamfurlong.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Bamfurlong.</p>
        <p>At Warrington on Saturday afternoon before a large attendance ot spectatcrs, in dull weather. In the first few minutes' play Murray almost beat_Davies,tbe visiting eustodian. Warrington were awarded a free kick, and Hackett put the ball in the goal mouth, but Davies cleared. A minute later Pierpoint almoit beat Dtvies with a good shot. After a good passing movement among all the Warrington forw ti IV!.y score I the first gee!, and Connor soon folio nother. War- rington were penalized and Mdg-al, te,red for Samtur- long. Half-time—Warrington 2 gals, Bamturlong one. During the second half the Warringtonians displayed superior form all round, and goals were ec)red by Jones, Hackett and Connor, the result readin r—Warrington five goals to one. Warringtoo displayed something like their proper form, and all through the game they ex- hibited brilliant form and the majority of their goals, especially those scored by Connor and Riley, were the outcome of really clever exhibitions of combination. For the winners, Downie at back exhibited a sound de- fence, and Aspinall between the sticks was vary safe. Hackett was excellent at half, and both Connor sod Riley of the forwarde displayed a dash which betokened success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Bowden v. Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>At Bowden on Saturd4y. The home aide made the pace rather warm for the visitors, and scored half-a- dozen goals against, one uy the visitors. Bmdcn, six ; Stockton Heath, one. Latchford v. Pendleton.</p>
        <p>This match vras Wayed At Pendleton on Siturclay,and after a very fast and exciting gam:, resulted in the visi- tors securing their fourth consecutive victory by the narrow margin of four goals to three. For the home team the backs played a dashing game, whilst for the vi,iit)rs Carter shone brilliantly is no forwards, sciaing the first three goals, whilst, the fourth was netted by F. Grounds. The hacks also played owl defensive game, Kirkland and Heald being especially prominent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Gospel Hall, Forster-street.</p>
        <p>On Sunday night Mr. H. Whitehouse addressed the meeting on the words of Job "Iknow that my Redeemer liveth." He pointed out how clearly, this showed Job's confidence and hope, even when circumstances w-re all against him. Some people like to translate the word " know " into " hope," and when asked " Do you know Christ as your Redeemer ?" tue reply i 3 " I hope so." Job could say "I know," Paul could say "I know," and John wrote to believers in Christ that they might know they had eternal life. This confidence gives peace in the midst, of trouble. The knowledge of a living Redeemer being now in heaven assures the believer that he too will shortly be there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>Foreign missionary anniversary services were held in the above placs of worship on Sunday. At the morn- ings, service the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. Theo- dore Bishop, who delivered an appropriate discourse. In the afternoon a service of a very unique character was held, when toe Rev. C. H. Hocken, Mrs. Hocken, and their little boy appeared in the costume of a Hindoh family. Mrs. Hocken fang some native lyrics and kanerese and Mr. Hocken gave an account of his experiences in India. Thers was a good attendance presided over by Mr. Faulkner, of York, and the collection realized a good amount. In the evening the Rev. C. H. Hocken preached, founding his discourse on Psalms 96 and 3, and spoke of the spiritual destitu- tion of the Elinduhs as under the dominion of idolatry. The proceeds for the day ans,u.ited to nearly £l4. 4On Monday the annual missionary me Aiaz was beld;presi ded over by Mr. J. S. Snodly, of Peolteth. The Rector on the War.</p>
        <p>The Rector (Rev Canon Willis) preached at Warring- ton Parish Caurch, on Sunday on the war in the Transvaal, taking for his text the words—" And David said, what have I now done ? Is there not a cause ? " I, Samuel xvii. 29). Tbe Rector asked why we as a nation were at war, and answered that it was to main- tain our supremacy in the Tr4nsvaal, to secure justice for the British and kindness for the natives. We were also fighting in defence of our owa possessions as well as in the interests of trade, commerce and civilization' Pie was of opinion that it was a jut war, as our duty clearly was to maintain the greatness and integrity of the British empire,as well as to secure equal rights and fair treatment for all the people' Then the question was asked "Could it have been avoided ? and to that he answered No ; in the first place beoause the Dutch were determined not to acknowledge British supremacy, in the second place because they were determined not to give equality and show kinduess, in the third place bs- cause they wished to drive us out of the land altogether, and in the fourth place because they had themselves de- clared war and invaded and annexed our land. In answer to the question " Why are other nations un- favourably disposed towards us ? " he would reply that it was because we were of ano'her race and tongue, be- cause we were isolated and independent, because we were prosperous, powerful and rich, because we hal condemned what was wrong, because there was a closer relationship and affinity with the Dutch than with us, and because they were a small and brave nation who settled in South Africa before we did. It was our duty to assist the Gevernment, to show sympathy for and give relief to the soldiers, especially the wounded and their families ; to have respect and cinsideration for the Boers, and to possess a powerful and trustful spirit during the progress of the struggle, trusting in God to bring all things right in the end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting of this Society was held on Sunday. Mr. 13. Farrar presided, and in the course of a short address he referred to some ot the good work which had been done by the society-. Critics who asserted that it was merely an entertainmeet did not know what they were talking about.—Councillor James Rimini, of Warringten, delivered the address. In a passing refer- ence to the war in South Africa, he said our soldiers might kill the Boers but he did not think their children would like us any the better for it. (Hesr,hear.) Force was not a remedy but he believed righteousuess was Proceeding to speak of the horns life of the People of this country he remarked that they did not take sufficient interest in their homes. By many it was neglected to a great extent. If they cared to look they would find there was much squelour and wretchedness in Warringtee. There were many lc- sanitary dwellines, and he wee; hopine to see many of the places which were now called houses levelled to the ground. He should vote for it without the least qualms of conscience. It was not altogether the stuitary Con- dition of the houses which wee at fault but the con- dition of many of the folks who lived there. (Hear, hear.) Women had always exorcised a good deal of in- fluence over men, and he believed many of the women in Warrington could make this town a good deal brighter and better if they made up their minds to do as. A tremetidom respoesitility rested upon the Christian Church in teaching the women for much de- pended upon them. He would like to see inPating3 for women in oonh.ction with that society. (Hear.hear.) In conclusion he asked his hearers to exert an influence for good in their own homes.—The Rev. J. Yonge said he had been thinkiee for Rome time of the clesirabllity ot establishing a weekly meeting for women in connection with the P.S.A. movement: He did not see why they should ant have a P.M E. for women. It would be necessary for women to work the seoiety and if any were walla?: to engage in this work he would be elect. to hear from them —Mr. Smiley and Mr. Coates also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. E. Powell and the Church Crisis. preaching at St. James' Church on Sunday evenina on the subject" flow is the Church constituted ?" the Rev. F. E. Pow.ll, vicar, took for his text the worth " Ar.,d no man tak4h the honour unto himself but when he is called of God, even as was Aaron." (Hebrews v. 4).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>STOP A COUGH IN ONE NIGHT.</head>
