      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>FLORENCE EDITH COOK,</head>
        <p>FLORENCE EDITH COOK,</p>
        <p>It seldom falls to the lot of a child to have the succession of ailments following one after the other as was the case with the little girl whose photograph we here reproduce. Measles, bronchitis. conges- tion of the lungs, and whooping couch were included in ber sufferings within a year, and reduced her System to a state of emaciation. Her mother in describing how the child was finally cured, s tys:— " 96, Verney Street, "South Bermondsey, London. S.E. " March, 3rd, 1899.</p>
        <p>" Dear Sirs.—l feel I cannot pea too highly of the great benefit our baby has derived from Scott's Emulsion. Last March she bad the measles, and this was follow(d during the year with two attacks of bronchitis and congestion of 'ht lungs, and in January lest by pneumonia. bron- chitis and whooping cough, which natura!ly brought her down very low and we-k. Seeing your advertisements appearing in the news- papers at this time, and readily! of the many cases Scott's Emulsion had benefited. I determined tl try the preparation for my little girl, and am truly thankful I did so. I got her one of your medium- sized bottles, and before this was exhausted she seemed to be much better and brightersso that I con- tinued tin tteatment. She has now tazen four bottles, and is looking a hinny, bright babr with a fine colour, very unlike the poor palo thit.g she was bi.fore taking your splendid remedy. Our little girl is now the picture of health, and words cannot expr^as our</p>
        <p>He showed that the apostolic succession was not the whole of the Catholic position, hut it wee, from the point of view of the organisation of the divine boiy, very important constituent to it. The apostolic sucees- sion was the pledge of historic continuity, aiel had al- ways been the mark of the E iglish Church. New, if any doctrine should hive died of ridicule that A ..octrille of the succession should have been dead and buried long ago, No doctrine hid been more exposcd to scoffing and contempt, and yet be claimed for it that nothing could be more consistent with sound reason and practi- cal commonsense. Apostolic succession merely meant that the same rule held good in the Chric- tian Church as in every well-ordered secular Government, namely that officers could not appoint themselves, but must derive their commission frem the supreme central authority or from persons empowered thereby. In all secular matters they awed upon that principle. Who were the officers in the army ? Just those who had received their commissima from tbeproper authorities. Who were the teachers in the school ? Just those—whether they were properly fitted for the poet or not—who had heen appointed by the proper autheritv, the school Board, the govereors, the trustees, or the managers. The same principle held good in Noncon- formist bodies. There might beplen'y of barristers who knew far more law, and would be far more competent to administer it than some of the judges, but a barrioter who should presume because of his superior education, superior skill and fitness to occupy the place of judee might run the risk of being made to occupy the place of the prisoner at the bar. In every department of our social organization the same principle held good. No officer could appoint himself, but must derive his com- mission from the recognized autherity, and in the recognized way; and that was just the principle in- volved in apostolic succession. Christ Church Latchford. The Vicar's Salary.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary vestry meeting of the parishioners of Christ Church, Latchford, was held in the schools on Saturday evening. The Vicar (the Rev. F. A. Screeton) presided, and there were also present :—Messrs J Fair- slough, Kirkham, Lowe, Margets, Longbettom, Dr J Gornall, Broadbent, Tinsley, Clare, Woods, Chapell, Norman, Starkey, Caxon, Warburton, and Dakin.—The opening business was of a formAl character, having re- ference to the posting of a list of the church fees. Having settled the matter the vestry decided to alter the time of the Sunday morning service from 11 to 10-45, the change to the effect from the beginning of the new year. The remaining business having reference to the vicar, he left the meeting and the chair was taken by his warden, Mr. Fairclough.— The Chairman said the encetiog had been called because Mr. Screeton had accepted the living in lieu of Mr. Prescott, who had resigned, and the ehatage necessi- tated certain financial arrangements. Mr. Screeton had some diffidence about accepting the living as it was only worth about £BO a year. Mr. Dakin and he (the speaker) had several conversations with him, and he told him that he felt sure the congregation would do their duty in the matter. It was understood that Mr. Screeton would be satisfied with £2OO a year. He (the speaker) informed him that he thought the congregation would have little difficulty in raising that sum. The matter being left at that it was thought desirable to have a meeting of the parishoners. One of the means that had been suggested was the establishment of pew rent's.— Dr. Gornall said the great movement during the latter Part of this century had bean to free the church. The ideal of a parish church was one which both rich and poor had an equal right to rezard as their own. He therefore felt disappointed that the question of pew rents had been brought forward. Heimagined that Mr. Screeton would have objectad to receive a portion of his income from such is source. Though as a matter of principle he objected to pew rents, he was not prepared to oppose any resolution that the vestry might egrets to. —ln further discussion it was stated that the Vinar in addition to the £BO would have a third of the ordinary offertories and the usual Easter offering. The balance to be raised, it was undeistood, was about £so.—Mr. Lowe proposed that the seats on the north side and trace- cent be allocated, and the seats on the south side remain free, all seats to be deemed free until appropriated.—</p>
        <p>(From a photograph.)</p>
        <p>W. B. SWEETMPN'S HAIR LOTION. The Best Remedy for FA.LLING OR WEAK HAIR, And Sure Preventative of Baldness. PRIOR 2s. CJ. PER BOTTLE. 5, RANP. PRNET, LIVERPOOL (Opposite Central Station).</p>
        <p>yltituds for all that Seott's Emulsion has done for ner. It scarcely seems posiible that, in an short a therd should ha such a change in our child alter all she ban gone through. I only wish I bad known of your preparation before, for I am con- vincel my child would then have been spared much euff.tiing. I can speak from experience of the wonderful properties of Scott's Emulsion, and it will always be a pleasure to me to recommend it.— Believe me, very sincerely yours, •1 (Signed) (Mils.) JANE CcoK." . .</p>
        <p>Surely the foreg4ing lett u. gives a very satisfac- tory account or Scutt's Emulsion and proves this remedy to be splendidly adapted to the needs of children. Scott's Emulsion enriches the blood, has a favourable effect upon digestion, pro- motes the making of healthy flesh, and gives the system the vital strength with which to resist dis- ease. Scott's Emulsion is a form of cod-liver oil which everybody can take. It does not upset digestion, and it h in palatable that children always lock forward to the time of taking it with eagerness, and it is often difficult to keep them from taking more than their dose. The best part of it all is that children quickly respond to Scot' s Emulsion and are restored to robust health.</p>
        <p>You can obtain a sample of Scptt's Emulsion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Buvrne, Limit..d, Manufacturing Chemists, 95, Great Saffron Hill, Lention, E C., and mentioning this paper All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woeds seconded.—Mr. Clarke said ke was opposed to Dr. Goma!' on the subj Act of pew rents. He thought a good many of the congregation would be willing and anxious to have seats assigned to them.-3ar. Cozon ti ,taght the principle was bad, and proposed as an amen lment that no seats be appropriated. Dr. Gornall s-concied.—The ameodnyeat was lost, five voting for it and nine againat.—Before the close of the meting Mr. Lowe drew attention to to fact that the churchyard was used as a playground,and was in fact the only play- ground io Latchtord. He proposed that it be fenced or that the Cerporatioh be asked to provide a playground. —lt was agreed that the churchwardens should give the matter their consideration.</p>
