      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>letters to the Obitor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. .otty:r3 of letters intended for publication must in all cares send their names and addresses to lb,. Editor. Natters intendt d for publicatiou must b 3 writtcn on one side only of the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>THE ASSISTANT OVERSEERSHIP. Tunstall's letter in your iciale of Saturday Was so long and too till of inaccuracies and animus, to admit of a reply at reasonable lam therefore reluctantly compelled to content my- self, with saying, simply, that what I wrote a fortnight ago is a truthful and strict account of the transactions and one that can be fully sustained —I am, your truly, J. SHAW GREEN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>FRACAS AT BURTONWOOD.</head>
        <p>FRACAS AT BURTONWOOD.</p>
        <p>Hoisting the Black Flag at the Vicar's House. At the Warringtcn County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day, before Messrs S It Armitage, and J Ball, Thomas Gordon, who is smployed as a horse keeper and stable master at a colliery, was summoned for assaulting Thomas Dutton, wheelwright, Burtonwood. on the 21st October,—Mr. J. W. Knowles, solicitor, appeared for the complainant and defendant was represented by Mr. A. Browne.-1 cross-summons had been taken out against Dutton, but Mr. Browne said ho might withdraw it owing to evidence he had heard recently. Mr. Knowles said that Dutton was a wheelwright carrying on business at Iterlenwoe,d, and Gordon was employed in a coal pit as horse keeper and stable mas- ter. The men had not spoken to each other for some years as Gordon was a supporter of the Vicar and Dutton was at any rate antagonistic to him. The two men therefore were not friendly. At 8.15 on the night in question Gordon came and knocked at Dutton's door. Dutton opened it and Gordon said "Do you want to see ine ?" and Dutton replied "I don't want to see you nor have anything to do with you" and closed the door. Gordon then told him that he would have to have some- thing to do with him. Dutton went into the house and eat down and Gordon commenced kicking at tho door. He eventually opened it and rushed at Dutton and hit him on the side of the neck behind the ear. Dut- ton then got a stick and struck Gordon several blows on the arms to prevent him from further assaulting him. There was a lamp burning cu the table and fearing that it might get knocked over in the struggle complainant rushed out of the house. Gordon followed him and they had a further scuffle in the garden. Defendant got hold of Dutton and pulled him into the road. Gordon then stumbled on some steps and injure i his head. Dutton ran cff in his stocking- feet to look for assistance and eventually a neighbour went back to the house. He found two carriage lamps lying on tbe kitchen floor broken, and there were also some blacking brushes. A chair was knocked over. P.C. Gerrie was afterwards called and saw the condition of the house. Thomas Dutton, the complainant gave evidence in support of this statement. In croes.examination he admitted having been before the court for assault before but the case was dismissed. Be denied that Gordon was lying in the road for three- quarters of an ho n. because he hit him on the tlead with a stick. A scan named Woolfine and P.C. Gerrie corrotor- ated. In crose-examination the:constable said the wounds on `Gerelon's head could have been caused hy a fall but tile notches of Dutton's stick cerresponded.with them. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne, for the defence, said he thought that when the Bench heard the evidence he should bring for- ward they could came to no other conclusion but that Dutton had been a brute te Gordon and was lucky that he did not stand charged with manslaughter or murder. Be purposed to lay information for unlawful wounding and ask the Bench to commit him for trial. Astley King, an ironmonger, of Eerlestewn, said that he was riding on his bicycle in Lumber-lane, Burton- wood, and when nearing the house of Dutton he heard a terrible noise of swearing and fighting. He saw the shadow of someone fighting in the kitchen and when he bad paAsed the gate the men Dattou and Gordon cacaos into the road fighting. All at once toes' crashed down one upon the other, and he ran up to the.es. The man who was on the top (Dutton) jumped up, and ran along- side the hedge. He (witness) turned his attentioe to the man on the grouted, and fouad his head was in a peel ut blood. He at first thought the man was dead, as he was totally uncseacious. He raised him up and obtained a chair from the people living next door and leaned him against it. He applied flour to stop the bleeding- He (witness) was an ambulance man having obtained a medal for ambulance work. When the men fell Duttin said " Now you b-I've done you now." Gordon was lying in the road about three quarters of an hour. Dutton came down the other side of the road and -.yolked quietly into the house. When Gordon came round he had no idea where he was. They bad practicelly to carry him home. Ile (witness) piclted up the stick produced and cave it to the police. The wounds on Gerden'a head could have been caused by the Stick vrhich wee j agged. Defendant also gave evidence, He said he went to see Dutton about his bad conduct towards bin. Dutton got a stiok and hit him several blows on the head in rapid succession which mule him unconscious. He went to the house a day or two Afterwards for his cap and Dutton said he would put a bullet through him if he did not leave the place. Ire cross-tx smination G irdoa admitted having had twelve months imprisonment for detreuding his em- ployers. A charge of stealing coal was made against him but wss withdrawn. The rea4istrates r-tired to consider till ir decision and co their return The Chairman said they had carefully considered the case and bad come to the conclusion that the whole evi- dence was against defendant. He would be fined Ll and costs or ice default would have a montlfa imprisonment. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles applied for the advLcate's fee and it WAS granted. The Vicar of Burtensrold (Rcv.E. J. Mitchell) said he avantc3 to say a word. "1" The Chairman : We cannot hear anything. The case</p>
        <p>Is over. The Vicar : Is it not a serious matter ? People can- not piss this man's (Dutton's) house-- The Chairman : We won't listen to that. The Vicar continuing said he had ben continually annoyed by Dut-.on. Be had hoisted the black flag at his h^,use and had erieil"D-ath to the Parson." He said he wished he and all his "crow " were in hell. Toe Magistrate 3 refused to listea t) what Mr. Mitchell had to say. Mr. Browne decidsd to proceed with the cross sum- mons. Gordon was put in th 2 witness box ani stated that he received the woun Is on his head from Dult m's stick. The Chairman said they were prepirel to think that Dutton had hit the man on the head, but they did Out think it ..as aa assault when Gordon C 41119 into his hence. They therefore cl.4.smilsed the case. The advocate's fee was kAused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>YOUNG LADY ATTACKED BY A</head>
        <p>YOUNG LADY ATTACKED BY A</p>
        <p>TRAMP.</p>
        <p>Serviceable Conduct of Warrington Cyclists. Oa Monday afternoen, as two Warrington gentle- men. named Mr. Garside and Mr. Woodier, were cyclieg through some lanes near Stretton, they ob- served a lady's music caqi lying on the roadside. They stopped, and, picking it up, examined it, and found that it belonged to a Miss Ellen Hatch, who reside at Stretton. They went to the residence of the young lady to deliver it, when Miss Hatch's pareets informed them that tueir daughter had pre vionsly left home in order t 3 co to a music lesson- Shortly after, Miss Hatch, who is a young lady of 17, returned in a nervous state, and informed her parents that etie had been waylaid by a tramp, who had mot only demanded money from her but had taken her music case from her,evidently believing it contained valuables. She managed to get away from hire, and then made the best of her way home. Whilst they were in the house talking over the affair a rough-looking man was observed passing the house, and .Nlies Hatch recognised him as the tramp who had waylaid her. Mr. Garside and Mr. Woodier then fellowed the man, who, seeing he was pursued ran across some fields, but Mr. Garside awl Mr. Woodier followed him, and after a struggle brought him back to the house, where he waq secured. Police Coustable Ruxten was sent for, and the man whoze name was James Barker, was given in eharge. He was brought betore the Dareebury raagistretes on Tuesday, who s.nt him to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Great Sankey School Board.</head>
        <p>Great Sankey School Board.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above Board was held on Tues- day evening,Mr. 'Talton presiding. There were also present—NlessrP. W. D. Jeans, jun. (clerk), Perks, Wright, and Kilshaw, the Rev. E. S. Jackson, and Mr. Clarke (schoolmaster). The schoolmaster re- ported that the average attendance for the past month WAS 250.6, and the fees paid .E 3 3s 2d. The Clerk nported that the continuation school was now in full swing, there being 40 students who at- tended regularly.</p>
