      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WAR. LATEST TELEGRAMS,</head>
        <p>THE WAR. LATEST TELEGRAMS,</p>
        <p>BOERS MOVING UPON COLENSO.</p>
        <p>A Boer movement i 3 developing to the south-east. Their object, it is bed,ved, i 3 to seize the railway near Colenso, about 15 mileA below Ladysmith, and cut our communications with Maritzburg and Durban.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WHITE REINFORCED.</head>
        <p>WHITE REINFORCED.</p>
        <p>It is understood that considerable reinforcements have reached Sir George White during the last forty-eight hours.—" Standard."</p>
        <p>Boer Invasion of Zululand.</p>
        <p>PIETERXARITZBTIRG, October 31, 3-55 p.m.</p>
        <p>Reliable news has reached here that the Boers are occupying parts of Zululand. It is reported that the enemy have taken Pomeroy, which is 50 miles away from Groytown. Psmeroy, it should be noted, is really as far north as Ladysmith, and is on the road from Dundee to Greytown. It is about 30 miles east of Lady- smith.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Fighting at Kimberley. Enemy's Loss Very Heavy.</head>
        <p>The Fighting at Kimberley. Enemy's Loss Very Heavy.</p>
        <p>CAPETOWN, WEDNESDAY, 10 35 il,lll.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant and Adjutant Lownde4,l4t Battaiion Loyal North Lancashire, severe cmpound fracture of thigh. Bullet removed ; progr.s4ing satisfactorily. Lieutenant Bingham,of Ist Loyal North Lanetshire Regiment,severe compound comm!nated trac'ure Itic thigh. Three splinters ; bone rPmor,d ; progressing sstisfactorily. Private Milner, lie North LaticAshire Regiment, severe thigh wound ; progressing 3atisf4ctorily. Gunner Bankier, Diamond Fields Artillery, compound fracture of arm ; progressing sstisfac•oi ily. Remairder of wounded progressing satisfhctorily.</p>
        <p>KekewiCh has been informed from various sourecs that the enemy's loss was very heavy on the occasicn ot the fighting at Kimberley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK. British Disaster at Ladysmith. 1,000 Men Captured After Heavy Fighting.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK. British Disaster at Ladysmith. 1,000 Men Captured After Heavy Fighting.</p>
        <p>The folhwing messige has been received tram Sir George White. _</p>
        <p>LADYSMITH, Oct. 30, 11.35 p.m.</p>
        <p>I have to report a disaster to a column Pent by toe to take a position on hill to guard thy I,ft flabk of the trcops in he opPrations to-day. Tre Royal Irish Fusiliers, N.:, 10 Mnuntairt B.ttery, and the Gloucester- shire Regiment over, surrounded In the hills, and atter losing heavily had to capitulate. Casualties not yet as- certained. A man ef Royal Irish Fusiliers employed as hospital orderly came in under flog of truce with a letter from the survivors (?) of the column, and asked for assistance to bury the dead. I fear there is no doubt of the truth of the report. I formed the plan in carrying out which the disaster occurred, and am alone respon- sible for the plan. No blame whatever attaches to the troops, as the position was untenable. [The number of troops its the two battalions would be under 1,500.]</p>
        <p>. - Inform Anon received later makes it clear that the dis- aster is due to the mules stampeding, and the conse- quent loss of guns and small arm ammunition reserve. They surrendered at two p.m., aftr seven hours con- tinued fighting. Over 20 of our dead has been counted lying on the open plateau which f,4ms the summit of the hill. Quite a hundred wounded were collected during the morning. 870 British primers were sent to Pre- totia.</p>
        <p>LADYSM ITH SHELLED. Sharp Fighting. Sir G. White telegraphed on October 30, at 4.30 p employed all troops here to-lay except an ob- ligatory garrison for the works. The force moved at 11 p.m. last night, and during some nieht firing the bat- tery mules stampeded with some of the gun', which, however, I hope to recover. I detai;ed two brigade divisions Royal Field Artill( ry and five battalione infan- try, aided by the cavalry under General _French, to atzack a position on which the enemy had yesterday mounted guns. We found this position evacuated, but our force was attacked with considerable vigour by what I believe were General Joubert's troops. They had many guns, and showed in great numbers. Our troops were all in action, and we pushed the enemy back for several miles, but did:not succeed in reaching his laagers. Our 1093P3 are estimated at between eighty and 100, but that of the enemy must have been much greater, the fire ef our guns appearing very effective. I now have some naval guns, which have temporarily silenced, and, I hope, will permanently dominate, the enemy's best guns, with which he has been bombarding the town at a range of over 6,000 yards. Arrival of Sir Redvers Buller at Capetown. Enthusiastic Reception. CAPSTOWN, TUESDAY. The arrival of General Buller evoked one of the most striking popular demonstration's ever seen at Capetown. The Dunottar Castle was expected yesterday afternoon, but to the disappointment of the crowd she failed to appear. Llte at night the steamer was signalled, and she was safely docked at nine .'clock this morning. Sir Redvers Buller, who was received by General Forestier- Walker, commanding at the Cape, entered a carriege and drove rapidly to Government House, preceded by a detachment of mounted police and escorted by the Cape Mounted Volunteers. The reception accorded the General was undeniably a splendid one. Cheer upon cheer was raised, and the enthusiasm displayed was in every way remarkable for its intensity. Shoats of "Bravo, General," and" Avenge Msjuba " were frequent. The General, wbose face remained impassive, acknowledged the cheers with a military salute. BOER LOSSES AT GLENCOE. Five Hundred Killed and Wounded. The General Officer commanding in Natal reports that the Beers' loss at Talana (near Glencoe) on October 20 was 500 killed and wounded ; that three Boer guns were dismounted, and disabled and left on the top of the hill. SOME COMPARISONS. The following comparisons will be of interest to those who are followiug the caurte of events in South Africa : Cape Colony is about the size of France. Natal ,•</p>
        <p>Orange Free State England and Wales. Transvaal Grest Britaia. It Durb• n to Pietermaritzburg... London to Peterborough Ladysmith York _ _</p>
        <p>' Glencoe Darlington ••• ~ " Newoastle • Newcastle St Laing's Neck ... „ Berwick „ Johannesburg ... „ Dundee " Pretoria • Aberdeen Capetown to Kimberley Sutherlandshire Blown( onteln ... „ John o' Groats Pretoria ••• „ Belgrade Pt Bulawayo Consta;:thoople •. Kimberley to Vryburg ••• .. Birmingham ~ Mafeking ••• .. Lancaster Pretoria to Natal Frontier ... „ Liverpool Mafeking ••• It Doncaster „ Bloemfontein Carlisle Bloemfontein to Kimberley ... „ Rugby TABLE OF LOSSES. Appended is a table showing the totals officially re- ported as killed or wounded in action, or missing. The figures include all i uoplernentary lists, when new names are mentioned, receivNl up t,) date. Where soldiers are reported " since dead," the fact is duly nritified after the name, but the number is not added, for obvious reasons, to those returned as killed in action. Oct. 15, Mafeking— Killed Wounded Oct. 20, Glencoe— Kill.-cl Wound,d Oct 21, 'glands Lasgte— Killed Wounded Oct 22, Crocodile River— Killed 2 Wounried Oct. 24, Rietfontein— Killed l2 Wounded Oct. 24, Kimberley— Killed 3 Wounded Oct. 28, Ladysnoith— Wounded</p>
        <p>632 Killed ll5 Wounded 577 MissiAg at Cilencee •'', Missing at Rietfontein Missing Crocodile River Woun,ied (including in the above totals) who have since been reported dead • l3 MissiNg portions of Ginucesters, Irish Fusiliers, and all Mountain Battery, say</p>
        <p>Tctll Lags —Killed Wounded Since Dead Missing ' 1,822</p>
        <p>Spiritualism.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Green, of Manchester. paid her first visit to the Warrington Spiritualist Society on Sunday, and gave addresses on "The gifts of the Spirit and "The evidences of Immortality," followed by clair- voyance. On Monday evening the platform was occupied by Mr. Llewellyn, a local medium, who gave a good address on "Sowing the Seed," and afterwards gave some clairvoyance.</p>
        <p>A. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER 'viz CLOCK ran BEST</p>
