      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 3, 1899.</p>
        <p>Bewsey Ward.</p>
        <p>J. Shaw Green (L) E. Cunniugton (lud) 195</p>
        <p>Majarity Orford Ward.</p>
        <p>L. Greening (0) 523 S. Gough (Ind)</p>
        <p>Majority Latchford Ward.</p>
        <p>A. JollAy (C) J Pemb3rton (Ind)</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
        <p>References at The Liberal Club.</p>
        <p>After the declaration of the poll,a gathering of Liberal workers and others was held in one of the committee rooms of the club.</p>
        <p>Councllor SIIAM GREEN on entering the room was heartily cheered, and was immediately called upon to make a speech. In complying with the request, he ttestiked the workers for the efforts they had put forth on his behalf, and said that although the majority was not so great as on the last occasion when he contested the ward, still when it was considered that the poll was a light one it would be readily seen that there was no occasion to be in any way dispirited. His workers had been from the very beginning cock- sure of victory and the necessity for strenuous exertion had not been so fully realised. Everybody, however, on their side was well satisfied, and he had very sincere pleasure in accepting the position at their hands of representing their interests for another period of three years. (Cheers.) If he might be permitted to say so, there was no ward in the town for which he had such a high esteem for as Bewsey and its workers. He desired to recoenise with thankfulness that the compact with the political parties had been kept. Others from the opposite side in politics had willingly and heartily joined with workers on their side and the result showed it. (Cheers.) Proceeding to refer to the action of the Ratepayers' Association and the yellow placard they had issued, he said it appeared to him that a grave charge could be raised against those who had written it. A serious charge was made against some of the hest members of the Town Counel,who with singleness of eye and honesty of purpose were striving to do their best for the town. (Cheers ) There was no such thing is an " inner circle "striving to keep information from the general members of the Council or from the public, and he wished also to refute the suggestion that any such thing as "hole and corner" work was done. At all events he had had twelve years experience on the Town Council, and he was altogether unconscious of any such thing. (Cheers.) He was proud of the two new representatives el, clod on the GMliCil this November, and he felt satisfied that the composition of the Council at the present time was a remarkably good one, and the great matters which were before the Corpor &amp;on at the present time would be entered upon and carried rut in a spirit of earnestness and desire for the welfare of the town, which must result in successful issues. (Cheers.) In conclusion he desired to personally express his thanks to Mr. Rogers, Mr. Naylor, and Mr. Edwards, who had specially devoted themselves on his behalf to the work of that day. (Cheers). -Councillor F. R. ROBKITS congratulated the workers upon the handsome victory, which he considered was in some respects more important than if it had been merely the result of a political fight, inasmuch as the re turn of the independent eindidate would have implied a vote of censure upon Mr. Councillor Shaw Green as one of the !fading promoters of the Corporation Bill, whilst a political fight would have meant that the man was chosen ler his 'colour. (Bear,h , rar. He looked upon Mr. Green MI one of the useful members of the Council, and his rejPetton would have been a serious matter (Cheer.) . . . _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON stated that he was satisfied with the re- sult of the contest. He thought they bad had to face a stronger opposition on the Dallam side than on any pre- vious occasion. They had to thank Conservative workers for the way they had helped them. One of their most hard-working opponents had been amongst their best workers that day. (Cheers.) A Votcm : Who is he ?</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON : M. Edwaris. (Cheers.) Proceeding, the spnaker remarked that ti.ey were also greatly in- debted to Councillor Rogers. He (Mr. Plinston) was proud of their returned representative. and should be pleased to fight for him again. (Cheers.) Mr. Ancngn also spoke, and referred to the excellent band of workers Councillor Shaw Green had had that day.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSEPH OWEN said he was sure they were all very pleased indeed at the result of the day's work. (Cheers ) The election had been won by united action on the part of the workers who bad had bet one aim and purpose before them and that was to place Councillor Shaw Green at the head of the poll. (Cheers.) In their representative—Mr. Green—they had one of the best men in the Warrington Town Council. If they followed the proceedings of the Gained they would be convinced of that. Not only did he further the in- terests of Bewsev Wardlbut of the town as • who'e. lie hoped at the next election they would le able to put in another good Liberal as they had done that day. (Cheers.) Mr. NAYLOB said he lookld upon Councillor Shaw Green as one of the finest representatives of Liberalism they had ever had on the Council. Mr. Green did yen- man uervice in connection with the recent Corporation Bill which was fraught with such important issues for the town. In the hands of Councillor Green the rate- payers' money was perfectly Safe) for ' he would carefully scrutinise the expenditure of the Corpotatinn. (Cheers). As Councillor Srt Aw GRRIN left the room,the audience sang " For he's a jolly good fellow " and afterwards raised three hearty cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS</p>
        <p>MEETING IN SUPPORT OF MR. PEMBERTON'S CANDIDATURE.</p>
        <p>A meeting in support ef the candidature of Mr. John Pemberton was held in St. James' School, Latchford, on Monday evening. Mr. Joseph Podrnore presided but the attendance was very meagre. In openiug the pro- ceedings Mr. PODMOTIE expressed his regret that there was such a poor attendance. One of the reasons why not many were present was that most of their supporters were de- ing work in the ward. Another reason was, he was very sorry to say, that there was in that ward,infloence at work, what he called undue influence, to alter or c n- trol the votes of workingmen. He was very sorry in- deed that at the end of the nineteenth century they had not the liberty which they were supposed to possess. Workingmen could not exercise their right to vote ac- cording to their wishes, but influence was brought to bear, which, to say the least was unfair. He should ' like to say a word about the citizen's union that had been formed in order to bring forward Mr. Pemberton and other men perhaps in the future if it was successful, as candidates independent of polities. They had a number of men in their midst, Conservatives, Liberals and Socialists. The three political parties had combined to fight the candidature upon lines of citizen- ship ter the good government and welfare of the town. (Hear, hear.) They had the sympathy of many,he was pleased to say. The Vicar of St. James' told him that he cordially supported Mr. Pemberton's candidature and entirely endorsed his programme. He had received a circular signed by persons representing two parties and he wanted to call attention to some matters mentioned on it. The iir,t was that they had entered into an agreement for the purpose of rendering unnecessary a political contest in any of the wards. That meant to say that they were taking out ef the werkingman's hands his political rights and that they were choos- ing what was best for him and he had not to have any tight whatever. He most emphatically protested against anything of that kir,d and claimed his right as a cit z-n to say whether he would have this man or that man to represent him. He did not think the compromise should have any influence upon the electors of Latchford ward because the object seemed to him in plain English to be to save a little cant. Mr. Pemberton was a workingman and he thought they would all agree with him that the Town Council would be a great deal better with a few workingman in it. If elected Mr. PPmberton would do his best and would let them kn )w perhaps evtry six months what be had done.</p>
        <p>Mr. PMMBERTON addressed the mei ting at considerable Iq t h He seid that the circular issued by the Liberals and Conservatives said the compromise had been effec- ted with the object of saving the ratepayers' money. Be had had a conversation with two Councillors and they said that the reason was that the Liberal and Conservative coffers were very low—(Lsugh- ter)—and they had no money to fight an election. They could not get men of intellect and capacity to spend £4O or £5O to win an election. He protested against the disfranchising 4 the ratepayers. They were entited to say who should represent them in the Council. What had we sent our soldiers to the Transvaal for ? We to-day were losing some of our best and noblest men in the war. They had gone out for the purpose of fighting for what ? The Government claimed that it was for the franchise of the people. They wanted Englishmen, Boers, and Uitlanders to be treated alike and have the franchise equally. We had sent our men out and were spending mi.lions of money to claim the franchise of all our citizens, yet, here in Warrington, the Town Council was just adopting what the Boers had been doing. (Laughter and applause.) Be claimed it was an insult to the ratepayers of Warrington. (Hear, hear.) He asked them for their votes as a protest against the undue methods they had applied to them. He asked them what had Alfred Jolley done whilst he had been in the Council? He did not give any statement of what he had done in his address. For some six months he had been using all his pewer and energy to try and raise the rents of the Market people. The returns for the market bad cleared for the rates of the town £2,000. If that was so he thought that instead of raising the rents they ought to be reduced a little. He was also informed that the gas had made a clear profit of £2,000 and that being so he claimed that the consumers ought to have it a little cheaper. Mr. Pemberton also referred to the housing of the poor. He said that many houses in the town were not fit for dogs let alone human beings to live in. (Hear, hear.) He was also in favour of play- grounds being provided ter children. He asked as a citizen for their votes. He had an honest intention, of purpose and a desire to do something right, and just for the betterment of the town in which they lived, (Ap- plause.).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>– On the mot'on of Mr. Stout a vote of confidence in Mr. Pemberton was unanimously pass2d. The meeting then terminated.</head>
        <p>- On the mot'on of Mr. Stout a vote of confidence in Mr. Pemberton was unanimously pass2d. The meeting then terminated.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENTS FARE BADLY.</p>
        <p>There was less enthusiasm this year in connection with the Municipal Elections at Warrington owing to the compromise of the two political parties not to contest any Wards for two years. The hands of the political leaders were, of course, forced to some extent by the action of the Ratepayers and Property Owners' Association and the Citizens Union, but as the result of the poll shows, diminished interest was taken in the contest. Not one of the Indepen- dent candidates was returned, and the majorities of the political candidates ranged from 175 to 495. Polling commenced at eight o'clock on Wednesday morning. The polling stations with presiding officers and poll clerks were as fellows • Town Hall Ward : Infants' school,Kendrick-street, presiding officers,Mesers. T S Steel and J W Whittle ; poll olerks, Messrs. James Houghtan and H P Brierley ; Bewsey Ward, 98, Hoyle- street and St. Ann's School, presiding officers, Maws. Joseph Smith and TII Spiriks • clerks, Messrs. W T Crank and H Lawrence ; Orford Ward, Silver-street School, presiding officers,Mesers. John Sutcliffe and F G Taylor; and clerks, Messrs. E J Smith and Joel Smith ; Latchford Ward, St. Jantes's school and Christ Church School, presiding officers, Messrs. A J Willett, Robert Crompton, and Basil Shearer ; and clerks, Messrs. J W Howarth, A Starkey, and F H Wilson, . . . - _</p>
        <p>A number of vehicles was used by both sides and every effort was made te secure support. Voting pro- ceeded quietly throughout the day, and became briefs at night. Canvassers on both sides worked hard in bring- ing up the voters but diffidence was show* by many, and a large number did not record their votes. Al! the caadidates had committee rooms with the exception of Mr. Cliffe who was also poorly supported with workers. The contest was keenest in Bsweey and Latchford wards. Councillor Shaw Green was opposed in Bewsey Ward by Mr. Elijah Cunnington, a candidate who had the advantage of being well-known in the ward. Here the closest fight of the day was witnessed. Mr. Green was loyally supported and was confident of the ultimate issue. In Orford Ward, Mr. Greeniag in a number of posters on the walls, stated that he " has no personal interest to serve," and the electors were asked to vote" f)r the honour of tho good old town " Both he and Mr. Gough were well supported by workers until the close of the poll. This contest excit.d more interest owing to the prominent part taken by Mr. Gough in the Ratepayers' Assuciation, and the heaviest poll of the day took place in this bard.</p>
        <p>There was a fair display of mural literature. Pro- minent amongst the p-_,sters was a large yellow bill issued by;the Ratopayers' Association whose candidates were Messrs E. Cunnington, J. Cliffs, and S. Gough. The placard gave various reasons why the ratepayers should vote for:their choice, including the following':—They do not bolieve in leaders of political parties disfranchising the -st,payers for two years. They ask for support because it is a notorious fact that the control and guid- ance of all matters of importance in the Town Council have Leen in the hands of an " inner circle," the actions of which are kept secret bocause they will not stand the light of day. Private interests, they allege, are con- sidered and worked for while the interests of the people are neelecteJ and too often sacrificed entirely. The inner circle is composed of those who consented tI the question- able practices that occurred during the taking of the poll, and thus duped thousands out of their votes, The Council helped to spend on deputations last year £5OO or £lO per week, of which they were afraid to give the public a etailed account. The eleventh reason for sup- port was "becawie the councillors had made the Council a bear garden, losing all control of their temper and for- getting their self-respect." _ _</p>
        <p>In Latchford Ward, Mr. Pemberton was the Indepen- dent candidate, te being the nominee of the Citizens' Union. He oppcoed the return of Councillor Jolley. Mr. Pemberton informed the electors that his candida- ture was an active protest against the disfranchisement of the ratepaT ere. H- promised in his address, to advo- cate tetter heuses for toe working classes and play- grounds for the children. HP asksed the electors "to be free, and not the slaves of any party." Mr. Jolley invited the electors to vote " for their old representa- tive." He had reeeivoi letters from Councillor W. Pier- point and Mr. T. B. Malkin, who were out of town, wishing him every success. Mr. Pierpoint, who was in London, wrote : " Sorry I shall nct be able to carry out my intention to vote for you to•morrow. I have pleasure in wishing Toll may be returned by a large majority." Punctually at eight o'clock the polling booths were ersed, and the ballot boxes were conveyei in haste to the Town Hall where the counting of the votes was pro- ceeded with. By nine o'clock the results were known and were declared from the steps at the Town Hall by the returning officers for the respective wards. The re- sults were received with cheers and derisive laughter in some instances. They were as follow : Town Hall Ward. J. Shillcock (C) 543 Cliffe (Ind) 4B Majority</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>REV. ISAAC SHIN MIN AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>THE REV. ISAAC SHIN MIN AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The following extract from a speech delivered by the Rev. Isaac Shimmin will doubtless be read with interest by many people in Earlestown,where the rev. gentleman is well known :—The Rev. Isaac Shimmin, a returned Wesleyan missionary from the Transvaal,has been attending a series of missionary meetings in Cumberland and Westmorland. and has given his impressions of the war. He said he knew as much of the inner life of the Transvaal and other parts of South Africa as most men in Eng- land. When first introduced to Paul Kruger, the old homely countryman, he received a very warm welcome to that country, but he could not help thinking that the old man was altogether incapa- ble of ruling such an important State as the Trans- vaal. The Outlanders only asked for the same rights that were given to the Dutch in Cape Colony, and they could leave questions of suzerainty or paramountcy to politicians such as Lord Salisbury. Mr. Chamberlain, and Sir William Harcourt. H. prayed God that very soon this awful war might be brought to an end, and he believed that when it wan over there would come a reign of real pros- perity and lasting peace; and the racial feeling of which we had heard so mach would have been eared net by peace and honour.but by the means and methods now adopted. (Cheers.) He did not intsnd to become a politician. He believed, however, as a minister, a Christian, and a man who had lived for a deaen years out there, and who knew most of the leading men on both sides intimately, that if ever there was a righteous war, this wae ene, and the God of battles would decide. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>GOOD TR3iPLARY ANNIVRRSITLY..</head>
        <p>GOOD TR3iPLARY ANNIVRRSITLY..</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlestown Lodge hold its 2/7th annual tea party and concert in the Co-operative Hall on Saturday evening, when the hall was filled by members and friends who speedily despatched tbe good things provided by the committee consist- ing of Sisters Warburton, Ord, Grant, Owen, Misses Thomas, Smith, Mole. Mrs Leatherbarrow, Messrs Leatherbarrow, Macklin, and Warburton. After tea Mr Francis presided over a large gathering of friends, the hall being well filled, when the follow- ing programme was rendered ; pianoforte solo, "Robins return," Miss Mitchell ; song. "The Giosies warning," Miss Thomas ; recitation "Is it nothing to you?" Mrs Francis ; song " The Chorister," Mks Woods; mouth organ and bones duet, Mr Wil- son end Mr J Phiflips song " Love is not for a day," Mr. Twemlos ; who very ably filled the place of Mr. Webster, as he was unable to be pre- sent. An address was given by Mr. Sykes, of Southport, who spoke strongly on behalf of the children. He related how going one day through the streets of Liverpool he was accosted by a little street arab who on questioning be found had had nothing to eat from the day becore, so he gave him a penny to get a bun, and watched him prepare to eat it. Ag he sat on the kerbstone he caught sight of a little girl he knew, and whistling to her to come he broke his bun in two and rave her half, although he had not tasted food for 24 hours him- self.—The Speaker W 49 loudly applauded on com- pletion of his address. Song, " That's the job for me," Mr. Stanley ; who on being encored gave "I'm nct." Selections by the mouth organ band leader, Mr. J. Wilson, which were a revelation to many as to the capabilities of this little instrument. Slng, " Gates of the West," Miss Woods, which was well received ; recitation, Nothing to wear." Mrs. Francis; song, "Oh that Chicken," Mr. Stanley; who for an encore gave" Miss Hooligan Lake " ; selec'ions by the mouth organ band Nearer home " ; song," The Blue Alsatian Mountains," Mr. Twemloe being loudly applauded, ani most ably accompanie on the piano by Miss Toombs. This brought to a close a most successful series of meet- ings, and should do some good.</p>
        <p>BRASS AND IRON BRDSTEADS, Belding. Wring- ing Machines Lambs. and Coal Vases. shefilel.i Cutlery and Plate.—J. S. GARLAND. Ironmonger. Bridge-street. Warrington —Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>CONVERSAZIONE. Literary and Philosophical Society at the Training College.</head>
        <p>CONVERSAZIONE. Literary and Philosophical Society at the Training College.</p>
        <p>There wee a pleasant gathering in the Warrington Training College on Monday evening in connection with the Warri: gton Literary and Philosophical Society. Tile ' President of the Society (the Rev. Morley Stevenson) had invited the members of the society and their friends to a conversazione and there was a large response. The following is s list of tbe guests who accepted the invita- tion of the President :—Mr and Mrs E Atkin, Captain and Miss Appleton, Mr and Mrs Ashton, Dr and Mrs Adams, Mr and Mrs Aitken, Mr D AMOS, Mrs and Miss Broadbent, Major and Mrs J D Buckton, Dr and Mrs Braids, Dr Battle, Dr and Mise Bowden, Mr T Bir- chall and Miss Birchen, Mr and Mr; C Sawkins Burgess, Miss Bell, Mr and Mrs Arthur llennett, Miss Bridson, Mr J Bernard, Miss Birkett, Mr and Mrs Arthur Browne, Mr and Mrs Birtles, Mr and Miss Chandley, the Rev E C E and Mrs Carleton, Mr and Mrs Carruthers, Miss Cleaver, Mr and Mrs Coxon, Miss Clifton, Mrs Dutton, Mr and Mrs Dakin, Mr and Mrs Vero B Davies, Miss Daunt, Mr R and Miss Dobsoe, the Rev L J T and Mrs Darwall, Mr and Mrs Denshana, Mr James Dunn. Mr and Mrs Dewhurst, Mr and Miss Dienum, the Rev W H and Mrs Drummond.Mr and Mrs J H Elelsten, Mr and Mrs Ewart, Mr and Mrs John Fairclough, Miss Fullagar, Mr Fisher, Mr and Mrs Forsbaw, Dr and Mrs Fox, Mr and Mrs John Fairhurst,Mrs H T and the Misses Fairclough, Miss Ferriman, Mr and Mrs James Fairclough, Mr and Mrs Frith, Mr and Mrs L Greening, Dr and Mrs Guest Gomel!, Miss I Greening, Mr C H Grundy, Mr, Mrs and the Misses Gibson, Mr Shaw Green, Mr and Mrs Heeketh, Mr and Mrs R G Henshall, Mr and Mrs Hamlet Houghton, Mr and Miss Haddock, Mr and Mrs Hutchinson.Mr and Mrs Harrieen, Mr and Mrs Howard, Mr and Mrs Hal, Mr and Mrs Jackson Holmes, Mr and Mrs James Hall. Mr and Mrs Holford, Mr and Mrs Hill,Mr A J Jolley,Mr and Mrs Kenyon, Mr and Miss Knowles, the Rev J J and Mrs Langham, Mr and Mrs James Lawton, Dr Langdale, Mr and Miss Longland, Mr and Mrs Long, the Rev H A and Mrs Lest( r, Mr and Mrs F W Monks, Mr and Mr, Madelev, Mr and Mrs May, Mr and Mrs McGreger, Mr and Mrs Milling. Mr and Mrs Monks, Dr and Mrs Nadas, Mr and Mrs Owen, Mr and Mrs Segar Owen, Mr and Mrs R Pearson, Dr and Mrs Peacocks!. the Rev F E and Ms Powell, Mr and Mrs Proud, Mr and Mrs T Proud, Mr C and Miss Plinston, Mr and Mrs R W Rewsen, Mr and the Misses Reynolds. the Misses Rimmer, Mr and Mrs W B Roberts, Mr and Mrs Robinson, Mrs Sharp, Dr. and Mrs Reginald Smith, Mr and Mrs W Silcook. Mr Sutcliffe, Mr and Mrs Skinner, Miss Saunders, Mr and Mrs J H Smethurst, Mr and Mrs! Joseph Smith, Miss and Miss M Timewell, the Misses Tinsley, Mr and Mrs J L Tunstall, Mr and Mrs L A Voisey, Mr and Mrs George Venn, Mr and Mrs John White, Dr and the Misses White, Mr George and Miss White, Mr and Mrs Edwin Woods, Mrs and Miss Willcocks,ethe Rev J Warnock. Mr and Mrs Woods, the Rev Canon and Mrs Willis, Mr and Mrs J E Wright, Mr Withinshaw, aMr and Mrs Welsby, Mr and Mrs W S Woods, Mrs Whalley, Mr and Mrs Williams, Mr and Mrs J Rymer Young. A reception was held in the students sitting roum, at which the guests were received by the Principal and Mrs. Stevenson. A short time was agreeably whiled away in conversation, whilst at intervals Miss Perrin very sweetly and effectively sang the following solos : St. Agnes Eve," " The Silver Ring," and " Husheen." Mr, W. S. Woods accompanied en the piano. A brief address was delivered by the Rev. MORLEY STEVENSON. He expressed his obligations to the mem - bers of the society for having done him the honour to elect him as president for the second time—an honour which he very much appreciated, and in return for which he could only say that it would be his endeavoar to do all he possibly could to promote the interests of the society in every way Is could. He thanked the audi- ence very much for having accepted his simple invita- tion and for assembling in twee large numbers. Be wished the attendance at all their meetings was as good as that he now saw before him. (Laughter.) He wished to make one or two observations upon the pro- gramme of the session. The Council had two objects in view in drawing up the programme; they had endeavoured to get certain lectures by those who did not live in Warrington, but who were pre-emineutly Qualified to give them, and they had also striven to make a special point of drawing out their local talent. That should be the object of the society. They would prt- sent to the members from time to time lectures which would be as first rate as they could get them, and they would foster in every possible way the talent which undoubtedly was to be found, even if it had to to looked for, in their town. (Applause.) The next lecture would be given on November 13th, by Miss E. L. Broadbent, I of Latchford, on " Wanderings in the Border Country," while on December 11th they would have an astronounca lecture from the Yen. Archdeacon Wilson, who was at one time headmaster of Clifton School, land who was facile princeps amongst the scientific men of the day. He had heard him lecture, and with Ithe exception of Sir Robert Ball he had never heard any- one who had made an astronomical lecture so ineereet- ing and so plain. It would be illustrated by lantern slides by Mr. Houghton, to whom they were all so much in- Idebted. (Applause.) On January 16th Mr. Alfred Derbyshire would lecture on "Some Eoglish Cathedrals." and on February 12th, the Rev. Henry Gow would give them a paper on " Robert Louis Stevenson," and it would be of exceptional interest. Then they would come back to Warrington on March 12th, when the Rev. F E Powell woula lecture on " The Relation of Psycho!, gy to Metaphysics and Physiology." He thought they would agree with him that that was a good programme. (Hear. hear.) He had heara it said in the town that the meet- ings of the society were not attended as well as they I used to b-e ant it had also been whispered that the speaking was not so good, but in answer to that he had only to say that he did not very often see the critics at the meetings. (Laughter.) He was not at all disposed to deny that the charge was justified to a certain extent, certainly with regard to the attendance, and when they considered the functions of a society of this kind it was not right that they should ask a lady or gentleman to come from a distance, or even not from a distance, and to take the trouble te write and read the paper before a small audience. Be hopsd that the session upon which they had entered tbat evening would be mantel by good attendance and appreciative audiences. (Ap- plause.) " Ancient Egypt " was the subject of a most interest- ing and instructive lecture by the Rev. Haskett Smith, subsequently given in the lecture room. The lecture was to have been fully illustrated by lime-light views, but the gas in the cylinder unfortunately gave out, and the latter portion of the lecture had to be given without the aid of the illustrations. At the outset the Lecturer stated net the 19th century would undoubtedly take rank in the history of the future as one of the most re- markable centuries the world had ever seen. It hail been a century of invention, research, enterprise, and it had been no less noteworthy for its achievements in re- gard to the great and mighty past,having witnessed the unlocking of many secrets hidden in the vast storehouse of time. And of no country in the world was that more emphatic-ally true than of that marvellously interesting land which it would be his pleasure to speak to them about for a short time that evening—Egypt, the land of the Nile. One hundred years ago little was known of the ancient history of Egypt. Reports were brought back from time to time of sublime ruins on the banks of the Nile, the walls and columns of which were covered with mysterious figures and hieroglyphics, hieroglyphics which no one was able to read. Now all these were deciphered, and it had also been proved that instead of the world being as supposed from the chronology of the Bible, only about 7,000 years old, there were living long before that time in Egypt,a race of people of the highest civilization, culture and religious enlightenment, and quite as high in every way as those who were so justly proud at the close of the 19th century. (Applause.) Proceeding to refer to the discoveries in Egypt, the stone, now known as the Rosetta stone carved 198 s.c. and first discovered in 1799, was shown on the screen and fully described. This was the key which had un- locked that mysterious chamber to which he had al- ready referred, and which had revealed to their knew- ledge the histories and mysteries of ancient Eeypt. Many and many a remarkable monument, tomb, temple, and palace bad since been discovered, innumerable treasures of priceless value, and these had been very carefully re- moved to Cairo, and placed in the magnificieat museum which had been built there—one of the most interesting museums in the whole of the world. A graphic account of the most interesting exhibits in this museum was then givers, and the Lecturer remarked that he once had the pleasure of shaking hands with one of the Pharaohs known as Ramasees 11., during the absence of the museum attendant. The glass ease containing the mummy had been temporarily opered, and he took ae- vantaee of the ab:ence of the attendant to shake hands with Pnaraoh. He took hold of his hand very gingerly, fearing he would bring it away with him. (Laughter.) The old Egyptians, he went on to say, had as firm a faith in the future life after death as we had, with i's judgments, tribulations.rewarde and punishmentsein the immortality of the soul and the resurrection of the body; but their faith differed in one material respect. They could not conceive the possibility of a body being raised to life after it had entirely disappeared and crumbled away into dust. They thought a necessary condition of resurrection was that the outer frame should be preserved intact till the day of the rrsurrection, and that the soul even after death was in such intimate communion with the body that as long as the outer frame remained intact the soul would flourish, but as the body decayed the soul pined and withered away, and that when the last atom crumbled to dust the soul went out. With such a faith they could easily understand that it was to them a matter of meat vital importance that measures should be adopted to preserve a body after death from destruc- tion. There were three great enemies against which they had to guard— self-decay, the convulsions of nature, and the depredation of man, and the lecturer showed the careful means which were ad-opting to pre- vent these occurring. Allusion was made to the mighty Sphinx, and to its great age, and be said that though it existed 6,000 years ago, it was as firm as ever, and if the world should last 6,000 years longer our descendants would gaze upon it still. Hs believed it to be the ensb!em</p>
        <p>of the invisible Head. It was the emblem of till infinite majesty, wisdom. and intelligence of Gad, the lion's body ID, ing the emblem of self-repose and of the power and strength and Pelf-sufficiency of God. H. lo .k upon c vsitiou at the head of ',Ale cemeers as Liao ing us that GA was the God of the dead and living, and its aEpect in facing the rising sun as telling us that He is the God of the re,urrection; and as time went on and as kingdoms rose and fimrished, waned and perished, and as humanity crept an, the wondrous emblem of Divinity still remained cairn and immutable, ever look- ing for the dawn of eternal day. (Applause.) aTtie Lecturer's and MisVerrin's services were acknow- ledged on tho propoeition of Mr. ,1 RYMER YOUNG, secon- ded by the Rev E C E CARLETON. _</p>
        <p>_ Refreshments were served at the close, and al:ogether a most eniiyable time was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S. A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meat-i-r-i-g of the P.S.A. So2iety was held in the Co-Doeratice Hall, Earlestown on Sunday afternoon. There was a large attendance. Mr. W. Brown presided, and an interesting address was given by Mr J. ?otter. Vocal selections were submitted by Miss L Jones and recitations were given by Mr. Huffer. ..virs. Latimer presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>RELIEF FOR THE FAMILIES OF RESERVE MEN . AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>A week or two ago,a reserve man belonging to the Shropshire Light Infantry, named William Ilodgeß, was called up to join his regiment. He was em- ployed at the Viaduct works as a striker, and he left behind him a wife and one or two children unprovi ied for. His shop mates in the smithy at the works have decided that the wife shall not want, and have resolved that each man shall con- tribute one penny per week,during Elodges,absence, for the support of his wife. Last week the contri- butions amounted to 19s sd. 1.0.G.T.</p>
        <p>The Hope of Earlesto—Wn— lodge weekly meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave-road. on Monday evening, Bro Francis C.T. presiding. After the usual opening services two members were re-admitted and two were initiated. A letter was read asking for the use of the piano,but the Lodge could not see their way to grant it for fear of dhmage. Bro Woods L.D read D.L. circular and return forms. Bro. and Sister Litherlaud of Warrington tendered the fraternal greetings of the Star of the East Lodge, Warrington. The programme being a social, Bros responsible, the Lodge was thrown open to visitors who soon filled the hall and they soon cleared the tables of the good things provided by the brothers. The mouth organ band playing selections after the tables had been cleared, the rest of the evening was spent in various games.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHURCH. The above church has during the past few weeks been closed for re-decoration, and cleaning of the organ, and for the installation of the electric lights description of which we gave a short time ago. The work having been completed, the church was re- opened for service on Sunday, when special services were held. The church was filled, especially at the evening service, when it was crowded, many being attracted there doubtless by the novelty of the elec- tric fighting. The services were conducted by the Rev Cornelius W Jones, of London, and at one time of Earlestown, who delivered interesting discourses. special anthems were sung by the choir" In the beginning,', and "A day in Thy Court," both being well rendered. At the evening service sacred solos were song by Miss M Barr, " The better land," Miss J Adams, "The good Shepherd," and Mr P Clare, " Be not afraid.' Collections were made in aid of the cost of renovation.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CONSERVATIVE CLUB. The first smoking concert of the season in con- nection with the above club, was held in the large bidiard room on Friday evening. Mr. C. B. F. Borron presided, and there was a large audience. The room looked very brilliant, having just been c,mpleted with the fitting of the electric light, and being switched on for the first time on Friday night was something of a novelty tu those present. The following programme was submitted, duet (piano and violin), Messrs A Walker and J H Peplow; song " Rule Britannia," Mr George Wright; song " Bells of Shandon," Mr T Sheridan; humorous song "Never to return again," Mr J Huddleston ; violin solo, Mr J H Peplow ; song "On to the front," Mr George Wright ; song " Let me like a soldier fall," Mr T Sheridan; humorous song, Mr J Huddleston ; song "Hearts of Oak," Mr T Sheridan. Mr A Walker ably presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION CONSERVATIVE ASSOCIATION. At the quarterly meeting of the Newton Division Cmservative Council, held on Saturday at the Town Hall, Newton, the following resolution was passed nil the motion of Ar. W. Valiant, seconded by Mr. T. J. Gillespie :—" That the Council of the Newton Divi,ton Conservative Association in meeting assembled whilst deeply regretting the necessity for armed intervention in South Africa, desires to express its unqualified approval of the course taken during recent negotiations with the Government of the Transvaal. and its entire confidence in, and support for, Her Majesty's Government in their policy on the South African question."—Lord New- ton and Lieutenant-Colonel Pilkington spoke in support of the resolution. SUDDEN DEATH AT EARLESTOWN. The Earlestown police have been notified of the death of Thomas Davison, a wagon maker of 205. Legh-street, Earlestown, which took place very suddenly on Sunday at the age of 59 years. It ap- pears the deceased left his work on gaturday at 12 noon, and went home. He complained of having pains in his body, and went to bed about 4 o'clock in the afternoon. About 11 o'clock next morning ha had some breakfast and about 12.30 noon came downstairs and went into the back yard. He said he was not much better and went back to bed. About 1.45 Nathaniel Jones a friend of deceased called at his house to see him, and on getting up- stairs found him dead in bed. Dr. Patterson had attp_.nded him for some time off and on for rheu- matic gout. There were no marks of violence on the body and ne suspicion of foul play. The facts were r-ported to the coroner.but it was not deemed necessary to hold an inquest.</p>
        <p>Charge Against a Warrington Painter. At the Chester Castle Petty Sessional Occasional Court on Thursday, Arthur Wilson of Warrington, and employed as a painter at Ellesmere Port, was char ged with committing an as:•ault upon a young girl at Ellesmere Port the previous night. —Prisoner, who denied the charge, was remanded.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS Fon ALL. UNDER THE CLOCK.—A&amp;</p>
        <p>Latchford Improvement Society.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above society was held in the Temperance Hall on Friday night, Mr. Ford, the president, presiding. Several nominations were r,- ceived for membership, and subjects promised for discussion. Mr. Drewitt gave an essay on " Home Rule," and reviewed in a reasonable manner the burning question about which so much has been said. He argued for many needed reforms. Ireland wanted justice, and he voiced the cry, " Ireland for the Irish." He alluded to the absenteeism of the land- lords,the poverty of the peasantry, and the conflict- ing and perplexing questions of government and management, &amp;c. Councillor Ellison, Mr. Harry Blea.e, and Mr. Shakeshaft were strongly in favour of Mr. Gladstone's scheme, or any other equitable arrangement for Ireland, in which she should have the rights and privileges of Canada, Newfoundland and other of our colonies. Messrs. Hill, Arthur rord,Osborne, Scc combated the essayist's position. Other speakers followed.</p>
