      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-11-03_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>SOMETHING ABOUT THE STORY.</head>
        <p>SOMETHING ABOUT THE STORY.</p>
        <p>On his way to visit his cousin, Sir Harry Beauworth, Alec Beauworth, heir to the title, is set upon by two men and knocked senseless. Sir Harry Beauworth has a bad reputation. Bob Rabbitts, son of the evil-looking landlor of the Dog and Duck," revives Alec, and de- termines, as ha has the chance, to enter Sir Harry's services for a time to see that Alec comes to no harm. Sir Harry has one daughter. Ruby, a beautiful and un- sophisticated girl, who has never been adowed to see the world. and whom her father intends to marry to an Australian. James Felton, who with his father, Tom Felton; appears to have a powerful interest over bins. These Feltons are the men who tried to kill Alec, though he never saw their faces, and it is obvious that they acted at the instigation of the baronet, who bad some secret reason for wishing to be rid of the heir. Alec when he sees Ruby, falls in love with her, and not knowing his danger determines, if possible, to stay and win her.</p>
        <p>Though he has no intention of allowing Ruby to marry Alec, Sir Harry Beauwortls, plays upon Aloe's affection in order to induce him to make a will in Bub) 'a favour. Alec consents, and hereafter his destruction is practically decided upon by Sir Harry and the Feltm's, so as to pave the way for a division of all the money and the estates which in the ordinary course, would have fallen to Alec. The bironet is in reality personating the real baronet who died many years ago in the Bush and the Fe!tons, knowing this secret, blackmail Sir Harry Beauworth and make him an ally in their nefarious schemes. With the assistance of a movable floor in his bedrooda Alec is preci- pitated into a dungeon and is left there to starve. His thrilling attempts to escape and the subsequent adven- ture; wnich befall him and the heroine Rutty impart a Monte Cristo fl 'your to this romance which draws to a spirited and satisfactory conclusion.</p>
      </div>
